module.exports=[45893,93247,69310,a=>{"use strict";let b=(0,a.i(92277).default)("arrow-up-right",[["path",{d:"M7 7h10v10",key:"1tivn9"}],["path",{d:"M7 17 17 7",key:"1vkiza"}]]);a.s(["ArrowUpRight",0,b],45893);let c=[{slug:"wichita-state-university-digital-badge-program-2026-1",title:"Wichita State University Digital Badge Program 2026",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wichita-state-university-digital-badge-program-2026/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/fully-funded.webp",intro:"In today’s competitive academic and professional environment, completing a degree is important, but it may not always be enough to help you stand out. Employers and universities increasingly value candidates who demonstrate continuous learning, practical skills, adaptability, and a genuine interest in professional development. The Wichita State University Digital Badge Program 2026 gives students, working professionals, and lifelong learners an opportunity to build focused skills through short, flexible online courses. Through the 2026 program, learners can choose from 30+ badge courses across areas such as Engineering, Business, Health Sciences, Professional Writing, Social Media, and other career-focused disciplines. Most of the courses available through this initiative are scholarship-funded, making international learning more accessible to eligible participants.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["Whether you are preparing for university, applying for an internship, beginning your career, considering a professional transition, or simply looking to learn something new, a Wichita State University badge can become a valuable addition to your profile."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What Is the Wichita State University Digital Badge Program?",paragraphs:["A digital badge is a university-issued credential that demonstrates that a learner has successfully completed a short course and achieved specific learning outcomes.","Unlike many informal online completion certificates, Wichita State University badges are academic short courses. They are connected to university credit and become part of the learner’s academic record. Successful completion is recorded as “BG,” meaning badge earned, on the Wichita State University transcript. The badge itself can also be shared digitally on platforms such as LinkedIn and added to a résumé or professional portfolio. (Wichita State University Catalog)","The courses are designed in smaller, manageable learning units, making them suitable for learners who may already be balancing school, university, employment, internships, or family responsibilities."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Wichita State University Digital Badge Program 2026: Key Highlights",paragraphs:["The 2026 badge opportunity includes the following benefits:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Most courses offered through the campaign are fully scholarship-funded, subject to availability",paragraphs:["Fully online and self-paced learning"],bullets:[]},{heading:"The ability to display the badge on LinkedIn, a résumé, and a professional portfolio",paragraphs:["Access for students, professionals, and lifelong learners"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Flexible course commencement after the program begins, provided all work is completed before the final deadline",paragraphs:["The exact workload, requirements, credit value, and scholarship coverage may differ by course. Wichita State University states that badges are generally worth 0.5 or 1 credit hour, and applicants should review the details of their selected course before enrolling. (Wichita State University)"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why Digital Badges Matter in 2026",paragraphs:["Education and recruitment are becoming increasingly skills-focused. Employers want to understand not only what qualification a candidate holds but also what the candidate can actually do.","A digital badge allows you to demonstrate learning in a specific area without committing to another full-time degree or long-term academic program. It can show that you have taken the initiative to learn beyond your existing curriculum or job responsibilities.","For example, a student may study Engineering but complete a badge in professional communication. A Business student may explore social media or globalisation. A working professional may strengthen skills related to employee performance, recruitment, exporting, cultural competency, or online presentations.","This combination of core education and supplementary learning can help create a more versatile profile."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How Can the WSU Digital Badge Program Help Your Career?",paragraphs:["One of the qualities valued by employers is the willingness to keep learning.","Technology, communication methods, workplace expectations, and industry practices continue to change. Candidates who actively develop new skills may be better prepared to respond to these changes.","Completing a digital badge tells employers that you did not wait for your college or organisation to provide every learning opportunity. You identified a useful area, committed time to it, completed the required work, and earned a formal credential.","This can reflect qualities such as:","Self-motivation","Time management","Curiosity","Professional discipline","Adaptability"],bullets:["It Can Demonstrate Your Commitment to Continuous Learning"]},{heading:"Willingness to learn independently",paragraphs:["The badge alone will not guarantee employment. However, when it is supported by projects, assignments, internships, academic performance, and clear career goals, it can strengthen the overall impression you create.","Many résumés contain broad statements such as “good communication skills,” “interested in business,” or “familiar with social media.” These claims become more credible when they are supported by relevant learning or practical work.","A badge can provide structured evidence that you have studied a specific subject.","For example:","Wichita State University – Professional Writing: Crafting Your Résumé and Cover Letter"],bullets:["It Can Add a Focused Skill to Your Résumé"]},{heading:"Digital Badge, 2026",paragraphs:["You can also add one or two relevant learning outcomes below the badge:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Learned to analyse job descriptions and tailor application documents",paragraphs:["Wichita State University’s résumé and cover-letter badge, for example, includes learning related to professional documents, job listings, persuasive content, grammar, conciseness, and the development of an effective résumé and cover letter. (","Wichita State University",")","The key is not to merely mention the badge. Explain what you learned and how it connects with your career direction.","LinkedIn has become an important professional platform for students, graduates, jobseekers, and working professionals.","After completing your badge, you may add it to the Licences and Certifications section of your profile. You can include:","Name of the badge","Issuing organisation: Wichita State University","Date of completion","Credential link or identifier, where available"],bullets:["It Can Strengthen Your LinkedIn Profile"]},{heading:"Skills connected with the course",paragraphs:["You can also publish a brief LinkedIn post about your experience. Instead of simply writing, “I am happy to share that I completed a course,” explain:","Why you selected the badge","What you learned","Which assignment or topic was most useful"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How the badge relates to your academic or professional goals",paragraphs:["A thoughtful post can make the credential more meaningful and may also help you begin conversations with classmates, recruiters, mentors, and professionals in your field.","Interviewers frequently ask questions such as:"],bullets:["It Can Give You Something Meaningful to Discuss in Interviews"]},{heading:"How have you prepared for this role?",paragraphs:["Tell us about a new skill you recently learned."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why are you interested in this field?",paragraphs:["A completed badge can provide a practical example for answering these questions.","For instance, you could say:","“Alongside my regular studies, I completed a digital badge from Wichita State University in online presentations. The course helped me understand how to organise presentation material, communicate effectively in a virtual environment, and improve the clarity of my delivery. I later applied those principles while presenting a college project.”","This is stronger than simply listing a badge because it connects your learning with an actual experience.","Students are often expected to choose a specialisation or career path before they have had enough exposure to different fields.","A short badge course can help you explore an area without immediately committing to a full degree, diploma, or expensive certification.","For example:","A student considering Human Resources can explore recruitment, job design, or performance management.","A learner interested in international business can study exporting, globalisation, or entering foreign markets.","A healthcare aspirant can explore medical terminology or culturally competent service delivery.","A student interested in content and communication can study professional writing, social media editing, or online presentations.","An engineering learner can explore a focused technical topic beyond the regular curriculum.","The experience may either confirm your interest or help you realise that another field is more suitable. Both outcomes are valuable when planning your education and career.","Students and recent graduates may not have several years of professional experience. Their profiles are therefore often evaluated through a combination of:","Academic performance","Internships","Projects","Volunteering","Extracurricular activities","Certifications and badges","Communication skills"],bullets:["It Can Help You Explore a Career Before Making a Major Commitment","It Can Support Internship and Entry-Level Job Applications"]},{heading:"Evidence of initiative",paragraphs:["A relevant WSU badge can become one additional piece of evidence that supports your application.","For example, a student applying for a marketing internship may benefit from a badge related to professional social media. A student applying for an HR internship may select recruitment or employee-performance coursework. A candidate applying for an international-business opportunity may choose exporting, globalisation, or foreign-market entry.","The badge should complement the role you are targeting. Selecting a course simply because it is available may be less useful than choosing one connected with your intended career.","Modern careers do not always remain within the boundaries of a single academic discipline.","An engineer may need communication and business skills. A healthcare professional may need cultural awareness and leadership skills. A business graduate may need digital communication and data-related capabilities. A content professional may need knowledge of branding, social media, and audience behaviour.","A badge allows you to add a complementary skill to your main qualification.","This can be especially beneficial for students who want to create an interdisciplinary profile, such as:","Engineering and business","Healthcare and communication","Technology and professional writing","Business and social media","Psychology and cultural competency"],bullets:["It Can Help You Build a More Interdisciplinary Profile"]},{heading:"Management and global affairs",paragraphs:["For students considering higher education in the United States, an online badge course can provide limited but useful exposure to an American university’s learning system.","Depending on the course, students may experience:","Online university learning platforms","Assignment-based assessment","Independent study","Academic deadlines","University communication systems","Applied learning activities"],bullets:["It Can Introduce You to a US University Learning Environment"]},{heading:"Instructor expectations",paragraphs:["The badge program should not be viewed as equivalent to completing a full degree or studying on campus. However, it can offer an introduction to international online learning and help students become more comfortable with independent academic work.","WSU’s international badge information describes the courses as an opportunity for students to gain exposure to a US university class experience. (Wichita State University)","Digital credentials are most valuable when the learner can demonstrate who issued them and what was required to earn them.","Wichita State University states that successful learners receive a digital recognition that can be shared on social media or connected to a digital résumé. The academic work also appears on the student’s transcript. (Wichita State University)","This provides greater credibility than simply mentioning that you watched online videos or informally studied a subject.","However, learners must successfully complete all course requirements. Students who do not complete the required work may receive an “NBG,” or no badge earned, notation. Badge results do not affect the student’s GPA. (Wichita State University)","Who Should Consider the WSU Digital Badge Program?"],bullets:["It Provides a Credential That Can Be Verified"]},{heading:"School and Undergraduate Students",paragraphs:["The program may help students explore subjects beyond their existing curriculum, build independent-learning habits, and begin developing a stronger academic and extracurricular profile.","Students under the required age should confirm the supervision and eligibility conditions before registering."],bullets:[]},{heading:"University Graduates and Jobseekers",paragraphs:["Graduates may select badges that complement their degree or relate to the roles they are targeting. The credential can be added to a résumé and discussed during interviews."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Working Professionals",paragraphs:["Professionals can use badge courses to refresh existing knowledge, explore a new responsibility, or add a specialised skill without interrupting their employment.","WSU states that its badge program was developed with working professionals in mind and that coursework is structured in smaller, manageable units. (Wichita State University Catalog)","Career Changers","A badge can serve as an introductory step for professionals considering a different field. It cannot replace the qualifications required for regulated or highly specialised careers, but it may help learners assess their interest and begin building foundational knowledge.","Lifelong Learners","Not every learner is pursuing a promotion, degree, or job change. Some may simply want to understand a new subject, improve communication, or remain intellectually active."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What Types of Badge Courses Are Available?",paragraphs:["The final 2026 course list may depend on scholarship allocations and seat availability. The opportunity is expected to include more than 30 courses across several broad areas.","Engineering","Engineering-related badges may help learners explore specialised technical concepts, analytical approaches, programming, optimisation, or other applied areas.","These courses may be particularly useful for engineering students who want exposure beyond their university syllabus."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Business and Management",paragraphs:["Business badges may include subjects connected with:","Human resource management","Recruitment","Employee performance","Job design","Global business","Exporting","Foreign-market entry","Globalisation","These subjects can be relevant to students considering careers in management, HR, consulting, entrepreneurship, international business, and operations.","Professional Writing and Communication","Possible areas include:","Résumé and cover-letter writing","Professional correspondence","Online presentations","Social-media editing"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Workplace communication",paragraphs:["These skills are useful across industries because professionals are regularly expected to write emails, prepare documents, present ideas, and communicate online.","Health Sciences","Health-related badges may introduce learners to areas such as medical terminology, healthcare communication, care services, or professional competencies relevant to health and social-service settings.","A short badge does not provide a medical licence or replace formal healthcare qualifications. It should be viewed as supplementary learning."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Behavioural and Social Sciences",paragraphs:["Learners may find courses related to cultural competency, intersectionality, service delivery, criminal justice, or other behavioural and community-oriented topics."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Social Media and Workforce Skills",paragraphs:["These badges may help learners improve their professional online presence, understand workplace expectations, or strengthen general employability skills."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How to Choose the Right Badge for Your Career",paragraphs:["The best badge is not necessarily the most technical or popular one. It is the course that fits your current stage and future direction.","Before selecting a badge, ask yourself:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What career or academic goal am I working towards?",paragraphs:["Choose a course that supports a real goal, such as preparing for an internship, improving communication, exploring HR, entering healthcare, or strengthening an engineering profile."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What skill is currently missing from my profile?",paragraphs:["Review your résumé and identify a gap. It may be technical knowledge, professional writing, presentation ability, social-media professionalism, or understanding of a particular industry."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Can I explain why I selected this badge?",paragraphs:["You should be able to explain the connection between the course and your goals in one or two clear sentences."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Will I have enough time to complete it?",paragraphs:["Self-paced does not mean that there is no deadline. You will need to complete the learning activities and assignments before the course-completion date."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Can I apply the learning?",paragraphs:["Choose a badge that allows you to create or improve something practical, such as a résumé, presentation, LinkedIn profile, job description, recruitment plan, or technical exercise."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How to Make the Badge More Valuable After Completion",paragraphs:["Simply earning the badge is only the first step. The career value comes from how you apply and communicate what you learned.","Add It to Your Résumé","Create a section titled:","Certifications and Digital Credentials","Professional Development","Additional Education"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Courses and Credentials",paragraphs:["Mention the full badge title, Wichita State University, and the year of completion."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Update Your LinkedIn Profile",paragraphs:["Add the badge under Licences and Certifications and connect it with the most relevant skills on your profile."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Save Your Assignments",paragraphs:["Where permitted, retain copies of projects, presentations, written exercises, or action plans developed during the course. These may become useful portfolio materials.","Do not publicly share protected course material or assignments if the university does not permit it."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Apply the Learning to a Project",paragraphs:["Use the skill in a college project, internship, volunteering assignment, workplace responsibility, personal portfolio, or professional activity.","Prepare an Interview Explanation","Be ready to explain:","Why you selected the course","What you learned","What assignment you completed","How you applied the knowledge"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How it supports your future goals",paragraphs:["This turns the badge from a line on your résumé into a meaningful career story.","Important Dates for the 2026 Program","Program Begins:","17 August 2026","Registration Deadline:","11 September 2026","Course Completion Deadline:","3 December 2026","Applications may close before the stated deadline once available seats are filled. Interested learners are therefore encouraged to register early."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Is the WSU Digital Badge Program completely online?",paragraphs:["Yes. Wichita State University currently describes its badge courses as online, and the 2026 international opportunity is intended to be fully online and self-paced. (Wichita State University)"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Do I need TOEFL or IELTS?",paragraphs:["No TOEFL or IELTS score is required for the international badge opportunity."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Are the badge courses free?",paragraphs:["Most courses available through the 2026 campaign are scholarship-funded. Scholarship availability may depend on the selected course, available seats, and final approval. Applicants should confirm the coverage applicable to their selected badge before completing enrolment."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Will I receive a certificate?",paragraphs:["Successful learners receive a digital badge issued by Wichita State University. The completed academic work may also be reflected on the learner’s WSU transcript. It is more accurate to call it a digital badge rather than a degree or professional certificate. (Wichita State University)"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Will the badge affect my GPA?",paragraphs:["No. Badge courses are recorded as BG, meaning badge earned, or NBG, meaning no badge earned. They do not affect the learner’s GPA. (Wichita State University)"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Can the badge guarantee a job or university admission?",paragraphs:["No credential can guarantee employment, promotion, internship selection, or university admission.","Its value depends on:","The relevance of the selected course","Successful completion","The skills developed"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Can I take more than one free badge?",paragraphs:["Under this opportunity, each participant may register for one scholarship-supported badge course."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How long does the course take?",paragraphs:["The international badge format is expected to involve approximately eight hours of learning, with additional time for homework and assignments. The precise workload can differ according to the course."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Can badge credit be used toward a degree?",paragraphs:["In some cases, badge credit may be considered toward degree requirements, but this is not automatic. Approval depends on the program, academic requirements, and Wichita State University’s policies. (Wichita State University Catalog)"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Register for the Wichita State University Digital Badge Program 2026",paragraphs:["A small learning opportunity can become the beginning of a larger academic or professional journey.","The Wichita State University Digital Badge Program gives learners an accessible way to explore a new subject, develop a focused skill, experience international online learning, and add a university-issued credential to their profile.","With limited scholarship-supported seats available, early registration is strongly recommended.","Know More","https://onewindow.co/wsu/"],bullets:[]},{heading:"https://zfrmz.in/TiBzKqos3D8wbTtTYC6u",paragraphs:["For guidance in selecting a suitable badge and completing the registration process, connect with Team One Window."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"how-to-write-an-sop-in-2026-why-generic-and-ai-written-statements-fail-2",title:"How to Write an SOP in 2026: Why Generic and AI-Written Statements Fail",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/how-to-write-an-sop-in-2026-why-generic-and-ai-written-statements-fail/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/sop-ow-works.png",intro:"Your SOP cannot sound like ChatGPT wrote it. That may sound dramatic, but for 2026 applicants, it is a real concern. AI has changed the way students write. A clean-looking Statement of Purpose can now be generated in seconds. The grammar may be perfect. The vocabulary may sound impressive. The structure may look neat. But admissions teams are not only checking whether your English is polished. They are trying to understand something much harder: whether there is a real student behind the statement.",sections:[{heading:"How to Write an SOP in 2026: Why Generic and AI-Written Statements Fail",paragraphs:["And that is where many SOPs fail."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why This Matters More in 2026",paragraphs:["AI is no longer a small trend. OpenAI reported around 700 million weekly ChatGPT users in 2025. That means students applying in 2026 are writing in a world where AI-assisted writing is completely normal.","Application volumes are also high. Common App reported that through January 1, 2026, application volume to returning members rose 7%, from about 7.08 million applications in 2024–25 to about 7.61 million in 2025–26.","So admissions teams are not reading a few hundred personal essays in a quiet room. They are reviewing huge numbers of applications, many of which now sound more polished than before.","That creates a strange problem: when everyone sounds polished, polish stops being impressive. What stands out is specificity."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Problem With Generic SOPs",paragraphs:["Most weak SOPs do not fail because the student is weak. They fail because the writing is too broad. A generic SOP usually says things like:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"“I want global exposure and career growth.”",paragraphs:["These lines sound safe, but they do not tell the university anything useful. Passionate about what exactly? Why this course and not another one? What experience proves this interest? What career path are you actually preparing for?","A strong SOP answers these questions naturally. It does not just say, “I am interested in data science.” It explains how that interest developed. Maybe the student worked on a college project, handled messy Excel data during an internship, or noticed how businesses use analytics to make decisions. That small detail changes everything.","Generic writing tells. Human writing shows."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why AI-Written SOPs Are Risky",paragraphs:["The issue is not that AI is “bad.” AI can help students brainstorm, organize thoughts, check grammar, and improve readability. Even UCAS says AI can be useful when used correctly.","The risk begins when students copy large parts of an AI-generated statement and submit it as their own. UCAS clearly warns that generating and submitting all or a large part of a personal statement from AI, while presenting it as your own, could be considered cheating and may affect offers. For 2026 entry onwards, UCAS has also moved to a more structured personal statement format with three questions, while keeping the total limit at 4,000 characters.","Common App also treats misrepresenting AI-generated substantive content as your own as part of its fraud policy.","So the real question is not only, “Will my SOP be detected as AI?” The better question is:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Does my SOP sound like something I can honestly explain in an interview?",paragraphs:["If the answer is no, the SOP needs work."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What the Numbers Say About AI Detection",paragraphs:["AI detection is becoming more common, but it is not perfect. Turnitin says that after one year of its AI writing detector, it had reviewed over 200 million submissions. Around 22 million, roughly 11%, showed at least 20% AI writing, and more than 6 million, around 3%, showed at least 80% AI writing.","But there is another side to this. A Stanford-linked study found that GPT detectors misclassified non-native English writing at a high rate. In one test, detectors wrongly flagged TOEFL essays by non-native English writers at an average false positive rate of about 61%. In the same study, 18 out of 91 TOEFL essays were unanimously marked as AI-generated by all seven detectors, even though they were human-written.","This is why students should not think only about “beating AI detection.” That is the wrong mindset. The safer and smarter goal is to write a statement that is genuinely yours: specific, verifiable, and connected to your real academic journey."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What Universities Actually Want to See",paragraphs:["Universities are not asking for a movie-style life story. They are not expecting every student to have built a startup, published research, or solved a global problem. They are looking for clarity.","Cornell’s graduate guidance says an SOP should show academic background, preparedness, fit with the program, motivation for graduate study, and readiness for graduate school. Princeton’s graduate guidance asks applicants to explain why they are applying, outline graduate study goals, discuss academic and career plans, and include relevant academic or professional experiences.","In simple terms, a strong SOP should answer:","Why this subject?","Why this course?","Why this university?","Why are you ready now?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Where do you want this degree to take you?",paragraphs:["If your SOP answers these clearly, it will already be stronger than most template-based drafts."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How to Make an SOP More Human",paragraphs:["A human SOP has small, believable details.","Instead of writing:","“I developed leadership and communication skills during my internship.”","Write:","“During my internship, I had to explain weekly campaign results to a sales team that did not work with data every day. That experience taught me how important clear communication is when numbers are used for business decisions.”","The second version works better because it gives the reader a real situation. It shows what happened, what the student did, and what they learned.","Instead of writing:","“I am passionate about artificial intelligence.”","Write:","“My interest in AI became more serious during a college project where I built a basic image classification model. The project taught me that accuracy was only one part of the challenge. Data quality, bias, and real-world application mattered just as much.”","The second version sounds more believable. It does not try too hard. It simply gives evidence.","The Right Way to Use AI for SOP Writing","Students can use AI, but carefully.","Use AI to:","Brainstorm possible points","Organize rough notes","Check if the structure is clear","Simplify long sentences","Find repetition","Create a final review checklist","Do not use AI to:","Invent your motivation","Create fake university research","Write your full SOP"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Make every sentence sound overly polished",paragraphs:["A good rule is simple: if you cannot explain a sentence confidently in an interview, remove it or rewrite it.","Final Thought","In 2026, a good SOP does not need to sound perfect. In fact, perfect can sometimes feel suspicious. What matters is whether the statement feels real.","Admissions teams want to see your academic direction, your preparation, your reasons for choosing the course, and your understanding of the university. AI can help clean the draft, but it cannot replace your experiences, decisions, and goals.","Your SOP should sound like you on your clearest day. Not like a prompt."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Ready to Apply for the 2026 Intake?",paragraphs:["Get your SOP reviewed before submission. A strong SOP is not created in one AI prompt. It is built through reflection, research, structure, and honest editing."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"study-in-europe-how-to-compare-germany-ireland-france-the-netherlands-finland-spain-italy-and-luxembourg-by-career-fit-3",title:"Study in Europe: How to Compare Germany, Ireland, France, the Netherlands, Finland, Spain, Italy, and Luxembourg by Career Fit",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/study-in-europe-how-to-compare-germany-ireland-france-the-netherlands-finland-spain-italy-and-luxembourg-by-career-fit/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Blog-8-1024x570.jpeg",intro:"Study in Europe: How to Compare Germany, Ireland, France, the Netherlands, Finland, Spain, Italy, and Luxembourg by Career Fit “Europe” is one of the most misleading words in study abroad planning. It sounds like one destination, but for students making real career decisions, it behaves like many different worlds. Germany is not Ireland. Ireland is not the Netherlands. France is not Finland. And students who compare them casually often choose badly. That is why students should stop asking,",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["Which European country is best for study abroad?","The better question is:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Which country in Europe is best for my course, my budget, and my career goals?",paragraphs:["For Indian students especially, this comparison matters more than ever. Students are no longer going abroad only for a degree. They are thinking about employability, work rights, post-study opportunities, salary potential, and long-term return on investment. A country that is excellent for engineering may not be the best fit for luxury management. A destination that looks affordable may not support the kind of career path a student wants. A country with many English-taught programs may still be difficult for long-term employability if local language matters in the job market.","So if students want to make a smart study in Europe decision, they need to compare countries by career fit, not just by popularity."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why Career Fit Matters More Than Country Hype",paragraphs:["A lot of students begin with shallow comparisons. They ask where friends are going, which destination is trending, or which country sounds more affordable. Those are understandable starting points, but they are not enough to make a strong decision.","A better comparison starts with a few practical questions:","Which country is strong in my field?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Does the country’s academic style suit the way I learn?",paragraphs:["Once students compare Europe through these questions, the shortlist becomes much smarter."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Study in Germany: Best for Engineering, Applied Sciences, and Technical Depth",paragraphs:["Study in Germany is often one of the strongest choices for students interested in engineering, automotive technology, manufacturing, computer science, data, and applied sciences. Germany’s appeal is closely tied to the structure of its economy. It has a strong industrial base, globally respected technical education, and a serious academic culture.","For students who want technical depth and a more structured academic environment, Germany can be a very strong fit. Many students also see value in Germany because public higher education is often more affordable than in several other global destinations.","But Germany is not the right fit for everyone. Students need to be realistic about the level of independence expected in the classroom, the documentation-heavy process, and the importance of local language for long-term integration. A student can complete an English-taught course, but in many sectors, German language ability still improves employability after graduation.","Study in Germany makes most sense for students who want a strong technical profile, are comfortable with an academically serious system, and are willing to adapt for long-term reward."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Study in Ireland: Strong for Tech, Pharma, Business, and English-Speaking Access",paragraphs:["Study in Ireland appeals to a different kind of student. Ireland is often attractive because it combines an English-speaking education system with strong visibility in technology, pharmaceuticals, finance, analytics, and business.","For Indian students who want Europe but prefer to study in English, Ireland is often easier to understand and navigate than some other destinations. It is especially attractive for students targeting software, data, business analytics, finance, international business, and life-science-linked sectors.","One reason study in Ireland stands out is the employer presence. The Irish market has strong multinational visibility, and that matters to students who want to build a degree plus career story in one place.","That said, Ireland is not always the best option for students focused mainly on low-cost living or lower tuition. It works better for students who are prioritising English-medium study, employability, and alignment with industries that already have a strong presence there."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Study in France: Best for Business, Luxury, Hospitality, Design, and Selective Institutions",paragraphs:["Study in France works well for students who want a country with strong international prestige, a diverse higher education system, and real career value in areas like luxury, management, hospitality, design, public policy, and international business.","France stands out because its education system is not one-dimensional. Students can choose between public universities, grandes écoles, engineering schools, business schools, and specialised institutions. This makes France especially attractive for students with specific subject interests and strong professional direction.","Students looking at study in France often find it especially compelling for luxury brand management, fashion-related education, hospitality, political studies, and business. It can also be strong in engineering and public research depending on the institution.","But France must be compared carefully. Some students may be drawn in by prestige without fully understanding the importance of institution type, language expectations, or selectivity. France can be a great career fit, but it suits students who are prepared to think strategically about the exact type of school and the long-term value of the program."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Study in the Netherlands: Ideal for International Classrooms and Applied Learning",paragraphs:["Study in the Netherlands is often a strong fit for students who want modern teaching styles, English-taught programs, and an internationally oriented classroom experience.","The Dutch system is especially useful for students who want flexibility in academic style. It offers both research universities and universities of applied sciences, which means students can choose between more theory-driven and more practical, profession-oriented pathways.","The Netherlands is often attractive for business, economics, logistics, design, sustainability, social sciences, engineering, and data-related fields. It also works well for students who want a more interactive and discussion-led academic culture.","For Indian students, study in the Netherlands often feels like a strong middle ground: international, English-accessible, academically serious, and professionally aware. But students should also keep in mind the cost of living and the competitiveness of housing. The Netherlands is a strong fit for students who value adaptability, classroom participation, and a career-oriented education experience."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Study in Finland: Strong for Innovation, Sustainability, and Future-Focused Education",paragraphs:["Study in Finland is often attractive to students who want a calm, modern, and innovation-focused academic environment. Finland’s education reputation is built on quality, student-centered systems, and a serious commitment to long-term thinking.","Students interested in study in Finland often look at information technology, AI, engineering, sustainability, business, design, healthcare, and education. Finland is especially appealing to students who want a future-facing academic system rather than a traditional prestige-led destination.","The country also offers a clear distinction between universities and universities of applied sciences, which helps students choose based on learning style. Some students thrive in Finland because the system values independence, problem-solving, and practical thinking rather than rote academic performance.","For the right student, Finland offers strong academic quality and a modern learning culture. It is especially good for those who value innovation, structure, and a more focused student environment."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Study in Spain: Good for Business, Hospitality, Tourism, and Student Lifestyle",paragraphs:["Study in Spain appeals to students who want a balance between academic value and a richer lifestyle experience. Spain is often considered by students interested in hospitality, tourism, international business, design, communication, and social sciences.","One of Spain’s strengths is that it offers a broad student experience. Cities like Madrid, Barcelona, Valencia, Seville, and Salamanca each provide different kinds of student life, and that variety matters for students comparing Europe more carefully.","Spain also works well for students who want to combine academics with language growth, especially if they see Spanish as a long-term asset. Study in Spain makes sense for students who want a more balanced mix of academics, lifestyle, and global exposure. It is especially attractive for those in business, hospitality, tourism, and communication-related fields."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Study in Italy: Best for Design, Fashion, Architecture, and Culture-Led Education",paragraphs:["Study in Italy is especially strong for students who want a destination with clear creative identity. Italy is often shortlisted for design, fashion, architecture, arts, luxury-linked business, and some engineering and business pathways.","What makes Italy different is that it is not just a place to earn a degree. For many students, it becomes part of the educational experience itself. The cultural environment, visual identity, design history, and academic heritage all shape how students experience the country.","That is why study in Italy works especially well for students in creative and design-led fields. It can also suit students looking for business programs with a luxury, fashion, or branding angle. Italy is stronger for students who want education tied to creativity, aesthetics, architecture, and cultural capital rather than a straightforward utilitarian path into industry."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Study in Luxembourg: Small Destination, Strong Fit for Finance and Multilingual Careers",paragraphs:["Study in Luxembourg is a more specialised option, but for the right student, it can be highly relevant. Luxembourg is often best suited to students interested in finance, business, accounting, European studies, law, governance, and multilingual professional environments.","Its value is not based on scale. Luxembourg does not offer the wide university landscape of larger countries. Its strength lies in its location, international character, financial ecosystem, and cross-border European positioning.","Students considering study in Luxembourg should usually be looking for a specific fit rather than a broad destination experience. It suits students who want a compact, international, finance-oriented environment and who understand that career logic matters more here than destination hype."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How Students Should Compare These Countries in Real Life",paragraphs:["The smartest way to compare European countries is not by asking which one is “best overall.” There is no universal answer to that. Instead, students should build shortlists around their own profile:","Preferred course and subject area"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Work expectations during and after study",paragraphs:["A student interested in engineering may find Germany or Finland more logical than Spain or Italy. A student targeting business and tech may find Ireland or the Netherlands more aligned. A student interested in luxury, hospitality, or design may find France or Italy much stronger than a more technical destination. A student thinking about finance and multilingual European careers may find Luxembourg a better fit than larger but more generic alternatives.","This is why “Europe” should never be treated as one destination category.","The Real Takeaway","Students do not choose Europe. They choose countries inside Europe, and those countries offer very different academic and professional realities.","Study in Germany is not the same as study in Ireland. Study in France is not the same as study in the Netherlands. Study in Finland, study in Spain, study in Italy, and study in Luxembourg all lead to different experiences and outcomes.","So before asking which European country is best, students should ask which country fits their career direction best. That one shift in thinking leads to better shortlists, better decisions, and better long-term outcomes."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"study-abroad-is-becoming-employment-led-what-indian-students-must-understand-4",title:"Study Abroad Is Becoming Employment-Led: What Indian Students Must Understand",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/study-abroad-is-becoming-employment-led-what-indian-students-must-understand/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/WhatsApp-Image-2026-07-07-at-13.21.17-1024x570.jpeg",intro:"For a long time, the Indian study abroad story was told in a very simple sequence. Get the grades. Build the profile. Secure the admittance. Arrange the finances. Get the visa. Fly out. That sequence still exists, but in 2026, it is no longer enough. A student can do every one of those things correctly and still make a weak study abroad decision if the course, country, and career pathway do not align.",sections:[{heading:"Study Abroad Is Becoming Employment-Led: What Indian Students Must Understand",paragraphs:["That is because the global study abroad market is no longer admission-led in the way it once was. It is becoming employment-led.","Students are still applying for degrees, but families are increasingly paying for outcomes: employability, work rights, career mobility, salary potential, industry relevance, and long-term security. In other words, the old question was,","Can I get in?","The new question is,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What will happen after I get in?",paragraphs:["That is the shift Indian students must understand.","Because today, a foreign degree is not valuable simply because it is foreign. It is valuable only if it builds the right skills, in the right market, with the right post-study pathway, at the right time.","That is why admission is not the finish line anymore. Employability is the test."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Old Study Abroad Playbook Is Weakening",paragraphs:["Part of the problem is that many students still use an older model of thinking. They choose destinations based on reputation, social proof, family familiarity, or the belief that any degree from a popular country will automatically lead to work.","That assumption has become much harder to defend.","The World Economic Forum’s Future of Jobs Report 2025, published on January 7, 2025, found that 39% of workers’ core skills are expected to change by 2030. The report says demand is rising not only for technical skills like AI and big data, networks and cybersecurity, and technological literacy, but also for human capabilities like creative thinking, resilience, leadership, and social influence.","LinkedIn’s Work Change Report, released in January 2025, makes the picture even sharper. It says that by 2030, 70% of the skills used in most jobs will change, and that since 2022, the rate at which LinkedIn users add new skills to their profiles has increased by 140%.","That means a degree is no longer enough if it does not translate into a marketable skill stack. Students are not walking into stable, slow-moving job markets. They are graduating into fast-changing, employer-filtered, AI-affected, immigration-linked systems. That is why study abroad decisions now need to be made like career decisions, not just education decisions."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Indian Students Are Still Moving at Scale, But the Risks Are Clearer Now",paragraphs:["According to a Ministry of External Affairs reply tabled in the Rajya Sabha on December 4, 2025, there were 1,254,013 Indian students in universities and tertiary institutions abroad as of January 1, 2025, across 153 countries. If students in schools are included, the total reaches 1,882,318.","That is a huge national footprint. But the same parliamentary reply also shows the harder side of international mobility. Over the last five years, Indian students were deported or denied entry in multiple destinations:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"114 Indian students deported from Australia",paragraphs:["The reasons cited by the MEA include:","Breach of student visa conditions"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Undertaking unauthorised employment",paragraphs:["Inability to maintain required financial balances","Non-payment of university fees","Insufficient attendance"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Inability to answer basic questions about the chosen field of study",paragraphs:["That last point is especially revealing. Students are not failing only because of bad luck or difficult immigration systems. In some cases, they are failing because the study decision itself was weak, underprepared, or misaligned."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Market Is Now Asking Harder Questions",paragraphs:["Host countries are increasingly selective about what kind of international students they want, what programs they will support, and what pathways they are willing to extend after graduation.","Canada","remains a major destination, but its post-study logic is now more labor-market-aware than before. Since November 1, 2024, most PGWP applicants must submit proof of language results. Canada’s field of study rules have also become more important, with IRCC now explicitly linking eligible programs to occupations facing long-term shortage. For 2026, Canada will not add or remove any eligible fields from the PGWP list. Canada is no longer saying, “Study almost anything and you’ll figure it out.” It is increasingly saying, “Your pathway after graduation depends on what you studied and whether it matches labor demand.”","The UK","still has strong academic brand value, but its migration policy is clearly becoming more outcome-conscious. As of July 6, 2026, the official GOV.UK Graduate visa guidance states:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"PhD graduates continue to receive 3 years",paragraphs:["The government’s own explanatory memorandum from October 14, 2025 makes the logic clear: too many graduates staying under the route were not moving into graduate-level roles the route was meant to support. The UK has also significantly reduced flexibility for dependents — in the year ending May 2026, applications from student dependents were 86% lower than before the rule change effective January 1, 2024.","The Netherlands","provides a useful contrast. The Dutch orientation year route remains a strong employment-facing feature. As updated by IND on June 12, 2026, graduates can stay for a year after graduation to look for work or start a business. But that only benefits students whose course profile, sector fit, and employability are strong enough to use that year well.","That is the recurring pattern across destinations: work rights matter, but only if the graduate is ready to turn them into work.","Degrees Do Not Employ Students. Profiles Do.","A university admits a student into a course. An employer hires a person into a role. Those are not the same decisions.","A student can graduate from a known university and still struggle if the profile is too generic. A student can also graduate from a less glamorous institution and do well if the degree is industry-aligned, skill-rich, and backed by internships, projects, and communication strength.","This is why curriculum matters more than course title alone. A generic business management degree is not the same as a business program with:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Internships and real-world application",paragraphs:["Employability sits in the difference between those versions."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Evidence on Outcomes Is More Uncomfortable Than Most Students Expect",paragraphs:["The OECD’s International Students in Higher Education work, published in 2026, makes several points Indian students should take seriously.","First, there is often a tension between recruitment messaging and the reality of post-graduation opportunities. Second, international graduates often start with lower employment rates or salaries than domestic graduates, even when they remain in the destination country — local language proficiency, relevant work experience, internships, and employer networks are key factors that make the transition easier.","Third, outcomes vary sharply by country. In Australia, for example, the full-time employment rate a few months after graduation was 74% for domestic graduates but only 52% for international graduates who stayed. That is a serious gap.","Australia’s own Jobs and Skills Australia study, released on September 25, 2025, reinforces the point. It was commissioned to understand how education, migration, and employment systems interact — and its very existence signals that governments are increasingly judging international education as a workforce pipeline, not just a study experience."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Internships, Work Experience, and Networks Are No Longer “Nice to Have”",paragraphs:["NACE’s Job Outlook 2025 found that employers continue to prioritise:","Problem-solving skills (88.3%)"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Ability to work in a team (81.0%)",paragraphs:["Written communication skills (77.1%)","Initiative (73.7%)","Technical skills (73.2%)"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Verbal communication skills (69.3%)",paragraphs:["Employers are not only screening for technical competence. They are screening for work readiness.","NACE’s internship data adds more weight. On April 20, 2026, NACE reported that employers expected to increase intern hiring by 3.9% in 2025–26, with 81% planning to maintain or increase internship hires. The average intern conversion rate for 2024–25 reached 63.1%, the highest in five years.","Internships are not side activities. They are one of the clearest bridges between study and employment. If a course abroad does not meaningfully improve access to internships, projects, lab work, or employer contact, students should ask harder questions about what exactly they are paying for."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Real-World Employability Failures Are Often Visible Much Earlier",paragraphs:["The most avoidable mistakes often happen before graduation. Recurring issues flagged in Indian government advisories across destinations including Canada, Germany, Finland, Ireland, and others include:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Misunderstanding local immigration rules",paragraphs:["Employability starts before class even begins. It starts with whether the student chose a credible program, understood the labor market, and entered the country with realistic expectations.","Strong advising in 2026 should ask:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Does the student’s background make that route realistic?",paragraphs:["That is the difference between selling study abroad and planning a career through study abroad."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What Indian Students Should Do Differently Now",paragraphs:["If study abroad is becoming employment-led, Indian students need a different checklist. They should ask:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Does this degree create a profile employers actually want?",paragraphs:["They should also separate three things that are often confused: university brand, degree content, and market value. Sometimes all three are strong together. Sometimes they are not. A famous brand with a vague course can still be a weak employability choice. A less flashy university in a strong labor market with a highly applied course can be a much smarter decision."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The New Rule Is Simple, Even If the Planning Is Not",paragraphs:["A study abroad decision should now begin with employability and work backward to admission, not the other way around.","In a labor market where:","39% of core skills are expected to change by 2030"],bullets:[]},{heading:"International graduates often face a real outcome gap versus domestic peers",paragraphs:["…students cannot afford a casual study abroad strategy.","The winners in this market will not simply be the students who get into foreign universities. They will be the ones who choose employment-relevant programs, build strong profiles, accumulate work exposure early, and understand the real labor logic of the destinations they enter.","Admission is important. It always will be. But admission is only the opening move.","Employability is the test."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"is-your-degree-ai-proof-what-students-should-know-before-choosing-a-course-abroad-5",title:"Is Your Degree AI-Proof? What Students Should Know Before Choosing a Course Abroad",tag:"Exams",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/is-your-degree-ai-proof-what-students-should-know-before-choosing-a-course-abroad/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Blog-8-1024x570.jpg",intro:"Choosing a course abroad has always been a high-stakes decision. Students are not just choosing a subject. They are choosing a country, a tuition burden, a future job market, and often the first few years of their adult life. In 2026, there is one more layer to that decision: artificial intelligence. Students and parents are now asking a question that barely came up a few years ago: will this degree still matter when AI becomes part of almost every workplace? It is the right question, but it is often framed the wrong way. The real issue is not whether a degree is completely “AI-proof.” Almost no degree is protected from change. The better question is this: will this course help you stay valuable in a world where AI takes over more routine tasks?",sections:[{heading:"Is Your Degree AI-Proof? What Students Should Know Before Choosing a Course Abroad",paragraphs:["That difference matters.","AI is not changing the job market in a simple way. It is not just “destroying jobs” or “creating jobs.” It is changing the mix of skills inside jobs. Some tasks are being automated. Some are being sped up. Some are becoming more valuable because humans still need to guide, judge, explain, and improve what AI produces.","That is why students need to stop thinking only in terms of degree titles and start thinking in terms of skills, exposure, and adaptability."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What the Data Is Saying",paragraphs:["Recent global data supports this shift.","The World Economic Forum’s Future of Jobs Report 2025, published on January 7, 2025, found that 39% of workers’ key skills are expected to change by 2030. It also found that the fastest-growing roles include big data specialists, fintech engineers, and AI and machine learning specialists, while many clerical and administrative roles are expected to decline. The same report says 86% of employers expect AI and information-processing technologies to transform their business by 2030.","That means students choosing courses today are not entering the same labour market that existed even five years ago.","LinkedIn’s Work Change Report, released in January 2025, adds another important layer. It says that by 2030, 70% of the skills used in most jobs will change, with AI acting as a major catalyst. LinkedIn also reported a 140% increase since 2022 in the pace at which professionals are adding new skills to their profiles. That is a powerful signal: the winners in this market are not the people clinging to one static qualification. They are the people who keep updating their skill stack.","The International Labour Organization made another important point in its May 20, 2025 update on generative AI and jobs. It found that one in four workers globally is in an occupation with some degree of generative AI exposure. But the bigger takeaway is this: the ILO says most jobs will be transformed rather than made redundant. AI is often changing work more than eliminating it outright."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What Makes a Degree More Resilient",paragraphs:["A future-ready degree usually does at least three things well:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"It gives students practical exposure, not just theory",paragraphs:["This is why the safest degrees are not always the most obviously technical ones. Yes, computer science, AI, machine learning, robotics, data science, and cybersecurity remain strong because they sit close to where investment and demand are growing. But many non-technical fields can also remain highly valuable if taught in the right way.","Healthcare is a good example. AI can support diagnosis, records, imaging, and workflow, but medicine, nursing, physiotherapy, mental health, and public health still rely heavily on human accountability, trust, communication, and ethical judgment.","The same applies to psychology, education, law, design, business, public policy, and communications. AI may assist the work, but it does not remove the need for people who can interpret complex situations, make decisions, understand human behavior, and communicate with nuance."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Course Design Matters More Than Course Branding",paragraphs:["A business degree that only teaches broad theory may leave students more exposed than one that includes analytics, digital transformation, operations, strategic communication, and industry projects.","A media degree that focuses only on basic content production may be weaker than one that teaches audience insight, storytelling, brand judgment, creative direction, and campaign strategy.","A computer science degree that teaches syntax without systems thinking, product thinking, and real-world application may be less powerful than a more applied program in the same field.","In short, generic profiles are under pressure. Stronger, more applied, more adaptive profiles are gaining value."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Does Your Destination Country Matter?",paragraphs:["A degree becomes more future-ready when it is connected to a real industry ecosystem.","Germany","— Engineering and applied sciences","Ireland","— Tech, pharmaceuticals, analytics, and business","Netherlands","— English-taught, internationally oriented, applied education","France","— Luxury, business, design, hospitality, and policy","Finland","— Innovation, sustainability, and future-focused education","UK"],bullets:[]},{heading:"— Finance, law, management, research, and one-year postgraduate study",paragraphs:["Students should not choose only by country popularity. They should ask whether the country’s economy actually supports the field they want to enter.","What the Big Names in AI Are Actually Saying","Satya Nadella",", Microsoft’s CEO, has consistently argued that AI should work like a co-pilot, not a replacement for human agency. His framing suggests that the future belongs to people who know how to direct AI, not just compete against it.","Sam Altman",", OpenAI’s CEO, argued in OpenAI’s official Built to Benefit Everyone plan, published in June 2026, that the future should not be about automating everything. As AI becomes more capable, the human role becomes more important in setting direction, making trade-offs, applying judgment, and bringing values, taste, and responsibility to work.","Jensen Huang",", NVIDIA’s CEO, told Carnegie Mellon graduates in May 2026 that retreating from technology only gives up the chance to benefit from it. Avoiding AI is not a strategy. Learning how to work with it is.","Demis Hassabis",", CEO of Google DeepMind, said in September 2025 that the most important future skill may be “learning how to learn.” If the workplace is changing fast, the most valuable graduate is not just the one with today’s hot skill, but the one who can keep learning across an entire career.","Dario Amodei",", CEO of Anthropic, has taken a more cautionary view. In a May 2025 interview, he warned that AI could eliminate up to 50% of entry-level white-collar jobs within one to five years — a signal that routine knowledge work could be hit faster than many expect.","Put together, the lesson is not “panic” and it is not “ignore the hype.” The lesson is: choose carefully."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Does the curriculum teach applied skills?",paragraphs:["Internships, labs, portfolios, capstones, case studies, and industry exposure make a course stronger than one that is purely theoretical."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Does it build human strengths AI cannot easily replace?",paragraphs:["Judgment, communication, empathy, leadership, ethical reasoning, creativity, collaboration, and complex decision-making."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Does it teach digital and AI fluency?",paragraphs:["Even non-technical students should graduate knowing how AI affects their field and how to use it responsibly."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Is the degree connected to a strong destination ecosystem?",paragraphs:["A good course in the wrong market may still leave students with weak opportunities."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Does the university seem awake to the AI shift?",paragraphs:["UNESCO IESALC warned in January 2026 that many universities still have a gap between fast AI adoption and proper student preparation. A university with no serious answer to AI may be falling behind."],bullets:[]},{heading:"So, Is Your Degree AI-Proof?",paragraphs:["Probably not in the absolute sense. But it can absolutely be AI-ready, AI-relevant, and much more resilient if it teaches the right mix of technical fluency, human strengths, and real-world application.","The most AI-resilient students will probably not come from one single degree category. They will come from programs that combine subject expertise with flexibility, applied learning, and strong human judgment.","That is what students should really be looking for before choosing a course abroad."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"beyond-the-cv-how-studying-in-luxembourg-can-change-the-way-employers-see-you-6",title:"Beyond the CV: How Studying in Luxembourg Can Change the Way Employers See You",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/beyond-the-cv-how-studying-in-luxembourg-can-change-the-way-employers-see-you/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Blog-7-1024x570.jpeg",intro:"Most students think they can build a stronger CV by adding more lines. One more degree. One more internship.",sections:[{heading:"Beyond the CV: How Studying in Luxembourg Can Change the Way Employers See You",paragraphs:["One more certificate.","One more country name.","So the study abroad decision often becomes a race to collect credentials that look impressive on paper.","But employers do not read a CV only as a list.","They read it as a signal.","A degree tells them what you studied. Your exposure tells them what kind of professional environment shaped you.","That is the difference many students miss.","The real value of premium study exposure is not only that it gives you a foreign qualification. It changes how you understand work, how you speak about industries, how you carry yourself in professional conversations, and how confidently you connect your education to the market.","This is where Luxembourg becomes an interesting case.","Not because it is the most obvious study destination.","Not because it is the biggest.","Luxembourg is one of those rare countries where students do not have to be far away from the professional world they are trying to enter.","Luxembourg is small in size but highly concentrated in terms of finance, investment funds, cross-border business, multilingual work culture, EU-linked institutions, professional services, fintech, and sustainable finance.","For the right student, that concentration can do something a normal CV line cannot.","It can recalibrate how employers see your readiness.","Your CV Is Not Just a List. It Is a Signal.","A CV does not speak only through words. It also speaks through context.","When an employer sees where you studied, the environment you were exposed to, the internships or projects you completed, the markets you understand, and how you explain your choices, they begin to form a picture of you.","They see you as more than just a student.","As a potential professional, employers can read you very differently.","This is why employers can read two students with similar degrees very differently."],bullets:[]},{heading:"“I completed a master’s in finance.”",paragraphs:["“I studied finance in an environment where investment funds, regulation, cross-border business, multilingual teams, and sustainable finance were part of the country’s professional reality.”","The degree may look similar.","But the second student carries more context.","That context matters.","High-tier employers are rarely impressed by academic qualifications alone. They are looking for signs of maturity, adaptability, communication, judgment, and professional awareness.","They want to know whether a graduate can understand the world outside the classroom.","This is where the environment begins to shape employability."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Hidden ROI of Premium Study Exposure",paragraphs:["When families discuss the ROI of studying abroad, the conversation usually turns to finances:","Tuition fees","Living costs","Part-time work","Post-study options","Salary expectations","Loan repayment","All of this matters.","But there is another kind of ROI that is harder to measure and often more powerful in the long run.","The ROI of exposure.","Exposure changes the student before the job market even evaluates them.","How industries speak","How professionals think","How networks operate","How business decisions are made","How regulation affects opportunity"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How confidence is built through proximity, not theory",paragraphs:["This kind of learning does not always happen through a lecture.","Premium study exposure is not about luxury.","It is about calibration."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why Luxembourg Creates a Different Professional Context",paragraphs:["Luxembourg should not be understood only as a study destination.","It should be understood as a professional environment.","The country has built a strong position in Europe’s financial and business landscape.","It is especially known for:","Investment funds","Banking","Cross-border finance","Wealth management","Insurance","Fintech","Sustainable finance"],bullets:[]},{heading:"European institutional activity",paragraphs:["Such an environment creates a very different kind of background for students.","A finance student in Luxembourg is not studying finance in isolation.","They are studying in a country where finance is part of the national identity.","That does not mean studying in Luxembourg automatically creates career success.","It does not.","But it can give students something valuable:","professional context."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Vocabulary of the Boardroom Starts Earlier",paragraphs:["There is a certain kind of confidence that does not come from memorising theory.","It comes from being around serious environments long enough to understand their rhythm.","Students begin to notice:","How people introduce themselves","How professionals discuss problems","How markets are connected"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How opportunity often moves through relationships",paragraphs:["You do not just learn the vocabulary of the boardroom.","You slowly begin to understand when and how to use it.","What Employers May Read Between the Lines","Employers are not simply asking:","“Does this student have a degree?”","They are asking:","“What does this experience say about the person?”","A Luxembourg study experience can suggest that the student has experienced a serious European business environment.","Luxembourg is multilingual and multicultural.","A student who studies there must learn to operate in an environment where people come from different countries and bring different ways of working.","Choosing Luxembourg is not the usual crowd decision.","It often signals a more deliberate career-oriented choice.","Luxembourg gives students proximity to industries.","Not guaranteed access. Not automatic employment.","But proximity.","Professional confidence comes from understanding your field beyond definitions.","It comes from seeing how classroom ideas appear in real organisations.","The Before-and-After Effect of Exposure","Before Exposure","“I want to work in business.”","After Exposure","“I want to work in international business environments where strategy, regulation, data, finance, and multicultural execution come together.”","Before Exposure","“I studied management.”","After Exposure","“Management is about coordinating across markets, cultures, rules, and business expectations.”","Before Exposure","“I want a finance career.”","After Exposure","“I am interested in how financial markets connect with fund structures, compliance, risk, sustainable investment, and European regulation.”","A Degree Can Sit Quietly. Exposure Should Not.","A degree, by itself, can become silent.","Exposure gives the student stories, examples, confidence, and context.","It helps them explain why they chose a field and how it connects to the market.","It gives the degree a voice."],bullets:["Context","Adaptability","Serious Intent","Industry Proximity","Professional Confidence"]},{heading:"But Luxembourg Will Not Do the Work for You",paragraphs:["An environment can create opportunity.","It cannot create effort.","Luxembourg will not transform a passive student.","Students still need to:","Ask questions","Attend events","Build relationships","Understand the local market","Improve communication","Learn professional language","Take internships seriously"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Build their profile proactively",paragraphs:["Luxembourg can place you near important rooms.","You still have to earn your place inside them."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why This Matters for Indian Students",paragraphs:["Many families still evaluate study abroad through country popularity.","UK feels familiar","Canada feels safe","Australia feels common","Germany feels affordable","US feels aspirational","But the better question is:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"“What kind of professional environment will shape the student?”",paragraphs:["For students interested in finance, business, fintech, regulation, sustainability, data, international management, or European markets, Luxembourg may offer a sharper kind of exposure.","Not broader.","Sharper.","The CV Is Only the Surface","A strong CV is important.","But a CV is only the surface of your career story.","Behind it, employers are trying to understand:","What shaped your thinking?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What environments have you handled?",paragraphs:["What professional language do you understand?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Can you operate in international spaces?",paragraphs:["Luxembourg gives students the opportunity to build a story that goes beyond:","“I studied abroad.”","The stronger story is:","“I studied in an environment that helped me understand international business, regulation, finance, culture, and professional expectations more deeply.”","Final Thought: Don’t Just Add a Degree. Build a Professional Signal.","A degree tells people what you studied.","Exposure tells them what kind of professional world has shaped you.","That is why Luxembourg deserves a different kind of attention.","Not as a mass-market study destination","Not as a shortcut"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Not as a guaranteed career ticket",paragraphs:["But as a premium, concentrated, internationally connected environment where the right student can build more than academic knowledge.","Professional readiness","Confidence","Context"],bullets:[]},{heading:"A stronger career signal",paragraphs:["At One Window Overseas Education, we help students and families look beyond country popularity and ask a more useful question:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Which destination will actually shape the student’s future?",paragraphs:["Because studying abroad should not only help you collect a degree.","It should help you become someone the market can understand, remember, and trust.","Thinking About Studying in Luxembourg?","Speak to"],bullets:[]},{heading:"One Window Overseas Education",paragraphs:["to understand whether Luxembourg fits your profile, academic goals, budget, and long-term career direction.","Book a free counselling session and make your study abroad decision with clarity."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"study-in-poland-for-indian-students-cost-career-scope-benefits-and-why-it-makes-sense-in-2026-7",title:"Study in Poland for Indian Students: Cost, Career Scope, Benefits and Why It Makes Sense in 2026",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/study-in-poland-for-indian-students-cost-career-scope-benefits-and-why-it-makes-sense-in-2026/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Blog-6-1024x570.png",intro:"The Indian student going abroad today is not thinking the way students did ten years ago. Earlier, the study abroad dream was simpler. Get into a foreign university. Move to a big country. Build a life abroad. Hope the investment pays off. For many years, that dream usually had the same four names attached to it: the US, UK, Canada, and Australia. These countries still have value. They still offer strong universities, global recognition, and large international student communities.",sections:[{heading:"Study in Poland for Indian Students: Cost, Career Scope, Benefits and Why It Makes Sense in 2026",paragraphs:["But the decision-making has changed.","Indian students and parents are no longer choosing a country only because it sounds prestigious. They are asking harder, more practical questions before the first application is even sent."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Will this destination still make sense five years from now?",paragraphs:["This shift is precisely why Poland is entering the conversation.","Not as the loudest study abroad destination. Not as the most glamorous option. But as a practical European option for students who want international education with better financial sense, real exposure, and a more balanced route into Europe.","For a new generation of Indian students, studying abroad is no longer only about the brand of the country. It is about outcomes. And Poland is quietly becoming part of that outcome-driven conversation."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Poland is benefiting from a new study abroad mindset",paragraphs:["The biggest change in Indian study abroad behaviour is that students are becoming more strategic.","They are not only asking: “Which country is famous?” They are asking: “Which country fits me?” That one question changes everything.","A student from a middle-class family may want global exposure but may not want to take on a very large loan. A student interested in technology may want a country where business and IT sectors are growing. A student interested in logistics may want exposure to a European economy where manufacturing, supply chains, and services matter. A business student may want an international classroom without the cost pressure of the UK or US.","Poland fits into this new behaviour because it offers a different kind of value. It is not trying to compete with the US on scale. It is not trying to compete with the UK on historical prestige. It is not trying to sell the same old immigration story that Canada once represented for many students.","Its value is more practical:","European education","Comparatively affordable study options","Growing cities","English-taught programmes"],bullets:[]},{heading:"A student experience that can feel more financially manageable for many Indian families",paragraphs:["That combination matters because students are no longer just buying the dream of studying abroad. They are calculating the deal."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The affordability angle is real, but it should not be oversold",paragraphs:["One of Poland’s biggest advantages is cost. For many Indian families, this is where the conversation begins. Tuition fees, rent, food, insurance, travel, visa costs, emergency expenses, and daily living expenses all add up quickly. A country may look attractive in a brochure, but the actual monthly and yearly cost can change the entire decision.","Poland is often seen as more affordable than traditional destinations such as the US, UK, Canada, and Australia. For students who want a European education without entering the most expensive study abroad markets, this is a major reason to consider it.","But affordability should not be misunderstood. Poland should not be sold as “cheap Europe.” A better way to describe Poland is “value-conscious Europe.” There is a difference.","Cheap means the student is only looking at price.","Value-conscious means the student is looking at the complete equation: What am I paying? What quality of education am I getting? What city will I live in? What industries exist around me? What exposure can I build? What kind of profile will I have after graduation? Will the degree make sense in India, Europe, or elsewhere?","A lower-cost destination is only useful if the course, institution, city, and career direction make sense together. Otherwise, even an affordable country can become a poor decision. This is where proper shortlisting becomes important.","Poland can be a smart destination, but only when the student chooses the right programme, the right city, and the right academic pathway."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Poland’s economy makes the destination more relevant",paragraphs:["A country’s economy matters more than many students realise. Students often focus only on the university or the course. But the country around the university plays a major role in shaping the student’s exposure.","It decides the industries around them. It decides the kind of companies they may interact with. It decides the internship culture. It decides whether the city has business activity. It decides what kind of international work environment the student gets to observe.","Poland has become one of Central Europe’s important economies. It has developed strong activity across sectors such as technology, manufacturing, logistics, fintech, business services, shared services, and research-related work.","This matters for Indian students because these are not abstract industries. They connect directly to the kind of courses many students are now choosing: computer science, data analytics, management, finance, engineering, supply chain, logistics, business, international management, and technology-led programmes.","Cities like Warsaw, Kraków, Wrocław, Gdańsk, Łódź, and Poznań are increasingly important in this conversation:","Warsaw","— the capital and a major business centre","Kraków","— known for its student environment and strong services/technology ecosystem","Wrocław","— a visible centre for business, technology, and international companies","Gdańsk","— connects strongly with trade, logistics, ports, and regional business activity","Łódź","— becoming more relevant for students looking at business, logistics, and creative industries","Poznań"],bullets:[]},{heading:"— has a strong business and academic environment",paragraphs:["For Indian students, the city matters almost as much as the country. This is why Poland should not be discussed only as an affordable study destination. It should be discussed as a country where cost, city, and employability can be connected more carefully."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Poland gives students exposure to European work culture",paragraphs:["Studying abroad is not only about lectures, assignments, and exams. The real value often comes from what happens around the degree.","Students learn how to manage themselves.","They learn how to communicate with people from different countries.","They learn how professional expectations differ across cultures.","They learn how to live independently.","They learn how to solve problems without family support nearby.","They learn how to present themselves in international environments.","Poland can offer Indian students this kind of European exposure without the same financial pressure that often comes with more expensive destinations.","Even if a student eventually returns to India, this exposure can still be valuable. A graduate who has studied in Europe may understand multicultural teams better. They may be more confident in international communication. They may have a stronger sense of independence. They may be more comfortable adapting to different systems. They may bring a broader perspective into Indian workplaces, family businesses, startups, or multinational companies.","Return on investment is not only about the first job after graduation. It is also about what kind of person and professional the student becomes through the experience."],bullets:[]},{heading:"English-taught programmes make Poland accessible, but language still matters",paragraphs:["One of the reasons Poland is becoming more attractive is the availability of English-taught programmes in several fields. This is important for Indian students because language can be one of the biggest barriers in Europe.","English-taught programmes reduce that barrier. They make Poland more accessible for students who want a European education but are not ready to study fully in another language.","However, this should not create a false sense of comfort. English may be enough for the classroom. But it may not be enough for daily life, networking, internships, part-time work, or local career opportunities.","Students who learn basic Polish can improve their day-to-day experience. They may find it easier to communicate in public places, build local connections, understand the culture, and explore opportunities outside the classroom.","A student who treats Poland as an English-only experience may limit themselves. A student who makes even a basic effort to understand the local language and culture may have a better experience."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Europe advantage is bigger than Poland alone",paragraphs:["When students choose Poland, they are not just choosing one country. They are entering the broader European education and professional ecosystem. This is one of Poland’s strongest advantages.","Europe gives students exposure to a region where countries are closely connected through trade, mobility, academic cooperation, business networks, and cultural exchange. Students can experience international classrooms, meet people from different countries, and understand how European systems operate.","For ambitious Indian students, this matters:","A degree gives academic value. The European environment gives perspective.","Students begin to understand how industries work across borders.","They see how companies operate in multicultural settings.","They learn how regulations, business practices, and workplace expectations vary across countries.","They become more aware of international mobility and professional possibilities.","This wider exposure can be valuable whether the student wants to build a career in Europe, return to India, or move into global roles later. Poland offers access to this European environment in a way that can be more financially practical for many families."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Poland fits students who are practical, not passive",paragraphs:["Poland is not for every student. That needs to be said clearly. No country should be sold as perfect.","Poland may suit students who are practical, adaptable, budget-aware, and willing to make the most of their environment. It may suit students who want a European education but do not want the financial pressure of more expensive destinations. It may suit students who are open to cultural adjustment and are ready to build their profile step by step.","But Poland may not suit students who expect everything to be familiar:","The weather can be different.","The food habits may take time to adjust to.","The language environment may feel unfamiliar.","The student may need to be more independent.","The job market may require patience, networking, and local understanding.","Not every university or programme will offer the same quality or recognition.","This is why students must research carefully. They should not choose Poland only because it seems affordable. They should check the course structure, university recognition, city, internship possibilities, language expectations, visa rules, part-time work conditions, and post-study options. A smart destination still requires smart planning."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The course must match the country",paragraphs:["One mistake students often make is choosing a country first and then forcing a course into it. That is not the right approach.","For Poland, students should think carefully about whether their chosen field connects with the country’s strengths and city-level opportunities. Poland can be especially relevant for students interested in areas such as:","Computer science and IT","Data analytics","Engineering","Management","Finance","Logistics and supply chain","International business","Hospitality and tourism"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Healthcare-related fields",paragraphs:["Manufacturing and industrial systems"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Business services and operations",paragraphs:["But the course must be evaluated properly. A student should ask:","Is the curriculum practical?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Is the course taught in English?",paragraphs:["Does the university have industry links?","Is the city connected to my field?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Will this programme help me build skills employers actually value?",paragraphs:["The right question is not simply: “Can I get admission?” The better question is: “Will this admission take me in the right direction?”"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Parents are right to ask about ROI",paragraphs:["Many students feel frustrated when parents ask too many questions about studying abroad. But parents are not wrong.","For most Indian families, this decision is emotional and financial at the same time. It involves trust, savings, sometimes loans, and a belief that the student will use the opportunity seriously. So when parents ask, “Will this be worth it?” they are not being negative. They are asking the most important question.","Poland’s appeal is that it allows families to have a more balanced ROI conversation. The cost may be more manageable compared to several traditional destinations. The student can gain European exposure. The economy offers relevant industry environments. The experience can help build independence and international confidence.","But the outcome still depends on the student. A country can create opportunity. It cannot guarantee success. The student has to choose the right course, build skills, adapt to the environment, learn beyond the classroom, network, and take responsibility for their own growth.","Poland is not a backup. It can be a considered choice.","Many students treat lesser-discussed countries as backup options. That is unfair and often incorrect. A backup is something a student chooses because the main plan failed. A considered choice is something a student chooses because it actually fits.","Poland should be seen through the second lens. For some students, the US, UK, Canada, or Australia may still be the best option. For others, Poland may offer a stronger balance between cost, academic access, European exposure, and career relevance.","The smartest students are not always the ones choosing the most famous destination. They are the ones choosing with clarity — comparing countries honestly, understanding personal priorities, and asking whether the destination supports their real goals.","Why Poland now?","Poland is becoming relevant because the study abroad market itself is changing:","Traditional destinations are becoming more expensive.","Visa rules in many countries are being discussed more carefully.","Families are becoming more cautious.","Students are becoming more outcome-focused.","The pressure to justify the investment is much higher than before.","At the same time, Europe is becoming more attractive to students who want international exposure, multicultural learning, and practical education pathways. Poland sits at the centre of this shift. It offers affordability without completely removing ambition. It offers European exposure without always demanding the highest budgets. It offers growing cities, international companies, and a developing student ecosystem.","Not because it is suddenly fashionable. But because it fits the moment.","Final thought","Studying abroad is no longer about following the crowd. It is about choosing a destination that makes sense academically, financially, professionally, and personally.","For some students, that may still be the US, UK, Canada, or Australia. For others, it may be a practical European destination like Poland.","Poland is becoming a smart study destination because it offers the kind of balance many Indian families are looking for: European education, comparatively affordable costs, international exposure, growing career relevance, and a wider professional ecosystem.","It is not the loudest option. It is not the easiest option. It is not for everyone. But for the right student, Poland can be a serious and sensible study abroad choice. And sometimes, that is exactly what a good study abroad decision should be — not the most glamorous, not the most popular, just the one that fits.","At One Window Overseas Education, we help students and families look beyond crowd movement and compare countries based on profile, budget, course fit, career goals, visa rules, and long-term value. Because studying abroad should not be about choosing the country everyone is talking about. It should be about choosing the destination that fits your future."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Thinking about studying in Europe but confused between Poland, Hungary, Germany, France, Luxembourg, or other destinations?",paragraphs:["At One Window Overseas Education, we help students shortlist countries and universities based on academic background, budget, career goals, and long-term ROI.","Book a free counselling session and find out whether Poland is the right fit for your study abroad journey."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"is-your-mba-actually-worth-it-the-roi-question-nobody-asks-8",title:"Is Your MBA Actually Worth It? The ROI Question Nobody Asks",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/is-your-mba-actually-worth-it-the-roi-question-nobody-asks/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/stop-assuming.jpeg",intro:"Every year, thousands of students spend months obsessing over one number: their target school’s ranking. They compare lists. They debate prestige. They build entire application strategies around the idea that if a school is ranked higher, it must automatically be the better decision. Almost none of them ask the question that actually matters:",sections:[{heading:"Which MBA gets me specifically to where I want to go and how fast does it pay back?",paragraphs:["That is the question that should shape the entire decision.","Because an MBA is not just an academic purchase. It is a career investment. And like any serious investment, it should be judged on return, not just reputation.","This matters even more now because MBA applicants are making decisions in a tougher environment. Cost has become a bigger barrier, job-market uncertainty is more visible, and students are thinking much harder about whether the degree will actually change their trajectory. GMAC’s 2026 Prospective Students Survey found that 46% of candidates globally cited cost as a top barrier to graduate management education, while 20% cited uncertainty about job prospects after graduation. That tells you something important: applicants are no longer asking only, “Can I get in?” They are also asking, “Will this make financial and professional sense?”","That is exactly the right shift.","A good MBA can absolutely be worth it. It can accelerate your salary, expand your network, make a difficult career pivot possible, and give you access to employers who would otherwise be hard to reach.","But “worth it” depends on where you are starting, where you want to end up, how much you spend, how long you step out of the workforce, and whether the school actually has a track record of taking people like you to the places you want to go.","Here is a simple framework to find that out."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Step 1: Define Your “After” With Precision",paragraphs:["Before calculating return, you need to know what you are returning to.","This is where a lot of applicants go wrong. They use broad, impressive-sounding goals that are too vague to evaluate properly.","They say:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"“I want leadership exposure.”",paragraphs:["“I want international opportunities.”","“I want a better career.”"],bullets:[]},{heading:"“I want to move into business.”",paragraphs:["None of that is useless. But none of it is precise enough to justify spending tens of thousands, or in some cases hundreds of thousands, on an MBA.","A stronger approach is to define your “after” in concrete terms:","Industry:","Consulting / Fintech / Finance / Tech / Luxury / Healthcare / Energy","Function:","Strategy / Product / Investment / Operations / Marketing / General Management","Geography:","Europe / North America / Southeast Asia / Middle East / India","Seniority in 5 years:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Manager / Director-track / Founder / Principal / Functional Leader",paragraphs:["That level of clarity changes everything.","Because ROI is never abstract. It is personal.","A student trying to move from engineering into consulting in Germany is solving a very different problem from someone trying to move from marketing into product roles in the Netherlands. A candidate hoping to break into private equity has a different ROI lens from someone who wants to return to a family business with better strategic and financial training.","If you do not define the destination clearly, almost any MBA can look attractive. Once you define it, some programs immediately stop making sense.","Example","Take two applicants:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Applicant A says: “I want an MBA because I feel stuck and want growth.”",paragraphs:["Applicant B says: “I want to move from operations into strategy consulting, preferably in Western Europe, and I want to be on a manager-track path within five years.”","Applicant B is already in a much stronger decision-making position.","Why? Because now they can ask useful questions:","Which schools place into consulting in Europe?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Which firms recruit there?",paragraphs:["How many alumni are in those offices?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What is the likely salary jump?",paragraphs:["How long would it take to recover the investment?","That is how ROI thinking starts.","Counterpoint","Not everyone has total clarity at the beginning, and that is fine. Some people genuinely use the MBA to explore. But there is a difference between being open-minded and being undefined.","If you still cannot describe the role, geography, and industry you are targeting, you need to ask a harder question:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Do I need an MBA right now, or do I need more clarity first?",paragraphs:["In some cases, a specialized Master’s may serve you better than an MBA at this stage, especially if your next step depends more on technical depth than on general management signaling."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Step 2: Map the School’s Alumni to Your Target",paragraphs:["This is the most underused tool available to every applicant and it takes about three hours on LinkedIn.","Search alumni from your target school who graduated in the last five years. Filter by your target:","industry","company","function","country","city","Then count what you find.","Not in a casual, “I saw a few people there” way. Count properly."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How many alumni from that school are actually working where you want to work?",paragraphs:["That is one of the clearest indicators of whether the MBA is likely to deliver on your goal.","Why this matters","A school ranked #8 globally with 12 alumni in your target sector and city may be worth less to you than a school ranked #22 with 400 alumni in exactly the firms, offices, and roles you care about.","That does not mean rankings do not matter. They do. Rankings affect employer perception, applicant quality, and brand value.","But rankings are broad signals. Your career is specific.","The alumni network in your specific target is your real asset. Not the ranking.","That network shapes:","who responds to your cold messages"],bullets:[]},{heading:"what kinds of internships and interviews are realistically accessible",paragraphs:["whether the school has real relevance in your target geography"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Student decision scenario",paragraphs:["Imagine you want to work in European fintech. You compare two schools: one has stronger global prestige, the other has visibly stronger alumni density in Amsterdam, Berlin, Paris, Luxembourg, and London fintech roles.","If your goal is highly targeted and Europe-specific, the second school may produce stronger ROI even if it is less glamorous in a general ranking table.","That is not a downgrade. That is precision.","Counterpoint","There is one important caution here: alumni mapping is useful, but it is not the same as guaranteed placement.","A school may have strong alumni presence in a sector because it attracts people who were already good candidates for that sector. In finance especially, prior profile matters a lot. So do not read the data too casually.","Use alumni mapping as evidence of relevance, not proof of inevitability.","A better way to interpret it is:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Is the network deep enough to create real access?",paragraphs:["That is the level of reading you want."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Step 3: Calculate Your Full Investment",paragraphs:["Tuition is the number schools advertise. It is not the number that matters most.","The real number is:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Real Investment = Tuition + Living Costs + Lost Earnings",paragraphs:["That third number is the one many students underestimate.","If you leave a job to study full-time, the salary you are not earning is part of the cost. So are foregone bonuses, promotion cycles, and the momentum you would otherwise carry in the workforce.","This is why program duration matters so much.","Illustrative example","2-Year US MBA","Tuition: $120,000","Living costs: $60,000","Lost earnings: $48,000","Total: $228,000","1-Year European MBA","Tuition: €45,000","Living costs: €18,000","Lost earnings: €20,000","Total: €83,000","These are illustrative numbers, not universal benchmarks. Real totals vary by school, scholarship, city, housing situation, and pre-MBA salary.","But the framework is correct. The longer the program, the more the economics shift.","That is why a one-year MBA can sometimes outperform a two-year MBA on ROI, especially if the target post-MBA market is similar."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What applicants often miss",paragraphs:["Many students fixate on the prestige of a program and ignore how much extra cost they are absorbing for that prestige.","Sometimes that extra cost is worth it. Sometimes it buys access to a different recruiter set, a different geography, or a far stronger long-term network.","But sometimes it does not. Sometimes it is just a more expensive route to a very similar destination.","That is what you need to test."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Student decision scenario",paragraphs:["Suppose two applicants want to pivot into consulting in Europe. One chooses a longer, more expensive MBA outside Europe. The other chooses a one-year European MBA with a strong local employer network.","If both end up in comparable consulting salary bands in Europe, the second candidate may recover the investment much faster, take on less debt, and preserve more financial flexibility in the first few years after graduation.","That does not mean the cheaper option is automatically better. The more expensive one may offer broader optionality or stronger global brand value. But the comparison needs to be explicit, not assumed."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Step 4: Calculate Your Payback Period",paragraphs:["Once you know the total cost, the next step is simple:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Payback Period = Total Investment ÷ Annual Salary Increase",paragraphs:["This is not the only way to measure ROI, but it is one of the clearest.","Example","A student currently earning €30,000 completes a one-year MBA and enters consulting at €75,000.","Annual salary increase: €45,000","Total investment: €83,000","Payback period: under 2 years","That is strong ROI.","As a working rule:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"3 to 5 years may still be reasonable, especially for a serious pivot",paragraphs:["Beyond 5 to 6 years deserves a much harder look","Why this matters","Payback period forces discipline. It moves the conversation away from “this school feels prestigious” and toward “this decision has a recoverable timeline.”","If the payback period is long, the MBA may still be worth it. But now you need a better reason:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Is it opening a geography or sector that changes your career path materially?",paragraphs:["If the answer is no, you may be overpaying for the signal."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Counterpoint: payback is not everything",paragraphs:["A short payback period is attractive, but it is not the whole story.","Some students choose an MBA because it changes the shape of their career, not just the first-year salary. For example:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"someone moving into a founder or investor ecosystem",paragraphs:["In those cases, the ROI may compound over time rather than showing up fully in year one.","Still, if the payback period is weak and the strategic upside is also unclear, the decision deserves more skepticism."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Step 5: Don’t Ignore the Non-Financial Return",paragraphs:["Three factors do not always show up neatly in salary comparisons, but they can significantly shift your ROI.","For many students, the MBA is not mainly about a salary increase. It is about changing lanes faster than they could on their own.","An MBA can be the fastest route:","from engineering into consulting"],bullets:["Career pivot access"]},{heading:"from a local market into a global one",paragraphs:["If it compresses a transition that would otherwise take three to five years, that has real value.","A candidate in a back-end operations role may not be badly paid, but may have very limited access to strategy-track roles. If the MBA helps them break into a consulting or general management path with far stronger long-term growth, the early ROI may undersell the true return.","For international students, this can materially affect ROI. As of May 19, 2026:","Germany generally allows graduates to stay for up to 18 months to look for skilled employment after completing a German degree.","The Netherlands offers an orientation year residence permit valid for 1 year.","France generally offers eligible graduates a 1-year post-study job-search or business-creation route.","These rules matter because time to secure the right job is part of the return. A degree is more valuable when it gives you a realistic runway to convert education into employment. A shorter or more restrictive route can change the risk profile significantly.","This is also one place where students should be careful about recycled advice online. Visa frameworks change. Country-specific rules should always be checked close to the application stage.","A strong alumni network keeps paying dividends long after the first job. It matters when:","you want to change companies","you want to change countries","you are fundraising","you are hiring"],bullets:["Post-study visa access","Network compounding"]},{heading:"you need fast, trusted industry insight",paragraphs:["This is why one of the best questions you can ask alumni is:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How often do you actually use your school’s network professionally?",paragraphs:["Not whether they “liked the community.” Not whether they “met great people.” Whether the network actually helped in practical career terms. That is the answer that matters."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Which MBA Fits Your Career Line?",paragraphs:["Different goals require different kinds of schools and different evaluation criteria.","Consulting","If you are targeting consulting, prioritize:","schools with visible recruiter access"],bullets:[]},{heading:"strong case interview preparation culture",paragraphs:["Programs such as HEC Paris, INSEAD, and London Business School are regularly part of this conversation in Europe because of their employer visibility and network strength.","Finance / PE","If you are targeting finance or private equity, prioritize:","alumni in relevant funds and financial hubs","location strength","employer access"],bullets:[]},{heading:"your own pre-MBA profile fit",paragraphs:["Finance ROI is often highly dependent on prior experience, not just the school.","Tech / Product","If you want tech or product roles, look beyond general prestige and ask:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"which geographies actually make sense for my post-MBA target?",paragraphs:["does the school have product-focused alumni I can map?","Entrepreneurship","If you want to build something, employer salary may matter less than:","founder network","incubator support","investor access","quality of peers"],bullets:[]},{heading:"ecosystem relevance",paragraphs:["Again, this is why ROI must be personal."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Before You Apply: A Quick Checklist",paragraphs:["Before you commit to an MBA, ask yourself:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Verified current post-study work options in your target country",paragraphs:["If you have not done these things yet, you are still looking at MBAs. You are not really evaluating them.","Final Thought","The students who get the best return from business school are not always the ones who got into the highest-ranked program.","They are the ones who chose with precision.","They asked where they wanted to go.","They checked which school actually led there.","They counted the full cost.","They measured the payback.","They talked to alumni.","And they treated the MBA as a strategic investment, not a prestige purchase.","That is the ROI question. Ask it early. Run the numbers honestly. Talk to the alumni. Then decide.","FAQ"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Is an MBA worth it financially?",paragraphs:["An MBA can be worth it financially if the degree leads to a meaningful salary increase, stronger long-term career access, or a faster career pivot that justifies the total cost."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How do you calculate MBA ROI?",paragraphs:["A practical MBA ROI formula is total investment divided by annual salary increase. Total investment should include tuition, living costs, and lost earnings while studying."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What is a good MBA payback period?",paragraphs:["A payback period under three years is often considered strong, while a payback period above five to six years usually deserves closer scrutiny."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Does ranking matter when choosing an MBA?",paragraphs:["Ranking matters, but it should not be the only factor. Alumni outcomes, employer access, network strength, and target geography often matter more for actual ROI."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Is a one-year MBA better for ROI?",paragraphs:["A one-year MBA can produce better ROI when it reduces tuition costs, living costs, and lost earnings while still delivering strong career outcomes in the target market."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What matters more: MBA prestige or MBA outcomes?",paragraphs:["For ROI, outcomes matter more. A school’s value depends on whether it helps you reach your target role, industry, and geography at a justifiable cost."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"study-in-europe-for-indian-students-9",title:"Study in Europe for Indian Students",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/study-in-europe-for-indian-students/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/study-in-europe-faqs.jpeg",intro:"Europe is one of the most popular study abroad destinations for Indian students because it offers high-quality education, globally recognised degrees, international exposure, and strong career opportunities. Many European countries have universities that offer English-taught programmes across fields such as business, engineering, healthcare, technology, hospitality, data science, finance, and management. Compared to countries like the USA, Canada, and Australia, several European destinations also offer more affordable tuition fees and living costs. Students can benefit from practical learning, multicultural exposure, part-time work opportunities, and post-study work options depending on the country. For Indian students who want quality education, global career exposure, and a broader international lifestyle, Europe can be a smart study abroad choice.",sections:[{heading:"Study in Europe for Indian Students",paragraphs:["Students should ideally start planning their study abroad journey at least 8 to 12 months before the intended intake. This gives enough time for university shortlisting, course selection, document preparation, application submission, financial planning, visa processing, and accommodation arrangements.","Students can apply for both bachelor’s and master’s programmes in Europe. However, many Indian students prefer pursuing a master’s abroad because postgraduate programmes offer more specialised course options, stronger career pathways, and better industry exposure.","Planning early also improves your chances of applying to better universities, meeting scholarship deadlines, and avoiding last-minute visa stress.","A study abroad consultant helps students make informed decisions at every stage of the process. This includes country selection, university shortlisting, course guidance, profile evaluation, application support, documentation, visa filing, and interview preparation.","A good consultant does not simply push one university or country. Instead, they help students choose options based on academic profile, budget, career goals, visa requirements, and long-term opportunities.","For Indian students applying to Europe, a consultant can be especially helpful because each country has different admission requirements, visa rules, financial documentation, language criteria, and timelines.","Not always. Many European universities offer programmes that are fully taught in English, especially for international students. Students can choose English-taught bachelor’s, master’s, and MBA programmes across several European countries.","However, learning the basic local language is highly recommended. Even if your classes are in English, knowing the local language can help with daily life, part-time jobs, internships, networking, public transport, shopping, and social interaction.","For long-term career opportunities, language skills can become a major advantage, especially in countries where local employers prefer candidates who can communicate with customers, clients, or teams in the native language.","The cost of studying in Europe depends on the country, university, course, city, and lifestyle. Some European countries offer highly affordable tuition fees, while others may have higher fees for specialised programmes such as management, business, medicine, finance, or technology.","In affordable European destinations, tuition fees may start from a few lakhs per year. In countries such as France, Ireland, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, or the UK, fees can be higher depending on the programme and institution.","Apart from tuition fees, students should also budget for accommodation, food, transport, health insurance, visa expenses, residence permits, and personal expenses. A realistic financial plan should include both tuition and living costs.","The student visa processing time for Europe depends on the country, embassy, intake season, application volume, and strength of the student’s documentation. In many cases, visa processing may take anywhere from 15 to 90 days.","Some applications may be processed faster if the documentation is complete and the student profile is strong. However, students should not assume that visas will be approved within a few days. It is always safer to apply early and keep enough time for embassy appointments, biometrics, interviews, and additional document requests.","Yes, Indian students can apply for several scholarships to study in Europe. Scholarships may be offered by European governments, universities, private foundations, international organisations, and country-specific funding bodies.","Some scholarships cover partial tuition fees, while others may provide full funding, living support, travel allowance, or monthly stipends. Eligibility depends on academic merit, course level, country, university, financial need, leadership potential, and application quality.","Students should check scholarship deadlines early because many scholarship applications close before regular admission deadlines.","Post-study work and permanent residency rules vary from country to country in Europe. Many European countries allow international students to stay back after graduation for a limited period to search for jobs or start professional work.","The duration of post-study stay may range from a few months to two years depending on the country and qualification. After securing a job, students may be able to convert their status to a work permit or residence permit.","Permanent residency usually depends on factors such as legal stay duration, full-time employment, tax payments, language skills, clean legal record, and country-specific immigration rules. Students should understand that PR is not automatic after studying in Europe. It requires long-term planning and compliance with local laws.","Yes, it is possible to study in Europe on a comparatively lower budget if students choose the right country, university, course, and city. Countries such as Hungary, Latvia, Malta, Italy, Greece, Poland, and some other European destinations may offer affordable study options for Indian students.","However, “low budget” should not mean poor planning. Students must calculate tuition fees, living expenses, visa costs, health insurance, accommodation, travel, and emergency funds.","Europe can be more affordable than the USA, Canada, or Australia in many cases, but costs still vary widely between countries and cities.","Indian students can explore several well-known scholarships for studying in Europe, including:","Erasmus Mundus Joint Master’s Scholarships"],bullets:["Why should Indian students choose Europe for studying abroad?","When should I start planning to study abroad in Europe?","Why do I need a study abroad or student visa consultant?","Do I need to learn the local language to study in Europe?","How much does it cost to study in Europe for Indian students?","How long does the Europe student visa process take?","Are scholarships available in Europe for Indian students?","How can Indian students get PR or a work permit in Europe after studies?","Is it possible to study in Europe on a low budget?","What are the best scholarships in Europe for Indian students?"]},{heading:"DAAD Scholarships for Germany",paragraphs:["Eiffel Excellence Scholarship Programme in France"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Chevening Scholarships in the UK",paragraphs:["Swiss Government Excellence Scholarships","Italian Government Scholarships"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Country-specific government scholarships",paragraphs:["Apart from these, many universities offer their own tuition waivers, merit-based scholarships, early-bird discounts, and financial support options. Students should check eligibility and deadlines before applying.","Admission requirements for European universities vary depending on the country, university, course, and level of study. However, common requirements usually include:","Academic transcripts","Passport","Statement of Purpose","Letters of Recommendation","CV or resume"],bullets:["What are the admission requirements to study in Europe?"]},{heading:"Portfolio for design or creative courses, if required",paragraphs:["Work experience for some MBA or management programmes"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Financial documents in some cases",paragraphs:["For many universities, a minimum academic score may be required. Some competitive universities and business schools may expect stronger academic performance, relevant experience, and a well-written application.","The Europe student visa process depends on the destination country. In most cases, students must first secure admission from a recognised university or institution. After receiving the admission letter, they can begin the visa application process.","The process usually includes:"],bullets:["How do I apply for a Europe student visa?"]},{heading:"Filling the visa application form",paragraphs:["Booking an appointment through the embassy, consulate, or visa application centre","Submitting required documents"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Providing biometrics",paragraphs:["Attending a visa interview, if required"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Waiting for visa decision",paragraphs:["Students should always follow the official checklist of the country they are applying to. Incomplete documents can delay or negatively affect the visa outcome.","Student visa requirements vary across European countries, but common documents usually include:","Valid passport","University admission letter","Academic documents","Proof of funds","Proof of accommodation","Health insurance","Visa application form"],bullets:["What are the common student visa requirements for Europe?"]},{heading:"Statement of Purpose or motivation letter",paragraphs:["Language proficiency proof, if required","Travel itinerary, if requested"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Visa fee payment receipt",paragraphs:["Some countries may ask for additional documents such as police clearance, medical certificates, blocked account proof, sponsorship letters, or an interview.","VFS Global is a visa application service provider that manages visa application submissions for several countries. In many cases, students submit their documents, biometrics, and visa applications through a VFS centre instead of directly visiting the embassy.","VFS does not make visa decisions. The final decision is taken by the embassy, consulate, or immigration authority of the destination country.","For students, VFS can make the submission process more structured by providing appointment booking, document collection, biometric services, and application tracking.","A Europe education consultant can help students with complete study abroad planning. This includes choosing the right country, selecting suitable universities, identifying relevant courses, preparing applications, reviewing documents, and guiding students through the visa process.","A consultant can also help students understand budget planning, scholarship options, language requirements, post-study work rules, and country-specific admission timelines.","For students who are unsure about where to begin, a consultant can simplify the process and reduce avoidable mistakes.","One Window Overseas Education helps Indian students plan their study abroad journey with a profile-based approach. Instead of suggesting countries only based on popularity, the team helps students compare options based on academic background, budget, career goals, course relevance, visa requirements, and long-term opportunities.","Students can get support with university shortlisting, course selection, application documentation, visa guidance, interview preparation, and pre-departure planning.","The goal is to help students make smarter study abroad decisions, not just faster ones.","One Window Overseas Education supports students from the initial counselling stage to the final stages of their study abroad journey. This may include profile evaluation, course and university shortlisting, application assistance, documentation guidance, visa filing support, mock interview preparation, and pre-departure guidance.","The team also helps students understand country-specific requirements, financial planning, accommodation basics, and important next steps before travelling abroad.","The duration of a study abroad programme depends on the country, course, and level of study. Most master’s programmes in Europe take 1 to 2 years to complete. Bachelor’s programmes usually take 3 to 4 years, depending on the country and academic structure.","MBA programmes may take 1 to 2 years, while diploma, foundation, language, and pathway programmes may have shorter durations.","Students should always check the exact course duration before applying.","Yes, many countries allow international students to work part-time while studying. The number of permitted working hours depends on the country and visa rules.","Part-time work can help students gain exposure, improve communication skills, understand local work culture, and manage some living expenses. However, students should not depend entirely on part-time income to fund their education.","Work rights, wage levels, and post-study work options vary from country to country, so students should check the official rules before applying.","Some European countries allow international students to bring a spouse or dependent family member, but the rules vary by country, course level, visa type, and financial capacity.","Countries such as the UK, Finland, Denmark, and the Netherlands may have dependent or spouse visa options under specific conditions. Other countries may have different rules or restrictions.","Students planning to apply with a spouse should check financial requirements, accommodation proof, visa rules, dependent work rights, and timelines before applying.","The main benefits of studying in Europe include:","Globally recognised degrees"],bullets:["What is VFS in the student visa process?","How can a Europe education consultant help me?","Why choose One Window Overseas Education for Europe studies?","How does One Window Overseas Education support students during the study abroad process?","How long do study abroad programmes usually take?","Can Indian students work while studying abroad?","Can I apply for a study visa with my spouse?","What are the main benefits of studying in Europe?"]},{heading:"English-taught programmes",paragraphs:["Affordable study options in several countries","Multicultural exposure"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Post-study work options in many countries",paragraphs:["Access to strong industries and international companies"],bullets:[]},{heading:"High-quality lifestyle and student experience",paragraphs:["Europe offers a wide range of choices for students with different budgets, academic profiles, and career goals.","Europe is a strong destination for master’s programmes because it offers specialised courses, research opportunities, practical learning, international classrooms, and strong industry exposure.","Many European master’s programmes are designed to connect academic learning with employability. Students can choose from fields such as business, engineering, data science, finance, hospitality, public policy, sustainability, healthcare, artificial intelligence, cybersecurity, and international management.","For Indian students, a master’s in Europe can be a valuable pathway to global education, career development, and international exposure.","France is one of Europe’s leading destinations for business and management education. It is home to several well-known business schools and offers programmes in management, luxury brand management, international business, finance, marketing, hospitality, and entrepreneurship.","France also has strong connections with global companies, especially in sectors such as luxury, fashion, finance, consulting, technology, hospitality, and international trade.","For students interested in management careers, France can offer a strong combination of academic quality, industry exposure, and European business culture.","Students can contact One Window Overseas Education through the official website, enquiry form, email, phone number, or social media channels. The counselling team can help with Europe study options, university shortlisting, course selection, visa guidance, documentation, and application support.","Students can also book a counselling session to understand which European country fits their profile, budget, and career goals.","One Window Overseas Education may support students with pre-departure and initial settlement guidance, depending on the destination and service plan. This can include guidance related to accommodation, travel preparation, airport arrival basics, local documentation, student essentials, and initial adjustment support.","The goal is to help students feel more prepared before and after they travel abroad.","Admission processing time depends on the university, country, course, intake, and application volume. In general, students may receive an admission decision within 15 to 90 days.","Some universities may respond faster, while competitive programmes or public universities may take longer. Students should apply early to avoid missing visa deadlines and scholarship opportunities.","A complete and well-prepared application can reduce delays.","Yes, a study gap may be accepted for Europe student visa applications, depending on the country, university, course, and reason for the gap. Students should be able to explain the gap clearly with supporting documents.","Work experience, internships, exam preparation, family reasons, health reasons, or skill development may be acceptable if properly documented.","A study gap does not automatically lead to rejection, but students must show academic seriousness, financial readiness, and a clear reason for choosing the course and country."],bullets:["Why study a master’s programme in Europe?","Why study management programmes in France?","How can I contact One Window Overseas Education for Europe study guidance?","What support does One Window Overseas Education provide after students reach Europe?","How long does it take to get admission to a European university?","Is a study gap accepted for Europe student visa?"]}]},{slug:"luxembourg-for-master-s-mba-students-why-smart-international-students-are-looking-beyond-traditional-study-destinations-10",title:"Luxembourg for Master’s & MBA Students: Why Smart International Students Are Looking Beyond Traditional Study Destinations",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/luxembourg-for-masters-mba-students-why-smart-international-students-are-looking-beyond-traditional-study-destinations/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/luxembourg-masters-mba.jpeg",intro:"Everyone talks about studying in Europe. But very few students stop to ask a sharper question:",sections:[{heading:"Where in Europe are the industries of the future actually growing?",paragraphs:["That question matters more today than ever before.","For years, international students followed a familiar study abroad map. The UK for business. Germany for engineering. France for management. The Netherlands for international education. These destinations still matter, and they continue to offer strong academic and career value.","But the world of work is changing faster than traditional study destinations are adapting to it.","Today, companies are no longer hiring only finance graduates, only technology graduates, or only management graduates. They are looking for people who can move across disciplines.","A finance student who understands AI.","A business graduate who can interpret data.","A tech professional who understands strategy, compliance, and regulation.","A manager who can work with digital tools, sustainability goals, and cross-border teams.","This shift is exactly why Luxembourg deserves more attention from Master’s and MBA students.","Not because it is “trending.” But because it sits at the intersection of some of Europe’s most future-facing sectors: global finance, FinTech, sustainable investment, digital transformation, cybersecurity, data, AI, business services, EU-linked institutions, and cross-border professional networks.","For students planning a Master’s or MBA abroad, the bigger question is no longer:","“Which country is most famous?”","It is:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"“Which place prepares me for the next 10 years of work?”",paragraphs:["That is a very different conversation. And Luxembourg belongs in that conversation."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Shift Students Are Beginning to Notice",paragraphs:["A Master’s degree or MBA is no longer just about getting another qualification. It is increasingly about positioning.","Where are you studying?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Will your degree connect with the direction in which the world is moving?",paragraphs:["Five or ten years ago, many students could still build careers in silos. Finance graduates stayed in finance. Technology graduates stayed in technology. Business graduates moved into management. Cybersecurity was treated as a technical back-end function. Sustainability was often seen as a reporting requirement, not a business priority.","That model is fading.","Today, employers across Europe are looking for hybrid talent. Business graduates need analytical thinking. Finance professionals need technology exposure. Managers need to understand AI, automation, and digital operations. Consultants need to work with data. Founders need to understand regulation, funding, sustainability, and international markets.","The future belongs to professionals who can move between industries, not remain trapped inside one.","This is why destination choice matters. A student is not only choosing a country for education. They are choosing the environment that will shape their thinking, exposure, network, and career direction."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why Luxembourg Is Becoming Relevant for Master’s and MBA Students",paragraphs:["Luxembourg has long been known as one of Europe’s leading financial centres. But reducing Luxembourg only to “banking” misses the bigger picture.","Today, the country has developed into a highly international business and innovation ecosystem connected to finance, investment funds, FinTech, sustainable finance, cybersecurity, digital transformation, data, AI, startups, and European policy-linked work.","Luxembourg’s Ministry of the Economy describes the country as a hub for startups and scale-ups looking to grow in Europe, supported by its central European location and multilingual culture. It also notes that Luxembourg’s startup ecosystem now includes around 700 startups, with public-sector support focused on innovation, entrepreneurship, scale-up growth, and attracting international entrepreneurs and investors.","For Master’s and MBA students, this matters because education is becoming ecosystem-driven. A classroom can teach frameworks. But an ecosystem teaches relevance.","Luxembourg’s strength is not just that it has strong sectors. Its strength is that these sectors overlap.","Finance is becoming digital.","Technology is becoming regulated.","Business is becoming data-driven.","Sustainability is becoming financial.","Cybersecurity is becoming a boardroom issue.","AI is becoming part of everyday management decisions.","That overlap is where many future careers will be built."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Luxembourg Reflects Where Europe’s Future Industries Are Heading",paragraphs:["One reason Luxembourg is interesting is that it reflects the direction in which Europe itself is moving: smaller, smarter, highly international, more regulated, and more innovation-led.","The country is not trying to compete by being the largest destination. It competes by being connected, specialised, and strategically positioned.","Luxembourg is closely associated with finance and investment, but it is also becoming increasingly relevant in newer fields such as FinTech, sustainable finance, digital transformation, cybersecurity, data strategy, and startup development.","Luxembourg’s national digital direction is also worth noting. The government’s data strategy describes data as a major driver of innovation, economic growth, public services, and digital transformation. It also identifies data, artificial intelligence, and quantum technologies as strategic areas within Luxembourg’s broader digital sovereignty ambitions.","For students, this is important because future-facing careers rarely grow in isolation.","AI needs regulation.","Data needs governance.","Finance needs technology.","Cybersecurity needs business understanding.","Sustainability needs investment structures.","Management needs digital fluency.","This is the kind of environment where a Master’s or MBA student can begin to see how different disciplines connect."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Rise of New-Age Master’s Programmes",paragraphs:["Across the world, students are moving away from purely traditional degrees and toward programmes that combine business, technology, analytics, finance, sustainability, and innovation. This is not just a university trend. It is a workforce trend.","Students want degrees that help them answer questions like:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How does finance work in a digital economy?",paragraphs:["What does cybersecurity mean for companies?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How do managers make decisions using data?",paragraphs:["Luxembourg’s ecosystem is especially relevant to students exploring these areas."],bullets:[]},{heading:"FinTech and Digital Banking",paragraphs:["Modern finance is no longer limited to traditional banking. The financial sector now involves digital payments, blockchain infrastructure, compliance technology, AI-powered banking systems, digital asset regulation, cybersecurity, and cross-border financial services.","Luxembourg has built a visible FinTech ecosystem through platforms such as the Luxembourg House of Financial Technology, which connects FinTech firms, institutions, and innovators. The LHoFT reports that from 2017 to 2025, its community hosted over 200 FinTech firms, included FinTechs from 30+ countries, and supported companies that collectively raised over €1 billion in five years.","For students interested in finance, technology, or business strategy, this kind of ecosystem is valuable because it shows where financial careers are moving. Finance is no longer only about spreadsheets and markets. It is also about platforms, data, security, regulation, and innovation."],bullets:[]},{heading:"AI, Data Analytics and Business Intelligence",paragraphs:["AI is no longer reserved for coders and engineers. Businesses now expect managers and analysts to work comfortably with predictive analytics, AI-assisted decision-making, data visualisation, automation tools, customer intelligence, and strategic analytics.","This is especially important for MBA and Master’s students. A future manager does not need to become a full-time software engineer. But they do need to understand how technology affects decision-making, operations, finance, marketing, risk, and strategy.","Luxembourg’s government has also placed data and AI within its digital development priorities, which signals that the country sees these areas as part of its future economic direction. For students, this means Luxembourg is not only a place to study business in the traditional sense. It is a place where business increasingly connects with data, AI, digital transformation, and innovation policy."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Cybersecurity and Digital Risk",paragraphs:["As companies digitise their operations, cybersecurity has become a leadership-level issue. It is no longer only an IT department concern. Companies now need people who understand risk management, digital governance, data protection, cyber compliance, enterprise security strategy, and the business impact of digital threats.","Luxembourg’s Ministry of the Economy identifies cybersecurity as part of its work in information technologies and describes cybersecurity as an important factor in the country’s economic attractiveness and digital trust.","This creates an important opportunity for international students. Cybersecurity is not only for students with a purely technical background. It is also becoming relevant for business students, MBA candidates, risk professionals, finance graduates, legal professionals, and consultants. In a digital economy, every serious organisation needs people who can understand risk."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Sustainable Finance and ESG",paragraphs:["Sustainability is no longer just a moral conversation. It is now deeply connected to finance, regulation, investment, reporting, and business strategy.","Europe continues to lead many conversations around ESG reporting, green investment, responsible finance, and sustainability-linked regulation. Luxembourg plays a visible role in this ecosystem. Luxembourg for Finance describes the country as a leading international platform for sustainable finance, supporting responsible investment funds, blended finance, green bond listings, ESG fund labelling, and other sustainable finance activities.","For Master’s and MBA students, this is a serious signal. Students interested in finance, sustainability, policy, consulting, investment, and international business can no longer treat ESG as a side topic. It is increasingly part of mainstream business decision-making. A student who understands both finance and sustainability may have a stronger profile than one who studies either area in isolation."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why This Matters Especially for MBA Students",paragraphs:["MBA students today are entering a very different job market from the one that existed a decade ago. Traditional post-MBA pathways still exist: consulting, finance, operations, leadership, strategy, entrepreneurship, and general management. But these pathways now demand a stronger understanding of technology and transformation.","Companies increasingly expect future managers to understand:","AI-driven business models","Digital operations","Innovation systems","Cross-border strategy","Data-led decision-making","Regulatory awareness","Sustainable growth","Cyber risk","Startup thinking","For MBA candidates with backgrounds in engineering, finance, consulting, entrepreneurship, IT, operations, or family business, Luxembourg can be an interesting destination because its economy connects many of these areas. The ecosystem is more integrated. And that is useful for MBA students who want their degree to be more than a classroom experience."],bullets:[]},{heading:"A Small Country with a Highly International Ecosystem",paragraphs:["One of Luxembourg’s biggest strengths is not size. It is connectivity. The country is compact, but its professional ecosystem is deeply international. It brings together government institutions, corporate offices, financial organisations, technology companies, startup networks, legal and policy institutions, and professionals from many nationalities within a relatively small space.","For students, this can be a real advantage. In larger destinations, it is easy to feel lost. Students may be one among thousands, competing in highly crowded markets with limited visibility.","Luxembourg offers a different possibility. Because the ecosystem is compact, proactive students may find more direct access to events, institutions, professionals, networking platforms, and industry conversations.","This does not mean opportunities are automatic. In fact, Luxembourg can be highly competitive. Its expat-driven environment means students must bring strong skills, communication ability, adaptability, and the confidence to stand out. But for students who are prepared, the compactness of the country can become an advantage. It allows them to become visible. And in today’s job market, visibility matters."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Cost Question: Expensive, But Not One-Dimensional",paragraphs:["Luxembourg is often seen as expensive. That is fair. Accommodation, food, lifestyle, insurance, transport, and daily expenses can be higher than in many other European countries. Students and families should not treat Luxembourg as a cheap destination.","But the cost conversation should not stop there. Luxembourg operates in a high-income economy. The point is not to say that every student will automatically earn a high salary after graduation. That would be unrealistic. The point is that Luxembourg’s economy functions at a high-value professional level, which is why it attracts international talent, companies, investors, and institutions.","So the smarter ROI question is not:","“Is Luxembourg expensive?”","The better question is:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"“Can I build the kind of profile, skills, network, and adaptability needed to compete in Luxembourg’s economy?”",paragraphs:["That is a more honest way to look at value. Luxembourg is not cheap. It is not easy. It is not automatic. But for the right student, it can be strategically valuable."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Luxembourg Is Not for Every Student",paragraphs:["This is important. Luxembourg should not be sold as a shortcut. It is not the right destination for students who only want a familiar English-speaking environment, a large Indian student community, or an easy post-study promise.","It is better suited for students who are:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Open to multilingual and multicultural environments",paragraphs:["Interested in business, finance, technology, data, sustainability, policy, or innovation","Willing to network actively","Comfortable with competition"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Ready to differentiate themselves",paragraphs:["In Luxembourg, a degree alone may not be enough. Students need skills, communication, initiative, adaptability, and a strong reason for choosing the country. That is exactly why Luxembourg is interesting. It rewards students who are not just looking for a destination, but for an ecosystem."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The Bigger Shift in Study Abroad Decisions",paragraphs:["The most important shift in international education is this: students are no longer choosing countries only because they are popular. They are choosing environments that feel future-ready.","That means asking better questions:","Which economies are adapting fastest?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Which destination helps me prepare for the next 10 years of work?",paragraphs:["For Master’s and MBA students, this matters deeply. A postgraduate degree is a major investment. Students are not only investing money. They are investing time, energy, career direction, and family trust. So the destination must make sense beyond the brochure.","Luxembourg reflects where Europe itself is heading: smaller, smarter, international, multilingual, regulated, finance-led, technology-enabled, and innovation-focused.","That is why smart international students are beginning to look beyond traditional study destinations. Not because the old destinations have lost value. But because the future of work is becoming more complex. And students need ecosystems that prepare them for that complexity.","For the right Master’s or MBA student, Luxembourg may offer exactly that. Not noise. Not hype. But relevance.","Final Thought","The best study abroad decision is not always the most popular one. It is the one that places you closer to the industries, networks, skills, and opportunities that match your future.","For students planning a Master’s or MBA abroad, Luxembourg deserves attention because it is not just another European country. It is a compact, international, high-value ecosystem where finance, technology, sustainability, data, policy, and innovation often meet.","And in a world where careers are becoming increasingly interdisciplinary, that intersection matters.","The future will not belong only to finance graduates, tech graduates, or management graduates. It will belong to professionals who can connect ideas across all three. That is why Luxembourg may be worth a serious look."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Not Sure Which Country Is Right for You?",paragraphs:["Confused about whether Luxembourg, Hungary, Germany, France, the UK, or another destination fits your profile? At One Window Overseas Education, we help students compare countries based on academic goals, career direction, budget, work opportunities, industry exposure, and long-term ROI.","Book a free country-shortlisting call and find out whether Luxembourg is the right fit for your Master’s or MBA journey."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"why-indian-students-should-look-beyond-canada-uk-and-australia-in-2026-is-luxembourg-the-smarter-study-abroad-bet-11",title:"Why Indian Students Should Look Beyond Canada, UK and Australia in 2026 — Is Luxembourg the Smarter Study Abroad Bet?",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/why-indian-students-should-look-beyond-canada-uk-and-australia-in-2026-is-luxembourg-the-smarter-study-abroad-bet/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/location-shapes-your-career-one-window.png",intro:"For most Indian families, the study abroad conversation begins in the same way. “Canada is good, right?” “What about UK?”",sections:[{heading:"“US is expensive, but worth it if the university is top-ranked.”",paragraphs:["And honestly, these questions are understandable. For years, Indian students have seen these countries as the default map of ambition. Canada meant affordability and immigration pathways.","The UK meant prestige and a shorter master’s degree. Australia meant lifestyle and post-study options. The US meant scale, STEM, and global recognition.","But 2026 is not 2016.","The study abroad world has changed. Visa rules are tighter. Costs are higher. Post-study work routes are being reviewed, reduced, or made more selective. Families are more cautious.","Students are more anxious. And the old idea of “go wherever everyone else is going” is no longer enough.","The smarter question today is not:","Which country is most popular?","It is:","Which country gives me the best mix of education quality, affordability, work exposure, safety, economic opportunity, and long-term career value?","That is where Luxembourg enters the conversation.","Not as a flashy replacement for Canada, the UK, or Australia. Not as a destination every student should blindly choose. But as one of the most underrated European options Indian students should seriously understand before finalising their study abroad plan."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The real problem: Indian students are still choosing countries emotionally, not strategically",paragraphs:["A lot of Indian students do not choose a country after deep research. They choose based on what they hear around them.","A cousin went to Canada.","A senior got into the UK.","A neighbour’s daughter moved to Australia.","An Instagram reel said Germany has free education.","A YouTube video said Ireland is the next big thing.","Slowly, a serious life decision starts becoming a crowd decision.","This is where many families go wrong. Studying abroad is commonly seen as a way to access prestigious universities, international exposure, and better-paying jobs. But as the research =shared by educationist R. N. Bhaskar points out, the real worth of foreign education is not just in rankings or popular university names. It depends on teaching quality, practical experience, total cost, and the economic condition of the country where the student plans to study.","That one idea changes everything in the conversation.","Because a famous country does not automatically mean a good outcome.","A high-ranked university does not automatically mean employability.","A foreign degree does not automatically mean confidence, skill, or return on investment.","And a popular destination does not automatically mean it is the right destination for  your  profile."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Situation 1: The student who chooses Canada because everyone is going there",paragraphs:["Imagine a student from Hyderabad or Vijayawada. Let’s call him Arjun.","He has a decent academic profile, a middle-class family, and a strong desire to move abroad.","His parents are supportive, but they are also stretching financially. Canada feels like the obvious choice because many people around him have gone there.","But when Arjun starts applying, he realises the Canada of 2026 is not the same Canada many seniors entered earlier.","Canada has been managing international student intake through caps and study permit allocations, and students now have to pay attention to rules like PAL/TAL requirements, programme  eligibility, and PGWP conditions. These are not small details; they directly affect whether a student can enter, study, work, and stay after graduation.","Now Arjun’s family faces a difficult question."],bullets:[]},{heading:"system may be strong, and the long-term pathway may be more realistic?",paragraphs:["This is exactly where families need a broader shortlist.","Not to reject Canada blindly. But to stop treating it as the only answer.","Situation 2: The student who wants UK prestige but","ignores the post-study runway","Now think of a student named Megha.","She wants a one-year master’s in the UK. Her family likes the idea because the course is shorter. The university brand sounds good. The total time away from India feels manageable.","But here is the issue: a one-year degree also means a very fast transition from classroom to job market.","The UK Graduate visa currently allows international students to stay after eligible studies, but the UK government has also moved toward changes in post-study timelines. For students, this means intake timing, job preparation, internships, networking, and course selection matter much more than before. The official UK Graduate visa page states that the Graduate visa gives permission to stay in the UK for at least 18 months after successfully completing an eligible course.","So Megha’s real question should not be, “Is UK good?”","It should be:","Can I build employability quickly enough?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Is my family comfortable with the financial risk if I do not get a role immediately?",paragraphs:["This is where many students need a more honest counselling conversation.","Prestige matters. But timing matters too.","Situation 3: The student who thinks a foreign degree","itself is enough","This is one of the biggest myths in Indian study abroad decisions.","A student thinks, “Once I get a foreign degree, things will work out.”","But that is not always true.","A degree is only one part of the outcome. What matters is what happens during the degree."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Did the family calculate total cost properly?",paragraphs:["Bhaskar’s point about teaching quality is especially important here. He argues that good teaching is what really builds confident and skilled students, not just certificates or university","names.","This matters because many Indian families still ask, “What is the university ranking?”","They should also ask:","Who teaches the course?","How practical is the learning?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What kind of economy will the student enter after graduation?",paragraphs:["That last question is where Luxembourg becomes interesting."],bullets:[]},{heading:"So, why Luxembourg?",paragraphs:["Luxembourg is not usually the first country Indian students think of. That is exactly why it deserves attention.","It is small. It is not as heavily marketed as Canada, the UK, or Australia. Most Indian students may know Luxembourg only as a wealthy European country, a finance hub, or a place","somewhere between Germany, France, and Belgium.","But sometimes, smaller destinations offer something bigger countries struggle to provide: lower crowd pressure, strong economic positioning, multilingual exposure, and access to a wider","European environment.","Luxembourg is one of the world’s wealthiest countries by GDP per capita. World Bank data shows Luxembourg’s GDP per capita was around"],bullets:[]},{heading:"US$137,781.7  in 2024",paragraphs:[", which reflects the country’s high-income economic base.","Now, GDP per capita does not mean every student will automatically get a job. That would be an irresponsible claim. But it does tell us something important: Luxembourg is not a weak or unstable economy. It is a high-value economy with strong sectors such as finance, technology, European institutions, law, business services, and multilingual professional work.","For Indian students, that matters.","Because the country you study in is not just a place where you attend classes. It is also the environment where you try to build your first international career."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Luxembourg: Small Country, Big Opportunities",paragraphs:["Luxembourg may be small, but that is exactly what makes it interesting.","Unlike larger study abroad destinations where students can easily feel lost in a huge system, Luxembourg offers a more compact and connected ecosystem. The country brings together government institutions, corporate offices, financial organisations, technology companies, European institutions, startup networks, and international professionals within a relatively small","space.","For students, this can be a real advantage.","A compact country often means more direct access to people, networks, events, institutions, and professional communities. Students who are proactive, confident, and willing to put themselves forward may find better opportunities to showcase their talent and gain visibility among diverse international audiences.","This is why “small country, big opportunities” is a fitting way to understand Luxembourg.","It is not just about studying in Europe. It is about entering a country where business, innovation, policy, finance, technology, and international talent often operate closely together. Luxembourg also has a strong focus on startups, innovation, and future-focused industries, supported by government-backed initiatives and a business-friendly environment.","But this also means the environment is competitive.","Luxembourg has a large expat-driven workforce, and students cannot depend only on having a degree. They need to bring strong skills, adaptability, communication, and the ability to stand out. In a country where opportunities exist but competition is serious, students must be ready to go the extra mile.","The cost of living is another point Indian families should understand honestly. Luxembourg is often seen as expensive, and that is true in many ways. Accommodation, food, lifestyle, and","daily expenses can be higher than in many other European countries.","But the cost conversation should not stop there.","Luxembourg also has significantly higher average salaries compared to many countries, which is one reason it continues to attract global professionals. This does not mean every student will","automatically earn well after graduation, but it does show that Luxembourg’s economy operates at a higher-value level.","So the real question is not simply:","“Is Luxembourg expensive?”","The better question is:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"“Can I build the kind of profile, skills, and network that helps me compete in Luxembourg’s high-value economy?”",paragraphs:["That is why Luxembourg is not for everyone. It is best suited for students who are ambitious, self-driven, open to multicultural environments, and willing to differentiate themselves.","For the right student, Luxembourg can offer more than a degree. It can offer visibility, access, and a serious platform to grow.","Luxembourg is not just “Europe”. It is a multilingual","career environment.","One thing Indian students often underestimate is language.","In Canada, the UK, Australia, and the US, English is the obvious advantage. In Germany, France, Italy, or Spain, language can become a barrier if students do not plan carefully.","Luxembourg sits in a different space.","Luxembourg is multilingual by nature. The country uses Luxembourgish, French and German in public life, while English is common in many international business, finance, technology and corporate environments. For Indian students, this means Luxembourg offers a different kind of European exposure, not just classroom learning, but everyday exposure to a multilingual and international professional culture.","This gives Indian students a different type of exposure."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Not just “English-speaking country exposure.”",paragraphs:["But European, multilingual, cross-border exposure.","That can be valuable for students interested in business, finance, data, public policy, law, technology, economics, European studies, and international careers.","But there is a catch.","Students must check the language requirements of each programme carefully.","So Luxembourg is not a lazy choice.","It rewards students who plan seriously."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The cost angle: Luxembourg may surprise Indian families",paragraphs:["For Indian families, cost is often the real pressure point.","A student may say, “I want to go abroad.”","But the parent is thinking:","How much will tuition cost?","How much will rent cost?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How long will it take to recover the investment?",paragraphs:["These are not negative questions. These are responsible questions.","When it comes to Luxembourg, families should not look only at tuition. They should calculate the full cost of studying and living in the country, including accommodation, food, transport,","insurance, residence requirements and personal expenses. Luxembourg is a high-income country, so it should not be promoted as a “cheap” destination. Its value lies in the larger picture:","a stable economy, European exposure, strong professional sectors and potential long-term career relevance.","This does not mean Luxembourg is “cheap.” Luxembourg can have high living costs. Housing especially can be expensive. So students should never market or understand it as a low-cost","destination without calculation.","But compared with countries where international tuition itself can be very high, Luxembourg creates an interesting ROI conversation.","The better way to explain it is this:","Luxembourg may not always be low-cost in living expenses, but it can be attractive when students compare tuition structure, work rights, economic strength, and European exposure","together.","That is the real financial angle."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Work while studying: what students need to know",paragraphs:["Many Indian students plan their abroad journey assuming they will work part-time.","That is understandable. Part-time work helps with living expenses, confidence, independence, and local exposure. But students should never build their entire financial plan assuming","part-time work will cover everything.","Indian students considering Luxembourg should understand the country’s student work rules before planning their finances. Part-time work can help students gain exposure and manage","some expenses, but it should not be treated as guaranteed income. The availability of work depends on language skills, course schedule, location, sector and the student’s own employability.","This is a useful point for Indian students.","But again, the blog should be honest.","Part-time work is not guaranteed.","English-only roles may be limited depending on the sector.","Students with French, German, or Luxembourgish exposure may have an advantage.","And students must balance work with academics.","So the right message is not, “Go to Luxembourg and easily earn.”"],bullets:[]},{heading:"The right message is",paragraphs:["Luxembourg gives students legal work access, but the students who benefit most will be those who build language skills, networking habits, and employable skills early."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Post-study stay: a realistic but not unlimited window",paragraphs:["Many students now choose countries based on one question:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"“Can I stay back after my studies?”",paragraphs:["This is practical. But it can also become dangerous if students only look at the length of stay-back and ignore the job market.","In Luxembourg, third-country nationals who complete their studies may apply for a residence permit to stay and look for a job or start a business after completing their studies. They must","apply before their student residence permit expires. The official Luxembourg public-service portal states that this residence permit is for students looking for work or setting up a business","after studies.","This gives students a pathway. But it is not a magic solution.","A post-study window is only useful if the student has:","A relevant degree.","A strong CV.","Some internship or project exposure.","Language readiness where required.","A clear job-market strategy.","The ability to network before graduation.","This is where Luxembourg should be positioned carefully.","Not as a “PR shortcut.”","Not as an easy immigration promise.","But as a serious European career environment for students who are willing to prepare properly."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The emotional truth: parents are not scared of studying",paragraphs:["abroad. They are scared of uncertainty.","When parents hesitate, students often think they are being unsupportive.","But most Indian parents are not against studying abroad. They are afraid of the unknown.","They are afraid of taking a loan.","They are afraid their child may feel lonely.","They are afraid of visa rejection.","They are afraid of sending their child to a country where they know nobody.","They are afraid of spending ₹30 lakh, ₹50 lakh, or ₹80 lakh without knowing what comes after.","This fear is valid.","And this is exactly why the “country selection” stage needs more honesty.","A family should not be pushed into a destination just because it is trending. They need to see the whole picture: tuition, living costs, visa rules, part-time work, post-study stay, job market,","teaching quality, safety, and student fit.","Luxembourg may not be the answer for every student. But for some students, it may reduce one kind of uncertainty: the fear of entering an overcrowded destination where policies are","constantly changing and competition is intense."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Luxembourg may be a strong option for students who",paragraphs:["Want a European education environment.","Are open to multilingual learning and cultural adaptation.","Are interested in finance, technology, business, economics, law, EU affairs, data, or research-oriented programmes.","Want a less saturated alternative to Canada, the UK, and Australia.","Are willing to build language skills.","Prefer a smaller, international country over a massive student destination.","Care about ROI but also understand that living costs must be planned carefully.","It may also suit students who are not just chasing a “foreign tag” but genuinely want a practical, globally exposed education.","This connects directly with Bhaskar’s argument that studying abroad should be about gaining perspective, learning effectively, and preparing for the real world, not merely collecting a foreign university name."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Who should not consider Luxembourg?",paragraphs:["Luxembourg may not be ideal for students who:","Want only an English-speaking country.","Are not willing to learn or adapt to a multilingual environment.","Need a large Indian student community for comfort.","Want multiple intake options and late application flexibility.","Are looking for an easy immigration promise.","Have not planned living costs properly.","Want a destination where every course and job pathway is already familiar to Indian families.","This is important.","A good counsellor should not sell every country to every student.","The right destination depends on the student’s profile, budget, career goal, family expectations,","risk appetite, and ability to adapt."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why Luxembourg should be on the Indian student shortlist in 2026",paragraphs:["Indian students are entering a new study abroad era.","The old formula was","Pick a popular country.","Choose a university.","Get admission.","Take a loan.","Hope everything works out.","The new formula has to be sharper:","Compare countries based on rules, cost, work rights, job market, economic strength, teaching quality, and personal fit.","Luxembourg deserves attention because it offers a combination that many Indian students are only beginning to understand: a wealthy European economy, international classrooms,","multilingual exposure, legal student work rights, and a post-study route for eligible graduates.","The combination of a high-income European economy, a culture that is multilingual, an international workforce, robust industries such as finance, technology, business services, and","institutions that are linked to the European Union, and access to a wider European environment is something that many Indian students are just beginning to comprehend. Luxembourg is a","country that deserves attention because it offers this combination.","That does not mean every Indian student will get the same result. But it does show that Luxembourg is not just a theoretical destination. It has a real international student ecosystem.","For the right student, that matters.","Final thought: Don’t follow the crowd. Follow the fit.","Studying abroad is not just about leaving India.","It is about choosing an environment where you can grow.","Sometimes that may be Canada.","Sometimes the UK.","Sometimes Australia.","Sometimes the US.","Sometimes Germany, Ireland, France, or the Netherlands.","And sometimes, for the right student, it may be Luxembourg.","The real mistake is not choosing one country over another. The real mistake is choosing without asking the right questions.","Before finalising any destination, Indian students and parents should ask:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Can my family afford the total cost, not just tuition?",paragraphs:["Does the course offer practical learning?","Are there legal work opportunities?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Am I choosing this because it fits me or because everyone else is going there?",paragraphs:["Because the best study abroad decision is not always the most popular one.","It is the one that makes sense, academically, financially, emotionally, and professionally. And in 2026, Luxembourg may be one of those smarter conversations Indian students should start having earlier.","Confused between Canada, UK, Australia, Europe, and newer destinations like Luxembourg?","At"],bullets:[]},{heading:"One Window Overseas Education",paragraphs:[", we help students and families compare countries based on profile, budget, career goals, visa rules, work options, and long-term ROI — not just","popularity.","Book a free country-shortlisting call and find out whether Luxembourg or another destination is the right fit for you."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"why-choosing-the-right-master-s-program-matters-more-than-ever-12",title:"Why Choosing the Right Master’s Program Matters More Than Ever",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/why-choosing-the-right-masters-program-matters-more-than-ever/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/right-masters-program-1024x571.jpeg",intro:"Every year, thousands of students, especially from India, reach the same point in their academic journey and ask the same question:",sections:[{heading:"Which master’s program is right for me?",paragraphs:["It sounds like a straightforward question, but anyone who has actually gone through the process knows it rarely feels simple.","There are rankings, university websites, social media opinions, counsellor recommendations, family expectations, career pressure, and, somewhere in the middle of all that noise, your own uncertainty. For many students, the process begins with excitement and quickly turns into confusion. And that is understandable. According to QS,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"45% of prospective international students consider five or more universities before making a final decision",paragraphs:[", which tells you how crowded and complex this decision has become."],bullets:[]},{heading:"For Indian students, the stakes are even higher. The Ministry of External Affairs says that",paragraphs:["nearly 1.25 million Indian students were pursuing higher education abroad as of January 2025.","That number reflects not just rising mobility, but also the scale of the decision students are trying to make in a highly competitive global education market.","And yet, despite how important this choice is, many students still approach it backwards. They begin with brand names. They begin with rankings. They begin with what seems popular at the moment. But the best decisions are rarely made that way.","The right master’s program is not simply the one with the strongest reputation or the flashiest brochure. It is the one that makes sense for your goals, your financial reality, your learning needs, and the kind of life and career you want to build after graduation.","That is why choosing the right master’s program matters so much. It is not just a two-year academic decision. In many cases, it shapes your first real career move, your professional network, your earning potential, your access to global opportunities, and your confidence in the direction you are heading.","So instead of asking, “Which university is best?” the better question is:","Which program is best for me?","Here are the","9 factors that actually matter","when making that decision.","Before you compare universities, tuition fees, or countries, it helps to pause and ask one question that sounds simple but changes everything:"],bullets:["Career Aspirations: Your Starting Point"]},{heading:"Where do I want to be in five years?",paragraphs:["That question matters because your master’s degree should not exist in isolation. It should connect to the work you want to do next.","A student who wants to build a career in data analytics should evaluate programs differently from someone aiming for academic research, public policy, product management, consulting, sustainability, finance, or entrepreneurship. Even when two students are interested in the same broad subject, their goals may demand different kinds of programs. One may need strong research supervision. Another may need internships and applied industry exposure. A third may need a program that opens doors in a specific geography or job market.","This is where many students make their first mistake: they start by searching for “top master’s programs” rather than defining what success looks like for them.","Your career aspirations influence:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"the subject you should pursue",paragraphs:["whether you should choose a research-focused or coursework-based program"],bullets:[]},{heading:"how much weight you should give to internships, faculty, curriculum, and post-study opportunities",paragraphs:["If you do not have perfect clarity yet, that is okay. Most students do not. But you need enough direction to evaluate programs with purpose.","A useful exercise is to write a short","career north star statement.","Just two or three lines. Something like:","I want to build a career in business analytics in a market where I can gain strong industry exposure and eventually move into strategy roles.","That one sentence becomes a filter. It helps you reject good-looking programs that do not actually move you toward the outcome you want.","When students skip this step, everything after that becomes harder. When they do it early, the entire search process becomes more focused.","Many students assume that if two universities offer a degree with the same name, the student experience and outcomes will be roughly similar. That is rarely true.","A master’s in Data Science at one university may be deeply technical and mathematics-heavy. Another may lean more toward business applications and visualization. A Management program at one institution may emphasise strategy and leadership, while another may focus more on analytics, operations, or entrepreneurship.","So much of your eventual value will come not from the degree title, but from the","actual structure of the program.","This is where you need to look closely at:","core subjects","electives","specialisation tracks","research opportunities","capstone projects","labs and practical components","interdisciplinary flexibility"],bullets:["Program Content and Specialization: What Will You Actually Learn?"]},{heading:"industry-linked coursework",paragraphs:["What you study on paper becomes what you can speak about in interviews, what projects you build, what problems you learn to solve, and how confidently you enter the job market.","This matters even more now because students themselves are increasingly looking beyond reputation and paying closer attention to quality and relevance. QS reports that","66% of students prioritise teaching quality when choosing a course, 58% link teaching quality to a high graduate employment rate, and 57% prioritise learning new skills for their future careers.","That tells us something important: students are not just choosing a university; they are choosing the kind of learning that will make them employable.","So when you review a program, do not stop at the overview page. Read the curriculum carefully. Look at the module descriptions. Check whether the course content is current. See if the learning is practical or purely theoretical. Ask whether the specialization matches where the industry is heading, not just where it has been.","Because at the end of the day, your degree title may get attention — but your skills are what will carry you forward.","Students often treat location as a lifestyle question. In reality, it is also a career question.","Where you study affects far more than weather, culture, or travel opportunities. It can shape:","internship access"],bullets:["Location and Global Exposure: Geography Is Not a Side Detail"]},{heading:"proximity to employers",paragraphs:["exposure to a specific industry ecosystem","networking opportunities","language environment","global visibility of the degree"],bullets:[]},{heading:"post-study employment possibilities",paragraphs:["A university in a city with a strong finance ecosystem may offer more relevant opportunities for a finance student than a stronger-ranked university in a region where that industry presence is limited. A technology-focused student may benefit from being near innovation hubs, startup communities, or major employers. A policy student may gain more in a capital city or international governance center than in a smaller academic town.","For Indian students, location also often overlaps with the promise of global exposure. Studying abroad can bring access to:","international classrooms","multicultural collaboration","exposure to different work styles"],bullets:[]},{heading:"improved communication and adaptability skills",paragraphs:["These are real advantages, especially in industries that value cross-border mobility and global teams.","But it is also important not to turn “abroad” into a shortcut for “better.” That is not always true. Some programs in India are highly competitive, academically rigorous, and more cost-effective than overseas options. In some cases, they may offer a better return relative to the student’s goals.","So the right question is not “Should I study in India or abroad?” It is “Which location gives me the strongest combination of learning, opportunity, affordability, and career relevance?”","Location is not just where you will live. It is the ecosystem you will grow inside.","A master’s degree is one of the biggest financial decisions many students and families will make. And yet, it is surprising how often that decision is reduced to a single number: tuition fees.","That approach is too narrow.","A better way to think about the financial side of a master’s degree is to evaluate the","total cost and long-term return.","That includes:","tuition fees","living expenses","accommodation","health insurance","travel","scholarship availability","assistantships or funding","part-time work options"],bullets:["Financial Considerations and ROI: Cost Matters, but Context Matters More"]},{heading:"time required to recover the investment",paragraphs:["This does not mean you should only choose the cheapest option. A more expensive program may still be the smarter one if it offers stronger outcomes, better employability, and greater long-term upside. But those outcomes need to be realistic and relevant to your goals."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The broader data supports why ROI deserves serious attention. OECD data shows that adults with a",paragraphs:["tertiary qualification earn, on average, 54% more than those with only upper secondary education,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"and the earnings advantage rises to",paragraphs:["83% for those with a master’s or doctoral qualification","across OECD countries.","That does not mean every master’s degree automatically guarantees a high return. It means the level of qualification can make a significant difference — but the return still depends heavily on what you study, where you study, and how effectively the degree connects to the labour market.","This is why students need to move away from emotional thinking like:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"“This fee is high, so the program must be premium.”",paragraphs:["“This course is cheap, so it must be a compromise.”","Instead, ask:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Will this degree justify its cost over time — financially, professionally, and personally?",paragraphs:["That is a much better question, and usually a much more honest one.","Rankings are useful. They can help you build an initial shortlist, spot broad quality indicators, and identify institutions with strong academic standing.","But rankings are often where students stop thinking, when they should actually be thinking more carefully.","A university may be highly ranked overall and still be average in the field you care about. On the other hand, a less globally famous institution may have:","an outstanding department","stronger faculty in your niche","better industry links"],bullets:["University Reputation vs Program Reputation: Not Always the Same Thing"]},{heading:"more relevant practical training",paragraphs:["stronger outcomes in your specific discipline","This distinction matters.","A student choosing a specialized master’s program should pay close attention to:","department strength","faculty expertise","curriculum relevance","research output in that field","employer recognition in that field"],bullets:[]},{heading:"alumni outcomes from that specific program",paragraphs:["In many cases, a specialized program at a mid-ranked university can produce better career results than a broad program at a more famous institution.","This is especially true in applied and fast-moving fields, where employer recognition may depend more on skill readiness and experience than institutional prestige alone.","QS’ student data also reflects that students are increasingly connecting quality with outcomes, not just brand. A high graduate employment rate matters significantly when students assess teaching quality and career value.","So yes, reputation matters. But the right kind of reputation matters more.","The goal is not to collect a famous university name. The goal is to choose a program whose reputation actually supports the path you want to take.","At some point, every student has to ask the question that cuts through almost everything else:"],bullets:["Career Outcomes and Placements: The Program Has to Lead Somewhere"]},{heading:"What happens after graduation?",paragraphs:["Because if a master’s degree does not meaningfully improve your trajectory, then even an enjoyable academic experience may not be enough.","This is why career outcomes deserve a central place in the decision-making process.","When evaluating a program, look for:","employment rates","top recruiters"],bullets:[]},{heading:"internship and co-op access",paragraphs:["average salary outcomes where available","career services quality","employer partnerships","industry mentorship"],bullets:[]},{heading:"alumni job titles and trajectories",paragraphs:["Do not just look for vague claims like “excellent employability” or “strong industry ties.” Look for specifics. Which employers recruit from the program? What kinds of roles do graduates move into? How quickly do students typically find work? What support exists during the job search?","The broader labour-market data makes this even more relevant. OECD figures show that employment rates rise with higher levels of tertiary attainment: 83% for short-cycle tertiary qualifications, 86% for bachelor’s degrees, 90% for master’s degrees, and 93% for doctoral qualifications among adults aged 25–64 across OECD countries.","Again, this should not be read as a guarantee for any single student or program. But it strongly supports the idea that advanced qualifications are often associated with better labour-market outcomes."],bullets:[]},{heading:"And prospective students are clearly thinking this way too. QS reports that",paragraphs:["56% of respondents say a high graduate employment rate is the most important factor when assessing a university’s teaching quality, while 51% say the most important factor in choosing a course is that it leads to their chosen career.","That is exactly why career outcomes should not be treated as a “later” question. They should be part of the evaluation from the beginning.","A useful test is this:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Can I clearly see how this program could lead to the type of job I want?",paragraphs:["If the answer feels vague, keep digging.","For students considering a master’s abroad, post-study work rights can be one of the most practical and decisive factors in the entire process.","You may choose a good university and a relevant program, but if the country’s work permissions after graduation are too restrictive, your ability to gain experience, recover your investment, and build an international career may be limited.","That is why post-study work and visa pathways should never be treated as an afterthought.","They directly affect your ability to:"],bullets:["Post-Study Work and Visa Options: Opportunity Depends on Policy Too"]},{heading:"stay in the country long enough to gain professional experience",paragraphs:["transition from student life to employment"],bullets:[]},{heading:"create longer-term migration or mobility options where relevant",paragraphs:["This factor becomes especially important for students whose decision to study abroad is connected not only to education, but also to early-career international work experience.","At the same time, this is one of the most time-sensitive parts of the entire decision-making process. Policies change. Governments revise rules. Work rights shift. Sponsorship conditions evolve.","So the principle here is simple:","Always verify visa and post-study work options through official government sources before making a final decision.","Not a blog. Not a social media post. Not a WhatsApp forward. Not even a university webinar in isolation.","This is one of those areas where “I thought that was the rule” can become a very expensive sentence.","University websites are designed to attract you. Alumni are far more likely to tell you what it actually feels like to study there.","And that difference matters.","A brochure can tell you a program is innovative, industry-driven, and globally focused. But alumni can tell you:"],bullets:["Alumni Insights and Peer Networks: What the Brochure Will Never Tell You"]},{heading:"how demanding the workload really is",paragraphs:["whether the teaching quality matches the promise"],bullets:[]},{heading:"how accessible faculty members are",paragraphs:["what the internship search actually feels like"],bullets:[]},{heading:"what kind of roles graduates are realistically getting",paragraphs:["This kind of information is often the most useful because it sits between marketing and reality.","Alumni can also help you understand softer but important aspects of the experience:","campus culture","pressure levels","support systems"],bullets:[]},{heading:"whether the course delivered on its stated outcomes",paragraphs:["A single thoughtful conversation with the right graduate can sometimes save a student from making an expensive mismatch.","The best way to approach alumni is with specific questions. Instead of sending a generic “Can you tell me about your experience?” ask things like:","How useful was the curriculum in your job search?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What kind of roles did your peers move into?",paragraphs:["Most alumni are willing to help if the message is respectful and well thought out.","Peer networks matter too. The people you study with often become part of your professional ecosystem. A strong cohort can improve your learning, broaden your perspective, and create opportunities long after graduation.","This is one of the least glamorous parts of the conversation, which is exactly why students often ignore it.","But personal fit matters a great deal.","Even a highly ranked, academically strong program can become difficult to succeed in if your daily environment is constantly draining you. Students do not perform only on the strength of curriculum. They perform within the realities of language, culture, distance, cost of living, climate, support systems, and mental well-being.","That means you need to consider:","cultural adaptability","language comfort","local support systems","distance from home","social environment","pace of life","cost of living"],bullets:["Personal Comfort and Lifestyle Fit: An Overlooked Factor That Shapes Performance"]},{heading:"how likely you are to adapt and sustain yourself there",paragraphs:["This is not about avoiding challenge. Challenge is often part of growth. But there is a difference between a healthy stretch and constant instability.","For some students, living in a completely unfamiliar environment can be energising. For others, it can create enough friction to affect confidence, focus, and performance. Neither response is wrong. What matters is self-awareness.","A good program should not only look good on paper. It should be a place where you can realistically learn, adapt, and function well enough to make the most of the opportunity.","Academic fit and personal fit are not separate conversations. They are deeply connected."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Final Thoughts: There Is No Perfect Master’s Program",paragraphs:["One of the most useful truths in this process is also one of the most reassuring:","There is no single perfect master’s program.","There is no one university that is right for everyone. No one country that solves every problem. No one ranking that can make the decision for you.","There is only the option that fits your ambitions, your budget, your learning style, your preferred environment, and your long-term direction better than the alternatives.","The smartest students are not the ones who simply chase rankings or follow trends. They are the ones who ask better questions. They look beyond the surface. They evaluate options across multiple dimensions. And they make intentional decisions rather than reactive ones.","That is what turns a university choice into a meaningful career decision."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What You Should Do Next",paragraphs:["If you are currently evaluating master’s programs, do not compare them randomly. At One Window, we help students go beyond shortlists and rankings. We work with them to build a vision board of their dream programs, map out realistic career pathways, and make decisions that align with both ambition and long-term outcomes. Because choosing a master’s program should not feel like guesswork, it should feel like a plan.","Build a structure.","Start by:","listing your top options"],bullets:[]},{heading:"verifying practical details like finances, outcomes, and visa pathways before you commit",paragraphs:["Because the goal is not just to get admitted.","The goal is to choose a master’s program that actually supports the future you want to build and One Window is here to help you.","FAQ","Choose the right master’s program by evaluating your career goals, curriculum, specialization options, costs, placements, post-study work opportunities, and overall fit instead of relying only on rankings.","You should consider career aspirations, program content, location, financial ROI, university reputation, career outcomes, visa options, alumni feedback, and personal comfort.","No, university ranking is only one factor. Program quality, faculty strength, specialization, industry relevance, and graduate outcomes often matter more."],bullets:["How do I choose the right master’s program?","What factors should I consider before choosing a master’s degree?","Is university ranking the most important factor when choosing a master’s program?"]},{heading:"How important is specialization in a master’s program?",paragraphs:["Specialization is very important because it shapes the skills you build, the roles you qualify for, and how closely the degree matches your long-term career plans.","Yes, career outcomes are one of the most important factors. You should look at placements, internships, recruiter connections, alumni roles, and employability support.","To judge whether a master’s degree is worth the cost, compare tuition fees, living expenses, scholarships, job opportunities, salary potential, and long-term return on investment.","Program reputation is often more important for specialized fields because a strong department or course can offer better academic and career outcomes than a higher-ranked university overall.","Yes, post-study work options matter because they affect your ability to gain international work experience, recover your investment, and build a longer-term global career path.","Speaking to alumni helps you understand the real student experience, teaching quality, workload, internship access, placements, and whether the program delivers on its promises.","Indian students can compare master’s programs effectively by creating a shortlist and scoring each option across factors like career fit, curriculum, cost, outcomes, location, and lifestyle suitability."],bullets:["Should I choose a master’s program based on career outcomes?","How do I know if a master’s degree is worth the cost?","What is more important: university reputation or program reputation?","Do post-study work options matter when choosing a master’s abroad?","Why should I speak to alumni before selecting a master’s program?","How can Indian students compare master’s programs effectively?"]}]},{slug:"europe-is-quietly-becoming-the-smartest-place-to-study-abroad-13",title:"Europe Is Quietly Becoming the Smartest Place to Study Abroad",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/europe-is-quietly-becoming-the-smartest-place-to-study-abroad/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Europe-Quietly-Becoming-Smartest-Place-Study-Abroad.jpeg",intro:"A real talk about why thousands of students every year pack up, head to Europe, and never look back, and why you might want to join them. From AI labs in Helsinki to climate innovation hubs in Amsterdam,  why the world’s brightest students choosing Europe to shape the future?",sections:[{heading:"The Case for Europe",paragraphs:["When most students think about going abroad for university, they picture two places: the US or the UK. Big names. Big campuses. Big price tags. But here’s what nobody tells you early enough: Europe has quietly become one of the most exciting, affordable, and genuinely life-changing places in the world to get a degree. And a lot of students are figuring this reality out too late.","I’m not talking about some nostalgic “study in a medieval town and find yourself” fantasy. I’m talking about world-class research labs, programs taught entirely in English, cities that run on clean energy, and, in some countries, completely free tuition in public universities. Yes, even for international students.","Quick reality check:","A full master’s degree in Germany or Norway can cost you less than one semester’s tuition at a mid-tier American university. The quality? Frequently higher. Let that sink in.","“The university of the future is not a building, it is a borderless intellectual ecosystem."],bullets:[]},{heading:"And Europe, right now, is building exactly that.”",paragraphs:["— Education Futures Forum, Davos 2025"],bullets:[]},{heading:"So, Why Now? What’s Changed?",paragraphs:["The timing actually matters here. The world is going through a massive shift. AI is changing every industry, climate change is reshaping what careers look like, and the ability to work across cultures and borders is no longer a “nice to have.” It’s essential.","European universities have moved fast to respond to all of these changes. They’re not just adding AI modules to old degrees; they’re building brand new programmes where students work alongside AI tools from day one. Climate science, green engineering, and circular economy thinking isn’t an elective here; it’s baked into the curriculum across nearly every field.","And the geopolitical reality? Studying in Europe, surrounded by 26 different countries, dozens of languages, and students from over 120 nationalities, that’s not just education. That’s training for the world you’re actually going to work in.","50+ countries","sending students to Europe","4M+ international","students enrolled in EU","40% more","AI research papers from EU vs 2020","26 countries","in the Schengen travel zone","Six Reasons","to Choose Europe"],bullets:[]},{heading:"01 Radically Low Tuition",paragraphs:["Germany, Norway, and Finland charge little to no tuition for international students. France and Austria offer world-class education for a few hundred euros per year,  a revolution in academic access."],bullets:[]},{heading:"02 AI & Deep Tech Labs",paragraphs:["From ETH Zurich’s robotics halls to Stockholm’s fintech incubators, European campuses are where the next generation of transformative technology is actually being built, not just theorised."],bullets:[]},{heading:"03 Green Futures at the Core",paragraphs:["Climate science, renewable energy, and circular economy design are woven into programmes across disciplines. Study engineering in Denmark and you will work on wind farm simulations as a first-year student."],bullets:[]},{heading:"04 One Continent, Infinite Campuses",paragraphs:["The Erasmus+ programme lets students move freely between partner universities across 33 countries, often with grants to cover travel and living costs. Your degree can span three countries."],bullets:[]},{heading:"05 The World in One Classroom",paragraphs:["European cities are among the world’s most culturally layered. Study alongside peers from 120 nationalities and leave with a global network  and a genuinely multicultural worldview."],bullets:[]},{heading:"06 Institutions With Centuries of Depth",paragraphs:["The University of Bologna was founded in 1088. Oxford in 1096. These are not just credentials; they are living intellectual traditions that continue to shape how knowledge is created and shared.","The Movements Reshaping European Education"],bullets:[]},{heading:"01 Human-AI Collaborative Degrees",paragraphs:["From Helsinki to Barcelona, universities are building programmes where students don’t just study AI  they use it as a research partner. You learn to direct it, question it, and govern it ethically. That’s a fundamentally different kind of education than reading a textbook about machine learning."],bullets:[]},{heading:"02 The Climate Campus Revolution",paragraphs:["Sustainability isn’t a buzzword. The EU’s Green Deal has funnelled billions into academic research infrastructure. New interdisciplinary schools of “planetary health” are emerging that combine ecology, economics, law, and engineering into programmes designed explicitly to address the climate emergency."],bullets:[]},{heading:"03 New Model: Studying in four campuses at once",paragraphs:["The EU’s flagship academic project links over 60 university alliances across member states into transnational institutions. Students in one country can earn joint degrees with peers on four campuses simultaneously, a genuinely new model of higher learning that has no parallel elsewhere in the world."],bullets:[]},{heading:"04 Quality of Life as a Learning Metric",paragraphs:["Scandinavian universities now treat student wellbeing as a core educational outcome. Flexible schedules, mental health integration, work-life balance policies, and universal healthcare coverage mean that studying in Europe is not just intellectually enriching; it is restorative."],bullets:[]},{heading:"05 English-Taught Programmes Explode",paragraphs:["Over 10,000 fully English-taught Master’s programmes now operate across continental Europe,  a fourfold increase since 2015. The language barrier that once dissuaded international students has largely dissolved, opening the continent to a truly global cohort.","✦ ✦ ✦","The best education is the one that changes","not just what you know —","but who you become.","Europe offers centuries of wisdom, a living laboratory of cultures, and front-row access to the technologies defining the next hundred years. The question is no longer why you should come. It is why you would go anywhere else.","A Final Word","Europe does that. Living and studying in a place where history, innovation, and radical openness coexist affects you. It stretches you in the best possible way.","The students who are going to lead things in the 2040s are deciding where to study right now. Some of them will choose safely and locally. Others will choose an education that takes them somewhere new and challenges their beliefs.","Pack your bags. The continent is waiting.","FAQs","Europe is becoming a popular choice because it offers high-quality education, lower tuition in many countries, strong career opportunities, and access to multicultural learning environments.","Yes, many European countries offer affordable tuition, and some public universities charge little to no tuition fees. Compared to other major study abroad destinations, Europe can be a more cost-effective option.","Yes, thousands of programmes across Europe are taught entirely in English, especially at the master’s level, making it easier for international students to apply without needing to learn a new language first.","Popular choices include Germany, France, the Netherlands, Sweden, Finland, Ireland, and Denmark, depending on the student’s course, budget, and career goals.","That depends on the student’s priorities. For many students, Europe offers a better balance of affordability, academic quality, cultural exposure, and future-focused education.","Yes, many European universities are known for strong research, innovation, and industry-linked programmes in areas like AI, robotics, engineering, sustainability, and fintech.","Students benefit from quality education, lower costs, international exposure, access to multiple countries, diverse classrooms, and programmes aligned with emerging global careers.","Yes, one of Europe’s biggest advantages is its connectivity. Students often have the chance to travel across neighbouring countries and experience different cultures during their studies.","Yes, studying in Europe can help students build international exposure, cross-cultural communication skills, and a broader professional network, which are valuable in global careers.","Students should consider their course, tuition budget, language of instruction, career plans, location preferences, and post-study opportunities before making a decision."],bullets:["Why is Europe becoming a popular study abroad destination?","Is studying in Europe affordable for international students?","Can international students study in Europe in English?","Which European countries are best for international students?","Is Europe better than the US or UK for studying abroad?","Are European universities good for AI, technology, and innovation?","What are the benefits of studying in Europe?","Can students travel while studying in Europe?","Does studying in Europe help with global career opportunities?","How do I choose the right university in Europe?"]}]},{slug:"unleash-opportunities-with-these-summer-programs-international-students-edition-14",title:"Unleash Opportunities With These Summer Programs: International Students Edition",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/unleash-opportunities-with-these-summer-programs-international-students-edition/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Summer-Program-Thumbnail-1024x576.png",intro:"As summer approaches, many students see it as a time to unwind, relax, and escape academic responsibilities. But if you believe the summer is just a season for lounging and losing momentum, you might be living in a comfortable delusion. In today’s hyper-competitive world, summers are no longer just a break—they’re a window of opportunity. If you’re serious about shaping your future, building a career, and standing out from the crowd, then it’s time to stop treating summer like a vacation—and start using it like an accelerator. This is your moment to gain real-world exposure, learn from top-tier educators, and engage with diverse peers across international borders. Summer programs abroad",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["offer exactly that:","transformative experiences that go beyond classroom walls. Whether you want to sharpen your academic edge, immerse yourself in new cultures, or expand your global network, these programs are designed to push you forward—not let you drift."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Benefits of Participating in Summer Programs",paragraphs:["Participating in summer programs offers a myriad of benefits that extend far beyond traditional classroom education. One of the most significant advantages is the opportunity to gain a global perspective. By engaging with students from diverse backgrounds and cultures, you can develop a nuanced understanding of the world, which is increasingly essential in our interconnected society. This exposure helps in improving empathy and broadening your perspective, making you more adaptable and open-minded.","Another key benefit is the enhancement of your academic and professional skills. Summer programs often feature courses and workshops led by renowned educators and industry experts. These sessions are designed to be intensive and focused, providing significant knowledge into specific subjects that may not be covered in your regular curriculum. Whether it’s advanced research techniques, innovative problem-solving methods, or leadership skills, the knowledge and experience gained can give you a competitive edge in your future career.","Moreover, summer programs are a fantastic way to build a global network. The relationships you form with peers, instructors, and professionals can open doors to future opportunities, whether it’s internships, job offers, or collaborative projects. These connections can be invaluable as you navigate your academic and professional journey.","The best-fit place:","When it comes to"],bullets:[]},{heading:"choosing a destination",paragraphs:["for your summer program, the options are virtually limitless. There are numerous prestigious programs available in countries such as the United Kingdom, France, and Germany, which is why Europe remains a perennial favorite. These countries are known for their rich academic traditions and cultural heritage, providing an immersive experience that combines rigorous learning with historical exploration. Cities like London, Paris, and Berlin are not only educational hubs but also cultural epicenters that offer endless opportunities for personal growth.","North America, particularly the United States and Canada, continues to attract international students for summer programs. Renowned institutions like","Harvard",",","Stanford",", and the"],bullets:[]},{heading:"University of Toronto",paragraphs:["offer a wide range of courses that cater to various academic interests. These programs are known for their innovative teaching methods and advanced facilities. Additionally, the multicultural environments of cities like Boston, San Francisco, and Toronto provide a welcoming atmosphere for international students, making it easier to adapt and thrive.","Top Summer Programs to Consider","If you’re looking for a","summer program","that stands out, several institutions and organizations offer exceptional opportunities. Below are some lists of universities that you can choose from:","International students can achieve their academic and personal goals by participating in a summer program at an institution in the US. Taking additional classes during summer school in the US gives enrolled students the best chance of finishing their degree requirements. This","advice","might be useful for people with a bachelor’s degree who are interested in living on a college campus in the United States.","Summer Law and English (SLE) at the","University of Southern California","Tufts University","’s STEM Scholar Program","The summer school program at","Yale University","English for academic purposes at","George Washington University.","English as a Second Language (ESL):","Manhattan University","*Note:","Summer schools in the USA are often held between May and August and can last two to twelve weeks. For more information, reach out to our advisors.","Studying abroad in Canada and participating in summer school programs can offer students an exciting chance to obtain a top-notch education. Students learning French and English during","summer programs","in Canada can also experience life abroad.","Check out the below 5 summer programs in Canada:","Vancouver Summer Program,"],bullets:["USA","Canada"]},{heading:"University of British Columbia",paragraphs:["Business Management, Tourism and Hospitality Management,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Vancouver Island University",paragraphs:["Professional Cook, Summer Programs, Certificate Programs –"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Thompson Rivers University",paragraphs:["Human Psychology, Summer Business Courses, and Language Courses –"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Bishop’s University",paragraphs:["Financial Accounting-I, Medical Office Administration, Pharmacy Assistant –","CIMT College","The","United Kingdom","stands out as a leading nation in providing excellent education, featuring a wider array of innovative courses than other countries, including short term courses. Numerous students opt for short-term courses or summer schools to study in the UK, seeking first-hand experience of the UK’s renowned education system."],bullets:["UNITED KINGDOM"]},{heading:"Check out the below 5 summer programs in UK",paragraphs:["Principles of Business and Management,","University of Sussex","Revolution in Biomedicine,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Imperial College London",paragraphs:["Pre-university summer school of law,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Kings College London",paragraphs:["Communication for PR and Advertising,","University of Arts London","Beginners in a specific language:","University of Westminster","France","has long been a beacon of academic and cultural excellence, making it a popular destination for international students seeking short-term and summer study programs. With its world-renowned institutions, rich intellectual traditions, and deep cultural heritage, France offers students a unique blend of rigorous academics and immersive experiences. From cancer research to environmental sciences and language immersion, France’s summer programs are as diverse as they are enriching, drawing students from around the globe to explore education.","Check out the below 5 summer programs in France","Cancer Epidemiology and Prevention,","IARC Summer School","Multidisciplinary Humanities and Culture,","Sorbonne Summer University","Social Sciences and Law,"],bullets:["France"]},{heading:"Sciences Po Summer School",paragraphs:["Life Sciences and Digital Health, University of","Strasbourg Summer School","Germany","is widely recognized for its commitment to cutting-edge research, strong academic foundations, and affordability—even for international students. In recent years, Germany has become a hub for high-quality, short-term academic programs that attract students from across the world. Whether you’re interested in STEM, environmental policy, or humanities, Germany’s summer offerings blend practical experience with strong theoretical learning in some of Europe’s most dynamic cities.","Check out the below 5 summer programs in Germany:","Economics, Law, and Medicine Modules,"],bullets:["Germany"]},{heading:"Munich International Summer University (MISU), LMU Munich",paragraphs:["Energy and Materials Science Research,","HZB International Summer Program","German Language & European Studies,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"FUBiS Summer School, Freie Universität",paragraphs:["Robotics and Artificial Intelligence,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"RWTH Aachen University Summer School",paragraphs:["Science and Engineering Internships,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How to Choose the Right Summer Program for You",paragraphs:["Selecting the right summer program involves careful consideration of several factors. First, you should evaluate your academic and career goals. Identify the subjects or skills you want to focus on and look for programs that offer specialized courses in those areas. It’s important to choose a program that aligns with your long-term objectives, whether it’s gaining expertise in a particular field, strengthening your research skills, or building a professional network.","Another crucial factor is the location. Consider the cultural and social environment of the destination and how it aligns with your interests and comfort level. Some students thrive in bustling urban settings, while others prefer quieter, more serene environments. Additionally, think about the language spoken in the host country and whether you are comfortable communicating in it or if the program offers support for non-native speakers.","Lastly, consider the program’s structure and","support services",". Look for programs that offer a balanced mix of academic rigor and extracurricular activities. Support services such as orientation sessions, academic advising, and cultural immersion activities can significantly enhance your experience. It’s also beneficial to read reviews and testimonials from past participants to get a sense of what to expect and whether the program meets your expectations."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Application Process and Requirements",paragraphs:["The application process for summer programs can vary widely depending on the institution and the program. Generally, you will need to submit an application form, academic transcripts, and letters of recommendation. Some programs may also require a","personal statement","or essay that outlines your goals and motivations for applying. It’s essential to start the application process early to ensure you have enough time to gather all the necessary documents and meet the deadlines.","Standardized test scores, such as the","SAT",",","ACT",", or","TOEFL",", may also be required, particularly for programs in English-speaking countries. Make sure to check the specific requirements of each program and prepare accordingly. If you need to take a test, plan your study schedule and register for the exam well in advance. Additionally, some programs may conduct interviews as part of the selection process, so be prepared to articulate your interests and goals clearly.","Financial considerations are also an important part of the application process. Many programs offer scholarships and financial aid to help offset the costs. Be sure to research the funding options available and submit any necessary applications for financial assistance. It’s also helpful to have a clear understanding of the total cost of the program, including tuition, accommodation, travel, and other expenses, so you can plan your budget accordingly."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Financial Considerations: Scholarships and Funding Options",paragraphs:["Attending a summer program can be a significant financial investment, but there are various scholarships and funding options available to help manage the costs. Many institutions offer merit-based scholarships that reward academic excellence, leadership, and other achievements. These scholarships can cover a portion or even the full cost of the program, making it more accessible to students from diverse backgrounds.","Need-based financial aid is another option to explore. Many programs have funds set aside to support students who demonstrate financial need. The application process for need-based aid typically involves submitting detailed financial information, such as income statements and tax returns. It’s important to provide accurate and thorough documentation to maximize your chances of receiving assistance.","In addition to institutional scholarships and financial aid, there are external funding sources to consider. Organizations such as the Fulbright Program, Rotary International, and various foundations offer grants and scholarships specifically for international students. These funding opportunities can be highly competitive, so it’s crucial to"],bullets:[]},{heading:"start your search early",paragraphs:["and prepare strong applications. Reach out to your school’s financial aid office or career services for guidance on finding and applying for external scholarships."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Cultural Immersion: What to Expect",paragraphs:["One of the most rewarding aspects of participating in a summer program is the opportunity for cultural immersion. Living and studying in a different country allows you to experience new customs, traditions, and ways of life firsthand. This immersive experience can broaden your cultural understanding and appreciation, helping you develop a more global perspective.","Expect to encounter cultural differences in various aspects of daily life, from social interactions to dining habits. It’s important to approach these differences with an open mind and a willingness to learn. Engaging with local students and participating in cultural activities can enhance your experience and help you build meaningful connections. Many programs offer cultural excursions, language classes, and workshops to help you acclimate and make the most of your time abroad.","Cultural immersion also involves adapting to a different educational system. You may find variations in teaching styles, classroom dynamics, and academic expectations. Embrace these differences as opportunities to grow and develop new skills. Being flexible and proactive in seeking support, whether through academic advisors or peer mentors, can help you navigate any challenges and make the most of your educational experience."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Success Stories: Alumni Experiences",paragraphs:["Hearing from alumni who have participated in summer programs can provide valuable insights and inspiration. Many former participants have gone on to achieve great success in their academic and professional careers, attributing their growth to the experiences and connections they gained during their summer programs. These success stories highlight the transformative potential of such programs.","For instance, an alumnus of the Harvard Summer School shared how the program helped them secure a prestigious internship at a top consulting firm. The rigorous coursework and networking opportunities provided a solid foundation and opened doors to career prospects that would have otherwise been out of reach. The experience also helped them develop critical thinking and problem-solving skills that have been invaluable in their professional journey.","Another success story comes from a student who attended the University of Oxford’s Summer School. They spoke about how the program deepened their passion for history and inspired them to pursue a Ph.D. in the field. The connections they made with faculty and peers have continued to support them throughout their academic career. The immersive environment and access to world-class resources were instrumental in shaping their academic goals and achievements.","A participant of the Stanford Summer Session shared how the program ignited their entrepreneurial spirit. Engaging with industry leaders and visiting tech companies provided real-world insights that fueled their ambition to start their own business. The skills and knowledge gained during the program, combined with the vibrant entrepreneurial ecosystem of Silicon Valley, gave them the confidence and tools to launch a successful startup."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Conclusion and Next Steps for Interested Students",paragraphs:["As we’ve explored, summer programs offer unparalleled opportunities for academic enrichment, cultural immersion, and professional development. Whether you’re aiming to deepen your knowledge in a specific field, gain a global perspective, or build a network of international peers, there’s a summer program that can help you achieve your goals. The experiences and skills you gain can have a lasting impact on your personal and professional growth.","If you’re interested in participating in a summer program, the first step is to research and identify programs that align with your interests and objectives. Take the time to read about the courses, faculty, and cultural activities offered by each program. Reach out to program coordinators or alumni to ask questions and gather more information. This proactive approach will help you make an informed decision and choose a program that best fits your needs.","Once you’ve identified potential programs, start the application process early. Prepare the necessary documents, such as academic transcripts, letters of recommendation, and personal statements. Pay close attention to application deadlines and requirements to ensure a smooth and timely submission. If financial considerations are a concern, explore scholarship and funding options and apply for financial aid as needed.","In conclusion, summer programs are a gateway to unlocking global opportunities and shaping your future. By participating in these programs, you can gain valuable experiences, expand your horizons, and build a foundation for success in an increasingly interconnected world. So take the leap, embrace the adventure, and make the most of the summer."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"france-as-a-study-abroad-destination-15",title:"FRANCE AS A STUDY ABROAD DESTINATION",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/france-as-a-study-abroad-destination/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Thumbnails-for-Website-YT-1.png",intro:"Are you considering Europe as your future study destination? If the answer is yes, you should surely pursue your education in France! Studying abroad has various advantages, and France provides a unique educational experience for individuals seeking a challenging and conducive environment to reach their goals. France, sometimes known as the French Republic, is a Western European republic that extends into South America via French Guiana and many islands in the Atlantic, Pacific, and Indian oceans. It borders France, Luxembourg, Germany, Switzerland, Monaco, Italy, Andorra, Spain, and the Netherlands. France has the most time zones of any country due to its broad territorial limits around the world. Consider living in a country with stunning scenery, such as the great capital city of Paris, the stunningly lovely French South villages, the endless Bordeaux vineyards, the fascinating city of Strasbourg, or the romantic French Alps wrapped in snow.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["France is a well-known European and global hub for trade, technology, tourism, philosophy, arts, culture, and science. If you study abroad in France, your resume may carry significant weight. France is one of the top locations to visit because it receives over 85 million people annually. In actuality, the country possesses the world’s seventh-largest economy and the fifth-highest number of Unesco World Heritage Sites.","Let’s look at some of the top reasons why you should make France your first choice for pursuing your next degree:","Quality education:","The French educational system is renowned for its exceptional quality and is often regarded as one of the finest in the world. French universities provide a wide range of programmes in several fields, such as business, engineering, the arts, and science. Studying in France is beneficial for this specific reason.","Diverse environment:","There are many international students studying in France, a country with a diverse population. It gives Indian students studying in France the chance to socialize with people from all backgrounds.","Affordability:","One of the key advantages of studying in France is the availability of affordable education. In comparison to other well-liked study abroad locations like the US and the UK, living and studying expenses in France are very affordable.","Opportunities to learn a language:","French holds significant global importance as one of the most widely spoken languages. Undoubtedly, studying in France offers Indian students the opportunity to immerse themselves in the language and enhance their fluency.","Opportunities for a career:","French businesses are well-represented worldwide. For Indian students, this means that earning a degree from a French university can lead to employment prospects abroad. Thus, one of the main advantages of studying in France is the abundance of professional prospects.","Here are the top 6 courses to take into consideration while choosing to study in France:","Tourism and Hospitality Management:","France, the world’s most visited country, attracts over 117 million international tourists each year, captivated by its rich history and iconic landmarks. This makes France an exceptional destination for studying tourism and hospitality management. The program provides students with the skills necessary to thrive in the dynamic tourism and hospitality sectors. If your career goals include excelling in various aspects of this industry, choosing to study in France is a smart and strategic decision.","International business :","A French master’s degree in international business offers a strong basis for comprehending international markets and company procedures in a world growing more interconnected by the day. After completing their master’s degree in France, international students are well-suited for positions in strategic management, international marketing, and commerce, which makes them an advantage to businesses with a global reach.","Data Processing:","Given the speed at which technology is developing, studying for a master’s degree in information technology in France makes sense. Many French universities provide a range of information technology specializations. Students can pursue careers in cybersecurity, AI, and data science, among other topics.","Luxury Brand Management :","Pursuing a master’s degree in luxury brand management in France is a dream come true for international students. Renowned for its iconic luxury brands and legendary fashion houses, France provides an unparalleled learning environment. This program equips students with essential skills for managing and elevating prestigious brands, covering everything from strategic brand planning to the intricacies of luxury retail. Immerse yourself in the unique educational experience that only France can offer.","Masters in Management:","There are numerous opportunities for international students to learn in France, but getting a master’s degree in management makes things even better. Many of the best universities in the world offer courses in management and business, making France a leader in this area. Because they have high standards, about one hundred French schools are known all over the world. With a master’s degree in management, students gain skills that are recognised around the world and can help them get jobs as managers in a wide range of businesses.","Accounting & Finance:","International students have several possibilities for studying in France, and earning a master’s degree in management only makes things better. Higher education in management and business is very good in France, thanks to its well-known universities that offer excellent programmes. Worldwide praise has been given to about one hundred French institutions for their high standards. As a student earns a master’s degree in management, they develop skills that are known around the world and can be used in a wide range of managerial positions.","France can also help you grow as a person and academically. You’ll have the chance to learn a new language, make friends from all over the world, and gain a global perspective on your studies. Plus, it looks great on your resume! So if you’re considering studying abroad, France is definitely worth considering. Bon voyage!","“Start on your academic adventure with the guidance of One"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Window Overseas Education",paragraphs:["Let us be the compass on your journey to studying in France and beyond.” Get in touch now"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"how-to-prepare-for-your-english-proficiency-test-16",title:"How to Prepare for your English Proficiency Test",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/how-to-prepare-for-your-english-proficiency-test/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/english-profiancy-test.webp",intro:"Being able to communicate in English is essential in today’s globalized society. English is used worldwide in academic discourse, business, and communication. Exams for English proficiency are necessary for a number of situations, including work, overseas travel, and higher education. These tests highlight the need for clear communication and the importance of English for corporate and global connectivity. English proficiency tests can be necessary in a number of common situations, such as when pursuing higher education overseas, looking for work, or moving abroad. People are frequently asked to prove their fluency in the language using standardized tests like the TOEFL, IELTS, PTE, Duolingo or other accredited evaluations.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["Check out the below 9 strategies to help you get ready for the English Proficiency Test:","Before you begin studying, ensure that you understand the exact format and syllabus of the English proficiency test you will be taking. This will allow you to better plan your time and focus on your studies. Most English proficiency examinations assess the four primary skills of reading, writing, speaking, and listening. Tests will differ in their exact format and content.","After you have a clear understanding of the format and content of the exam, take some time to look at your present English proficiency. This will enable you to recognize your areas of weakness and adjust your study strategy. You could assess your English proficiency in a few different ways, such as by taking practice exams, speaking with an English teacher, or doing an online or in-person placement test.","Make an achievable study schedule that works with your schedule once you’ve decided what has to be achieved. Even if you just have a little amount of time each day to study, make sure you schedule it. It’s crucial to have adequate expectations for oneself and to create realistic goals.","Consider getting an English instructor if you’re finding it difficult to study on your own. A tutor may assist you in determining your areas of weakness, putting together a study schedule, and giving you specific feedback. By offering you the chance to practice with English speakers who happen to be native speakers, tutors can also help you get better at speaking and listening.","Improving the depth of your vocabulary is one of the best ways to get better at a language. This can be accomplished in a variety of ways, such as by reading books, watching movies, or listening to podcasts in English. To assist yourself in learning new terms, you can also use vocabulary apps or flashcards.","Writing is a fantastic method to increase your vocabulary and grammar. Even if it’s only for a little while, make an effort to write in your journal every day in English. If you make any mistakes, use a grammar checker and dictionary to guide you.","Constant practice is the best technique to enhance your speaking abilities. Look for chances for conversation in English with fellow learners or native speakers. By repeating what you hear when you watch English-language TV shows and movies, you can also practice speaking the language on your own.","Grammar, vocabulary, and comprehension can all be greatly enhanced by reading. Read as much as you can in a range of genres, including magazines, novels, and nonfiction. Digital articles and blogs are also available for reading.","After spending some time studying, assess your performance with a practice exam. There are several paid and free practice exams available online, and this can help you find any areas where you need to improve.","Conclusion","Mastering English goes beyond building vocabulary; it includes refining tone, grammar, and overall comprehension. Achieving fluency allows you to converse more naturally and confidently. Learning English at home offers the advantage of progressing at your own pace, making mistakes, and trying again with resources readily available online. Ready to elevate your English skills? Connect with our expert advisors at One Window for personalized guidance and support."],bullets:["Recognize the exam’s content and requirements","Pay attention on your particular area of weakness","Build an effective study schedule","Through a tutor, prepare yourself faster!","Strengthen your vocabulary","Keep writing daily journal in English","Practice speaking regularly","Try to read as much as you can","Do a practice exam"]}]},{slug:"steps-to-select-university-17",title:"Steps to Select University",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/steps-to-select-university/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2016/06/Choose-Uni.png",intro:"The first step towards an international education is to decide which country and which universities are your top choices. This decision can be made based on various parameters. Each parameter is important in its own way and will aid you in making an informed choice after intensive research. Following are the things you need to consider before deciding which universities you want to apply to: The first step in choosing the right university is to decide what subject your interest lies in. you need to evaluate what your future goals are and which course/courses comply with meeting those goals. It is important to prioritize the course over college. This is imperative because what you study will be based on your interests and plans for your career. You cannot choose a college just because it is ranked high. You need to choose a college, which has an excellent department in your desired course. Eventually, the course will get you to your career path, not the college. Therefore, choose your course wisely and make sure your passion lies in what you choose to pursue.",sections:[{heading:"6 Steps to Choose a University",paragraphs:["After deciding on your preferred course/ subjects you wish to study, it is time to research the top universities offering that course. Prepare a list based on your choice ranking each from 1st to last. Find out everything about the university and the course details of the selected course. Consider university rankings but don’t be fooled by rankings based on other courses. Check the rankings for each university based on the subject/ course you want to take up. Look for the universities that are best in the chosen field, no matter their size or rank overall. For example, A university may be ranked lower on the world’s top universities, but maybe among the best for a certain subject. You need to make sure you consider the rankings depending on the course. If a university is the best in a certain field, it is more important to consider that how it is placed on the world charts.","You will prepare a list of your top choices of universities you wish to apply to. Now you need to evaluate each university based on internal, minor details. You should find out more about the course details, the infrastructure, the location, the faculty, and the research being done in the university. Gather all the information you need to make an informed choice. Study the details of the course, the requirements for each university, the premises/campus structure. Read about the research the university is conducting and also see if any particular thing interests you. You can also connect with the alumni of the university or the course and find out further about the course and the college. This will give you inside knowledge about the university.","The next step is to research the location of the university. If you need a city environment, then make sure you apply to universities located in cities. If you prefer a quieter and greener environment, then choose a university located in the countryside or outskirts. Research about the weather conditions in the country or city you decide to apply to. If you cannot live in certain harsh climate conditions, then make sure to eliminate those choices from your shortlisted universities. Eventually, you should be able to live comfortably wherever you go to study.","One of the most important things in choosing a college is the cost incurred. You should evaluate and examine the fees of each college and see if it is suitable for your pocket. Find out about the scholarship requirements and criteria and see if you can qualify for the same. There are various finance options available as well to get you through your education. You can consider these options and make a list of the colleges that offer your course and fit into your budget. While calculating the cost, you should not forget to include the cost of living in a particular city. This will help you know what to expect in terms of expenditure.","This step goes along with assessing the cost incurred. You should find out about your accommodation options: campus hostels or something private outside the campus. It should suit your preferences. If you cannot live with roommates then, it is preferred to live outside in private accommodation. If you don’t wish to travel every day for your classes, then you should opt for the campus hostels. The campus hostels also provide you an atmosphere full of activities and engagements. Decide what you are cut out for and make a wise choice considering your budget as well.","After considering all these factors, you will have a clear understanding of where you should go and why you made the choice. This is the best way to evaluate your options and make the correct decision about which university is best for you."],bullets:["Choose The right course for yourself / give preference to the course more than university","Research the top universities offering the subject/course of your choice/university rankings","Evaluate the shortlisted universities based on the infrastructure, the course details","Research about the demographics of the city where the university is located","Cost, Scholarships and Financial Aid:","Living options available – Campus hostels/private accommodation"]}]},{slug:"top-5-academic-success-strategies-for-study-abroad-18",title:"Top 5  Academic Success Strategies For Study Abroad",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/top-5-academic-success-strategies-for-study-abroad/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/academic-success-strategies.webp",intro:"Studying abroad offers more than just academic knowledge; it’s a pathway to developing crucial life skills and intercultural understanding. In today’s interconnected world, a global education is increasingly vital, promoting both personal and career growth. Through exposure to diverse cultures, international students gain invaluable perspectives and skills, fostering adaptability, communication, and critical thinking. However, navigating a new academic environment can be both rewarding and challenging. International students often face hurdles such as cultural adjustment, academic rigor, and social integration. Overcoming these obstacles requires strategic approaches. Preparing thoroughly before departure, seeking support services provided by universities, and actively engaging in campus life are key tactics for success. Despite the challenges, studying abroad offers numerous benefits. It’s an opportunity for intellectual and personal development, exposing students to new ideas and ways of life. Cultural immersion enhances understanding and appreciation for diversity, while networking opportunities provide a global perspective and potential career advancements.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Top 5 essential strategies",paragraphs:["A systematic approach to time management is necessary for achieving a balance between personal research and academic duties. Create an organized timetable that accommodates social events, study time, and cultural exposure. Setting achievable goals and prioritizing your work can help you stay organized and get the most out of your study abroad experience.","It is essential in order to truly discover and understand every aspect of the foreign country and fully embrace the local culture. Engage with locals and other students, take part in cultural events, and fully immerse yourself in the community. Developing ties with locals can lead to insightful conversations and a greater understanding of the history and traditions of the host nation.","Understand all the academic resources that your university has to offer. To improve your educational experience, make use of academic workshops, writing centers, and tutoring services. Seek advice from professors and advisors to effectively handle any academic obstacles and make the most of the educational opportunities available to you.","For academic success overseas, you have to adapt your study habits to fit the demands of the local system of education. Recognize the assessment criteria, research standards, and teaching strategies used in your host nation. To satisfy your professors’ expectations and facilitate an easy adjustment to the new learning environment, modify your study techniques appropriately.","Traveling abroad for academic purposes should be approached with an open mind and an ability to accept different viewpoints. Accepting diversity and cultural differences can extend your perspective and improve your educational experience. Develop flexibility to overcome obstacles and take advantage of chances for both academic and personal development.","Conclusion","Studying overseas can be a life-changing experience that provides priceless chances for both academic and personal growth. Through implementation of these productive strategies, international students can achieve academic success while simultaneously cultivating a more profound comprehension of varied cultures and viewpoints. Let your overseas academic experience serve as a springboard for a prosperous and satisfying future by approaching the trip with an open mind and a forward-thinking attitude.","Ready to start on your overseas education journey? Visit","ONE WINDOW","and tap into our expert guidance for a smooth transition. Explore endless opportunities, gain invaluable insights, and make informed decisions with our dedicated team.","One","Window Overseas Education"],bullets:["Make time management a priority :","Embrace Cultural Integration :","Make Use of the Resources on Hand :","Adjust to the Academic Environment in Your Field of Study :","Build an Open Mind and Adaptability :"]}]},{slug:"scholarship-vs-student-loan-understanding-the-pros-and-cons-19",title:"Scholarship Vs. Student Loan: Understanding The Pros And Cons",tag:"Funding",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/scholarship-vs-student-loan-understanding-the-pros-and-cons/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/scholarship-vs-student-loan.webp",intro:"Deciding to study abroad is an exciting but daunting step, especially when it comes to sorting out the finances. Many students think about options like scholarships and student loans. In this guide, we’ll look closely at these choices, talking about their advantages and disadvantages. Studying in another country can be fantastic for your education and personal development. But let’s face it, it’s expensive. That’s where scholarships and loans come in handy. They both have their good and bad sides, which we’ll go over together. Scholarships",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["A lot of people tend to be confused in understanding what scholarships are? However, it is a grant that the government, an NGO, or any other organization pays for. It can help the applicant focus on their goals more effectively and lessen the stress of financial issues. Under this, a grant or payment is given to a student to help pay for their studies, based on how well they do in their academics or in other areas of research.","PROS","By funding all or a portion of your tuition, scholarships save you and your family money by reducing your financial burden.","Academic success, extracurricular involvement, and potential are frequently taken into account when awarding scholarships. Getting a scholarship is entirely up to the university, but it recognizes your hard work and ability.","A few scholarships include networking opportunities that can help you meet important people in your profession and improve your chances of landing a job.","Scholarships, in contrast to student loans, have no repayment obligations, so you can graduate debt-free.","CONS","Competitive:","Many applicants compete fiercely for a small number of scholarships. It can be difficult to stand out among a crowd of exceptionally skilled applicants.","Strict Eligibility:","Scholarships may have strict requirements for eligibility, which may limit the number of qualified candidates by evaluating things like GPA, country of origin, or subject of study.","Restricted Coverage:","Although scholarships pay for tuition, they might not cover living expenses, travel expenses, or other ancillary charges.","Not Guaranteed:","It’s critical to keep in mind that scholarships are not assured, and getting one might be extremely challenging.","Student Loans","For studying abroad are financial products designed to help students cover the cost of education, living expenses, and other related costs while pursuing a degree outside their home country. These loans can come from various sources, including government programs, private lenders, and international organizations.","PROS","Access to Funds:","In order to pay for tuition and other expenditures without having to make an upfront payment, student loans offer instant access to funds.","Flexible Repayment:","With the support of many student loan repayment options, you can start making payments once you graduate and have a more steady job.","Possibility of Higher Education:","Due to financial limitations, student loans may make it possible to pursue higher education that may not have been possible earlier.","CONS","Accrued Interest:","Over time, interest on student loans accrues, which could result in a larger total repayment amount than the original loan.","Debt Responsibility:","After graduation, repaying student loans may result in a large debt load that limits your options and financial freedom.","Uncertainty:","It may be difficult to make timely student loan repayment payments given the state of the economy and employment prospects following graduation.","Conclusion","Selecting between student loans and scholarships is a crucial decision that might influence your academic path and beyond. Every option has pros and cons, so it’s important to carefully assess your position and objectives before deciding.","We at","One Window","are committed to giving you the direction and encouragement you need to make the right decision for your future, thanks to our skilled advisers. To confidently begin this life-changing journey, get in touch with us right now."],bullets:["Financial Relief:","Merit Recognition:","Networking Possibilities:","No Repayment Requirement:"]}]},{slug:"is-study-gap-accepted-in-the-uk-20",title:"IS STUDY GAP ACCEPTED IN THE UK",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/is-study-gap-accepted-in-the-uk/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Study-gap-accepted-in-UK.webp",intro:"If the answer to your questions is a big “yes,” then you have come to the right place. You will be thrilled to know that the UK is the top-recognized country for having the most universities that allow students to enter with a study gap. That means your dreams of studying in the UK are no longer a cause for concern.",sections:[{heading:"What’s the study gap?",paragraphs:["After completing their secondary education, a few students choose to take a break from their studies for a variety of reasons, including health, finances, short courses, or work experience. The break’s duration could be a year or more. Therefore, we refer to the period of time between a student’s previous qualification and their subsequent higher qualification as the study gap.","Come on, let’s delve deeper into the various strategies to address the challenges associated with a study gap:","When planning to study abroad, prospective students often worry about whether universities will accept a study gap. In the UK, study gaps are typically accepted, although the specifics can vary by institution and course.","Reasons for study gaps include:","Gaining work experience","Dealing with personal issues","Traveling"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Preparing for further studies",paragraphs:["While some universities and highly competitive programs may scrutinize the reasons for the gap more closely, many institutions adopt a flexible approach and evaluate applications holistically. Providing a clear and honest explanation for the study gap, demonstrating how the time was used productively, and including relevant documentation can greatly enhance the chances of acceptance. As each university may have different policies, thorough research and preparation are essential for presenting a strong application."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What is the acceptable period for UK study gaps?",paragraphs:["The UK is well known for always being a very supportive and welcoming country for international students who want to pursue their higher education. The UK accepts study gaps for a specific number of years. The UK accepts different time periods for study gaps when pursuing a bachelor’s degree and a postgraduate degree.","Bachelor’s Degree: Students with a maximum study gap of 2 years can apply to any university in the UK. Certain universities allow students who have a 3-year study gap to pursue their education despite the 3-year study gap. If you have a fundamental cause for the year gap, many UK universities will accept your application.","Postgraduate Degree: Students who have a five-year study gap after their bachelor’s degree can apply to universities in the UK. If your year gap does not exceed 5 years, the likelihood of rejection is relatively low. For postgraduate aspirants, if your study gap is more than 10–15 years, there is no need to worry; if you have all the valid reasons and documents of proof to justify your gap, the universities will still welcome you.","Students can fill in the huge gap by pursuing a diploma or any pre-masters course, which can act as a bridge to study in the UK more easily.","There are 4 different methods to bridge the study gap:","The students who have exceeded the study-year time period can bridge the year with different methods. Bridging the study gap can make your application process much easier. You should clearly explain the various methods to the immigration officer and the university to which you have applied to ensure a smoother process.","Work experience:","Gaining work experience during your study gap can significantly enhance your application to UK universities. Make sure to have all your work experience certificates and documents to make your application process easier.","Short Courses:","Many students start doing short courses after their secondary education, which is again an advantage for applicants with a study gap. To enter the gates of UK universities, you must provide a certificate of the short courses you completed while applying to the universities.","Health Issues:","These are students who have medical conditions that prevent them from taking a break from their further studies. There is no need to worry about the study gap while applying to UK universities for health-related reasons. If you provide all the necessary documents and health certificates related to your treatment, your current health condition, and a fit-to-certificate, it will make your application process more understandable, especially when considering your study gap.","Financial issues:","Students facing financial crises and family issues are also eligible for the study gap visa in the UK. To ensure consideration, the applicant must clearly explain the issues to the immigration officer.","The top 10 universities allow study gaps:","Many universities in the UK allow for study gaps. Here is a list of the few universities that do so:","1.","Cranfield University","2.","Coventry University","3.","Brunel University","4.","King’s University, London","5.","University Of Greenwich","6.","Bangor University","7.","The University Of Bradford","8.","The University Of West London","9.","Cardiff Metropolis University","10.","Bath Spa University","CONCLUSION:","A study gap should not deter you from pursuing your dreams of studying in the UK. The country’s inclusive and flexible approach to education ensures that students from diverse backgrounds, including those with study gaps, are welcomed with open arms. By leveraging work experience, completing short courses, or providing valid reasons and documentation for your break, you can bridge the gap effectively. The UK offers numerous opportunities to integrate your unique experiences into a successful academic journey. With a variety of supportive universities ready to accommodate your circumstances, your aspirations of higher education in the UK are within reach. So, gather your documents, articulate your journey, and take the leap towards an enriching educational experience in one of the world’s most esteemed educational destinations.","If you have a genuine desire to study in the UK, you need to make sure that you are using your study properly and providing strong justification for it. Do contact us at"],bullets:[]},{heading:"One Window Overseas Education",paragraphs:[", where we will provide you with personal assistance and a guarantee for a simple visa application process.","Also read:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"IS STUDY GAP ACCEPTABLE IN CANADA – onewindow",paragraphs:["Is studying abroad in UK worth it for Indian students? – onewindow"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"07-tips-to-make-friends-while-you-study-abroad-21",title:"07 Tips to Make Friends While You Study Abroad",tag:"Life abroad",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/07-tips-to-make-friends-while-you-study-abroad/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Thumbnails-for-Website-1.webp",intro:"While many of you may be thinking about studying abroad, if you are a person who does not like to socialize, then here are some tips for you that can help you become a better individual. It is an old saying, “Your character can be defined by looking at your friend circle!”. So it is really important for you to understand that socializing is a must for your career growth, especially when you are in a new place, but in the right way. Thus, making friends is not just a social activity; it’s a crucial part of personal and career growth. Are you ready to socialize and make lasting bonds while you are on your journey to advance your career? Then you must surely read these seven useful tips to make friends, which not only help you open up but also teach you a lot more things.",sections:[{heading:"07 Tips to Make Friends While You Study Abroad",paragraphs:["Tip 01: Follow your Hobby:","By engaging in activities you love, you’ll be more likely to meet people who share your enthusiasm. Take up interests that interest you to meet like-minded people. A lifetime of friendships is built on common interests, whether they be in sports, music, the arts, cooking, or travel. “Every friend symbolizes a world within of us, one that may not have been born until they arrive, and one that is only created by this meeting.” Anaïs Nin","Tip 02: Join Local Clubs or Groups:","Entering local clubs or groups focused on your interests is the next step after identifying what they are. You can look for these groups online, in the local paper, or by asking people in the area for suggestions. Doing activities with a group will let you meet people who like the same things you do, which will make it easier to start talking and make friends. Use platforms like Meetup, which host groups for everything from hiking to tech meetups, to find clubs that share your hobbies. Being in a club lets you meet new people on a regular basis, which may eventually lead to friendships.","Tip 03: Social Events and Clubs:","Attending social events and festivals is another great way to meet new people and make friends abroad. Many cities and towns have regular events, such as street fairs, concerts, and cultural celebrations. These events are perfect opportunities to immerse yourself in local culture and meet like-minded individuals. You can find information about upcoming events through local newspapers, online event calendars, or by asking locals for recommendations. Don’t be afraid to step out of your comfort zone and try new experiences – this is an excellent way to meet people with similar interests.","Platforms like","Eventbrite","can guide you to exciting events around you.","Tip 04: Volunteer for Local Organizations:","Volunteering for local organizations is a fantastic way to make friends abroad while also giving back to the community. By volunteering, you’ll have the chance to work alongside others who share your desire to make a positive impact. This not only provides opportunities for friendship but also allows you to learn more about the local culture and issues facing the community.","Tip 05: Practice Your Language Skills:","When making friends abroad, you must also learn the local language. Being completely engaged in the language shows your commitment to comprehending and appreciating the local way of life in addition to improving your communication. To become better, take classes, look for a language exchange partner, or watch regional TV series and movies. It will be simpler for you to make new acquaintances and develop closer friendships the more at ease you are with the language. Using apps like","Tandem","and","HelloTalk",", you may learn and teach languages in a conversational environment. Take part in language exchanges.","Tip 06: Be Open-Minded:","One of the most important aspects of making friends abroad is being open-minded and embracing cultural differences. Each country has its own customs, traditions, and ways of doing things, and it’s essential to approach these differences with curiosity and respect. By showing an interest in learning about the local culture and values, you’ll demonstrate that you’re genuinely invested in building meaningful connections.","Tip 07: Being Socially Active:","Use Social Media and Online Platforms In today’s digital age, social media and online platforms can be powerful tools for connecting with new people abroad. Join Facebook groups, follow local Instagram accounts, or use apps like Meetup to find events and groups that interest you. These platforms can help you discover hidden gems in your area and put you in touch with like-minded individuals who are also looking to make connections.","Conclusion:","A great way to broaden your horizons and learn more about other cultures is by making acquaintances overseas. You may improve your chances of meeting people who share your interests and creating enduring connections by doing things you love, volunteering, attending local events and groups, being open-minded, using social media, and being patient and persistent. Making friends overseas may be a genuinely life-changing experience, so don’t be scared to push outside of your comfort zone and enjoy the experience. For more studying abroad tips and tricks, reach out to us today.","Ready to turn strangers into friends and make your study abroad experience unforgettable? Here are some super cool tips on making friends in a new country. Whether you’re hitting up local hotspots, joining clubs that spark your interest, or simply chatting with classmates, there’s a whole world of friendship opportunities waiting for you. Don’t miss out; check out our guide at","One Window and start building your global network today. Let’s make your time abroad the adventure of a lifetime!"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"summer-programs-short-term-opportunities-22",title:"Summer Programs & Short-Term Opportunities",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/summer-programs-short-term-opportunities/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Summer-Programs.webp",intro:"Summer study abroad programs often cover more than one country; you will certainly not be lacking in experience due to the restricted schedule. Summer study abroad programs are often bustling with students, even though many opt to go during the school year. You may easily fit schoolwork into your summer schedule because the programs are available for a limited period of time, and many of them provide short-term chances. Here are the top 3 countries with summer programs:",sections:[{heading:"(Exploring Destinations, Volunteer Programs)",paragraphs:["International students can achieve their academic and personal goals by participating in a summer program at an institution in the US. Taking additional classes during summer school in the US gives enrolled students the best chance of finishing their degree requirements. This might be useful for people with a bachelor’s degree who are interested in living on a college campus in the United States.","Check out the below 5 summer programs in united states :"],bullets:["USA:"]},{heading:"The summer school program at Yale University is being debated",paragraphs:["English for academic purposes at George Washington University."],bullets:[]},{heading:"English as a Second Language (ESL): Manhattan University",paragraphs:["Summer schools in the USA are often held between May and August and can last two to twelve weeks.","Studying abroad in Canada and participating in foreign summer school programs can offer students an exciting chance to obtain a top-notch education. Students learning French and English during summer programs in Canada can also experience life abroad.","Check out the below 5 summer programs in Canada:"],bullets:["Canada:"]},{heading:"Human Psychology, Summer Business Courses, and Language Courses – Bishop’s University ( 1 year )",paragraphs:["Financial Accounting-I, Medical Office Administration, Pharmacy Assistant-CIMT College ( 5 months)","The United Kingdom stands out as a leading nation in providing excellent education, featuring a wider array of innovative courses than other countries, including short term courses. Numerous students opt for short-term courses or summer schools to study in the UK, seeking first-hand experience of the UK’s renowned education system.","Check out the below 5 summer programs in UK:"],bullets:["UNITED KINGDOM:"]},{heading:"Beginners in a specific language: University of Westminster ( 2 months)",paragraphs:["Top 5 benefits for international students who are planning summer programs are:","Earn extra credits in less time:","Studying abroad in the summer can help you stand out from the competition in addition to being an interesting and enjoyable method to get your degree. During the summer semester, gaining extra credits can allow you to enroll in interesting classes outside of your major or go through your course more quickly.","Receive more attention in smaller classes:","Smaller class sizes provide opportunities for hands-on learning and interactions between students and teachers, as opposed to enormous lecture halls where you’re just a body in a seat.Perhaps more excitingly, you could study abroad under the guidance of a professor who is conducting a special kind of research in the field—something you would never be able to do on campus or in a fall or spring program.","Get a chance to meet diverse student groups:","Summer programs attract a larger student body than more “traditional” fall or spring programs because they are open to students worldwide. Being unrestricted by the academic year or college credit modules, summer programs also offer the freedom to concentrate on alternative subjects. There are numerous ways to do this.","Spend less money than last semester:","Some students think studying abroad is beyond their reach due to the cost but one of the perks of going overseas during the summer is that you can knock out multiple credits under one program fee.","Overcome your travel anxiety with a shorter program:","Study abroad programs in the summer could be overwhelming for first-time visitors who are unsure about their ability to adjust to the new environment but there are various programs to fit your needs, ranging in duration from a week to the whole summer.","Conclusion:","Your dreams of learning abroad and spending the summer abroad can come true thanks to programs that connect people from all over the world. It can be scary to study abroad, so make sure you have all the things you need if you’ve never been abroad before. In this case, short-term classes might be helpful.","You will be able to experience what it’s like to study abroad without having to carry too much. If you receive acceptance, don’t pass up the opportunity to study abroad for a brief period. Get ready for an exciting adventure! Even though it’s only for a short time, there will be a lot of work to do and beautiful sights to see. If you are still anxious or feeling stuck on how to move forward,you can always reach out to our experts at","One Window","for a free counseling session."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"5-easy-steps-to-apply-for-admission-for-abroad-education-23",title:"5 Easy steps to Apply for ADMISSION For Abroad Education",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/5-easy-steps-to-apply-for-admission-for-abroad-education/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/onewindow-premium-plus-serv.png",intro:"Studying abroad is a dream for students who want to pursue their education in a different country, exploring varied cultures, learning new languages, and broaden their horizons. However, if you are not familiar with the requirements and procedures applying for admission to a foreign university can be a daunting task. That is why we have prepared this guide to help you with the 5 easy steps to apply for admission for abroad education.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["Irrespective of your choice to study in the USA, UK, AUSTRALIA, or any other country, these steps will help you plan for your journey and achieve your goals."],bullets:[]},{heading:"1st STEP: Research about the place you are going to visit",paragraphs:["The 1st Step is as astonishing as you have only dreamt of it, but it is the most important one.","The first step in the application process is to research well about the courses, universities and locations that you are interested to join.","You can always reach out to our expert counsellors, for various aspects of studying abroad, such as the academic quality, tuition fees, scholarships, visa requirements, living expenses, culture and lifestyle of the destination you choose."],bullets:[]},{heading:"2nd Step: Application Process",paragraphs:["Once you have narrowed down your choices, and you have taken your first leap. After narrowing down your options and starting the journey, it’s important to connect with an experienced counsellor who can help you through the application process.","A counsellor can help you with choosing the right course and university for your:","Goals","Preparing your documents","Filling out the application forms","Writing your SOP’s and LOR","Taking the required tests and","Applying for your student visa.","You can book a free appointment with one of our highly skilled “Application and Counselling Team” who are experts in study abroad counselling and have first-hand experience for guiding students to achieve their right path."],bullets:[]},{heading:"3rd Step: Creating an Application",paragraphs:["The next step is creating a suitable application to universities that you have shortlisted.","You have to submit various documents along with your application form.","The documents such as:","your academic transcripts","Test scores,","CV or resume,","Essays and"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Portfolio (if applicable)",paragraphs:["Are needed to be submitted along with your application.","The documents may vary depending on the country, university and course you are applying for, so make sure you check the specific requirements on the university’s website or take updates with your counsellor.","*Please note that You will also need to pay an application fee for each university you apply to. This may also vary on each and every university you choose to apply."],bullets:[]},{heading:"4th Step: Wait for the Reply",paragraphs:["After submitting your application, you will have to wait for the university’s response. This may take from a few weeks to a few months depending on the university and the intake season.","If your application is successful, you will receive a letter of offer and an acceptance form from the university.","You need to read the offer letter carefully and check for conditions that may apply,","Such as","Paying a deposit or","Meeting certain academic criteria.","You will also need to sign and return the acceptance form to confirm your enrolment."],bullets:[]},{heading:"5th Step: Apply for your VISA",paragraphs:["The final step is to apply for your student visa. This is a very important and sensitive part of your study abroad journey.","As it determines whether you can enter and stay in your chosen country legally. You will need to follow the visa application process and requirements of the country you are going to study in.","You will also need to provide various documents such as:","Your passport","Offer letter","Financial proof","Health insurance and","Medical tests.","Our counsellor can assist you with preparing and submitting your visa application and advise you on how to deal with any issues that may arise.","In conclusion of this blog, we believe to see an opportunity of how Studying abroad can be a life-changing experience that can open up many opportunities for your personal and professional growth. If you are ready to take the next step towards your study abroad dream, contact us today and we will help you make it happen!","For Further information on studying abroad, please visit our website here or fill out this form to get in touch with one of our counsellors.","We look forward to hearing from you soon!","HAPPY STUDYING!"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"2023-guide-to-apply-for-wsu-digital-badge-program-latest-guide-2023-24",title:"2023 Guide to apply for WSU:Digital Badge Program [Latest Guide 2023]",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/2023-guide-to-apply-for-wsudigital-badge-program-latest-guide-2023/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/2023-guide-to-apply-for-WSU.png",intro:"Are you looking for a way to enhance your overseas education experience and gain valuable skills that can boost your career prospects? If so, then you must not leave this opportunity provided by Wichita State University (WSU), which is a public research university located in Wichita, Kansas, USA. Whether you are a working professional or a student wanting to acquire multiple certificates/degrees, WSU has come up with their",sections:[{heading:"“DIGITAL BADGE PROGRAM”",paragraphs:[". This can be a benefit to add to your portfolio, and this is a not-to-miss opportunity for you who want to explore international education system by being at your own place.","Let’s get to know more about this program.","WHAT IS A DIGITAL","BADGE PROGRAM?","A badge program is a type of online learning that allows you to earn digital badges upon completion of certain tasks. A digital badge is a picture of your accomplishment.","This program can help you enhance your overseas education experience by providing opportunities to learn:","New skills","Explore different cultures","Develop your personal growth and"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What are the benefits of a DIGITAL BADGE PROGRAM?",paragraphs:["A badge program has many benefits for international students, such as:","Flexibility:","You can complete the badge courses online and at your own pace.","Affordability:","The badge courses are affordable and cost-effective.","Quality:","The badge courses are taught by experienced faculty who are subject matter experts in their fields.","Recognition:","The badge courses are part of your permanent academic record and will appear on your official transcript.","Relevance:","You can choose from a variety of badges that suit your interests and career goals, and which are relevant to your interest of learning."],bullets:[]},{heading:"WHAT ARE THE BADGE PROGRAMS OFFERED BY WICHITA STATE UNIVERSITY?",paragraphs:["WSU offers over 70 badge courses in various disciplines, such as:","ENGINEERING BADGES:","These badges include topics listed below:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Computer Programming",paragraphs:["Composite Structural Engineering Technology"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Optimization and root finding",paragraphs:["Focusing on your development of technical skills and your knowledge in engineering.","BUSINESS BADGE:","These badges cover topics such as:","Professional writing","Presenting online","Professional correspondence etc.","Helping in improving your communication skills and business acumen.","HEALTHCARE BADGES:","Topics covered by these badges are mentioned below:","Medical terminology","Anatomy and physiology of lactation"],bullets:[]},{heading:"ASL in healthcare settings",paragraphs:["And many more courses to choose from.","BEHAVIORAL SCIENCES BADGES:","These badges include topics such as:","Introduction to Criminal Justice"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Achieving cultural competency",paragraphs:["NAAD specialty clinical certification and much more.","And much more to boost your interpersonal skills and communication skills.","GENERAL WORKFORCE SKILLS BADGES:","The covered topics in this badge are:","Age at work","Origin at work","CMFGT/CSET hands-on laboratory and many more.","These badges can be helping you develop your personal growth, and cultural awareness skills."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How do I enroll in a DIGITAL BADGE PROGRAM?",paragraphs:["To enroll in a badge program at WSU, you need to follow these steps:","Find the badge or badges you are interested in from the badge catalogue."],bullets:[]},{heading:"To enrol and apply, follow the enrollment procedures",paragraphs:["Add the badge to your course schedule and get going.","*Note: All the work must be completed by the last day of the semester."],bullets:[]},{heading:"HOW CAN ONE WINDOW OVERSEAS EDUCATION COMPANY HELP YOU?",paragraphs:["If you are planning to study abroad at WSU or any other university around the world, you might need some guidance and assistance from experts who have been there and have experience.","This is where","ONE WINDOW OVERSEAS EDUCATION","comes in.","One Window","is one of the leading overseas education companies in this field, we offer one-stop solutions for all your overseas education needs.","ONE WINDOW","can help you with:","Best fit university","Scholarship assistance","Application documentation","Education loan assistance","Visa interview coaching","Conclusion:","A badge program is a great way to enhance your overseas education experience and gain valuable skills that can boost your career prospects.","WSU offers a wide range of badge programs that suit your interests and goals. You can enroll for free in these programs online and at your own pace.","Dreaming of studying abroad?","ONE WINDOW","is your ticket. Our experienced team will be your guiding stars, simplifying your journey. Unveil more on our website. Get in touch with us today to start your learning adventure.","!! HAPPY STUDYING !!"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"how-to-deal-with-cultural-shock-25",title:"HOW TO DEAL WITH CULTURAL SHOCK",tag:"Life abroad",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/how-to-deal-with-cultural-shock-2/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/gre-gmat.png",intro:"Education abroad has always been a popular option for young individuals. Given the quality of education and living, work-options, the desire for a new experience, research prospects, etc., many students choose overseas education to improve their future. The education and lifestyle in India and overseas are vastly different, but the most striking distinction is the culture. Regarding their culture and beliefs, Indians are exceedingly rigorous and specific. Even if the western culture has already begun to get a foothold in India, it nevertheless adheres to the traditions and beliefs passed down from its forefathers. Indians have adapted to our culture, environment, language, and people. Moving to a place with an entirely different culture, language, and people can cause a major “culture shock.”",sections:[{heading:"What is culture shock?",paragraphs:["The term “culture shock” often refers to the feeling of confusion or disorientation one has upon encountering a culture or environment with which one is unfamiliar. This is something that almost all overseas students encounter, and as such, it is extremely frequent. The crucial step is to recognise and address it. There is nothing wrong with requiring a little more time to acclimatise to the new environment, as many students respond to culture shock in their own unique ways. Culture shock may help you adapt to a new environment in some ways.","Factors causing cultural shock?","Soon enough, you become acutely aware of how drastically your lifestyle has changed. You may feel completely overwhelmed by your newfound freedom. Disruptions to your daily routine can make it hard to know what to do in order to survive. Your life is something you have complete control over, so it’s vital that you act responsibly and make your own choices. To give just a few examples, if you don’t do the dishes, wash the clothes, shop for food, or do the tasks at hand, no one else will.","Even if you have a solid command of the language spoken in your study nation, interacting with the locals will be a whole new experience. Local slang may be a component of the country’s everyday vernacular, to which you will become accustomed with time. For instance, Canadians refer to $1 as a loonie and $2 as a toonie, and a Double-Double is one of the most popular coffees here.","Each nation’s educational system is unique in its organisation, methods of instruction, grading scales, required coursework, due dates, awarding of financial aid, etc. There can be an overwhelming quantity of data to process and familiarise oneself with. Do your best not to let all this new information overwhelm you; instead, take it leisurely and attempt to comprehend it piece by piece so you can easily adapt to it.","Each nation’s educational system is unique in its organisation, methods of instruction, grading scales, required coursework, due dates, awarding of financial aid, etc. There can be an overwhelming quantity of data to process and familiarise oneself with. Do your best not to let all this new information overwhelm you; instead, take it leisurely and attempt to comprehend it piece by piece so you can easily adapt to it.","Every culture, including your new setting, has some unwritten but widely accepted rules. These laws directly affect how the society runs on a daily basis. For instance, it’s customary to apologise or say “thank you” far too frequently in Canada, even for the most insignificant of things. If not, even if it wasn’t your intention, you might be seen as disrespectful. In Australia, even when meeting for the first time, a stranger may address you as mate.","It’s natural to crave for familiar foods and the comforts of home. One solution is to seek out stores and eating establishments that stock items you enjoy. If you’re feeling homesick, learning to cook and hosting people can help. What’s even better is to go on the phone and make those calls back home.","Also read how to overcome homesickness.","Common symptoms of Homesickness","Early on, when you are still in the process of adjusting to your new environment, it is pretty usual to encounter these symptoms. The severity may vary according on the individual.","Feeling homesick is normal. It is typical to miss one’s native country and the people who reside there. It also occurs if you are unwilling to adapt to your new surroundings and attempt to maintain as much of your previous habits as possible. It is best to adapt and allow oneself to find and create new memories.","It is typical to have an irregular or distinct sleep pattern (earlier due to jet lag, later on due to difference in time zone). However, this may be prolonged due to protracted contemplation, stress, lack of confidence, or other similar factors.","While some students like to immediately begin networking, meet new acquaintances, and tour the city, others would rather remain alone and avoid all public places. In your early years, it is normal to lack the motivation to leave the house, but it is not advisable to maintain this disposition for an extended period of time. It is essential to cultivate a social group that keeps you busy.","Some students have difficulty performing academically or professionally due to their incapacity to manage stress. Students may struggle to comprehend the new educational structure. It is essential to determine the source of this suffering and address it.","Some students find it relatively easy to assimilate into the new culture, whereas others attempt to adapt their personalities to the norms of the new community. This could result in noticeable personality changes. For instance, altering one’s accent to seem more professional, a sudden change in attire, an inability to control spending, etc. Allowing these changes to emerge organically is preferable to forcing them."],bullets:["A different way of living:","Language:","Academics:","Weather conditions:","New societal rules:","Missing home or food :","Anxiety, depression, loneliness:","Homesickness:","Disturbed sleep pattern:","Remoteness or Isolation:","Decreased productivity:","Poor time management: As an international student, it is essential to manage time effectively. If you miss a certain deadline for a project, for instance, you may lose significant grades. Occasionally, the lack of a timetable (either too much to accomplish or too much spare time) might throw off your schedule. Prioritizing your activities and striking a balance between work and leisure time is beneficial.","Drastic personality change:"]},{heading:"Overcoming cultural shock",paragraphs:["Culture shock is manageable if you remain cool and eventually embrace the differences. Here are some excellent strategies to assist you:","Be aware that experiencing culture shock is quite common for any and all overseas students. You are not the only one going through this. Just give yourself some time to acclimatize to the new surroundings and don’t rush the process.","Do not attempt to deal with it on your own. It is in your best interest to maintain connections with family and friends who can assist you in remaining motivated. Maintain an open mind and investigate this fresh perspective on the situation.","You are going to be amazed by the quantity of social and cultural events that are organised on campus and in the surrounding area. Make sure you take part in them on a consistent basis. Volunteer your time if you have the opportunity. It is a wonderful opportunity for learning, and it might even provide a wonderful platform for networking.","Check the limitations of your visa and try to find something that you can do part-time if at all possible. You will be able to make better use of your time and also earn some additional money for yourself if you do this. It goes without saying that this will count as useful experience in the event that you apply for jobs in the future.","Although it may take some time, you should make an effort to participate in a wide variety of social contacts. Building connections with other people and gaining an understanding of the issues they face will be facilitated by this. You might even have the opportunity to go out together and discover new restaurants or sites in the city.","You should keep up with the interests you had before you arrived here, such as reading, gardening, cooking, or working out, among other things. The majority of universities also offer a variety of hobby clubs where you can participate based on the activities that most interest you. In that case, why don’t you try making your own?","If you are in difficulty, do not hesitate to seek your advisers, counsellors, or instructors. Most colleges have facilitators who are well-trained to assist students in such situations. Feel free to contact them if necessary. Taking on such challenges on your own could be more detrimental to your mental health than you realise.","Remember that culture shock is an extremely normal reaction when travelling to a new setting, affecting the majority of international students, so you are not alone. It is manageable with patience and gradual adaptation to the new culture. Utilize it as a springboard to discover new chances and become more adaptable.","If you require any sort of assistance, please contact One window. We will gladly listen to you and help you out."],bullets:["Acceptance :","Keep connected:","Attend cultural events :","Find work:","Socialize :","Go for your hobbies :","Take professional help if needed:"]}]},{slug:"will-backlogs-affect-your-study-abroad-dream-26",title:"WILL BACKLOGS AFFECT YOUR STUDY ABROAD DREAM",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/will-backlogs-affect-your-study-abroad-dream/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/backlogs.png",intro:"One of the reasons students stop themselves from studying abroad is because of their backlogs. Although universities abroad accept students with strong academic credentials, some do accept international students with a history of backlogs. A backlog doesn’t stop your dream of studying abroad. Universities abroad are aware of the fact that there can be various reasons for which a student may have a backlog at their previous institution. What are backlogs? A backlog is an exam that you didn’t pass the first time you took it. This could be because you weren’t prepared enough or because you couldn’t go to class for some reason. To get into any international programme, you need to make sure you don’t have any unfinished work from the past.",sections:[{heading:"What is a backlog certificate?",paragraphs:["It’s a certificate that your school gives you with information about your missed classes. If you don’t have any backlogs, your school won’t treat you the same. However, even if you don’t have any backlogs, your international school may still ask for a transcript to verify your academic record."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How are backlogs counted?",paragraphs:["Different institutions check backlogs differently. While some universities may consider the number of subject backlogs, others may count your number of attempts to clear a failed subject.","To make sure we understand, let’s put them into two groups:","Countries such as the United Kingdom and the United States consider failed subjects to be backlogs regardless of how many attempts you made to clear them.","For example, if you fail in one subject but attempt it twice or thrice during different semesters to clear that subject, then the number of backlogs will be counted as one and the number of attempts, i.e., three or two, will not matter.","Australia and other nations classify the number of attempts as “backlogs.” For example, if you fail a subject but try to pass it three times over the course of separate semesters, the number of backlogs will be counted as three as the number of attempts is equal to the number of backlogs. These countries evaluate your commitment to that subject or education based on the number of attempts.","Top 6 countries that accept backlogs:","Some universities in Canada accept students with up to five backlogs and a minimum of 70% in bachelor’s degrees, whereas other universities accept application forms from students with up to seven or eight backlogs and a minimum of 65% and above in bachelor’s degrees.","Students who have more than 10 or 12 backlogs can apply for PG diplomas in Canada. Students with a higher number of backlogs are granted admissions on a case-by-case basis. Reach out to our counsellors if you are having trouble with a backlog history and want to study in Canada; they will be able to point you in the appropriate direction.","All private universities in Australia accept not more than 7 or 8 backlogs, while some universities accept a maximum of 2 to 3 backlogs. Just in case you opt for public institutions instead of private ones, you should have less than or equal to 8 backlogs.","In New Zealand, institutions accept applications from students with a maximum of 12 to 15 backlogs, and universities evaluate applications from students with fewer than five backlogs.","For students with backlogs who want to study in New Zealand, a strong IELTS score is essential. You may be able to explain away your academic backlogs if you have an IELTS score of 6.5 and enough research projects or extracurricular activities indicated in your essay.","Institutions in the UK don’t mind if a student has 15 backlogs, but they do need a good score on an English-language test, like an overall band of 6 on the IELTS. Even though there may not be specific requirements for backlog acceptance, you must have a good IELTS score to get in.","Most universities inAmericaa prefer students with zerobacklogs,s but in some exceptional cases, they might accept a maximum offive5 backlogs provided you have a good GRE score between 31 and -325. Also, when there are 10 to 12 backlogs, the university decides who gets in based on what they think is best.","Most Irish universities will accept students with up to 4 or 5 backlogs, but some will only accept students with 2 or 3 backlogs.","Note that the number of backlogs might not be a matter of concern at colleges in Ireland.","In general, your backlogs don’t affect the visa processing as long as your university has accepted your application and granted confirmation of admission.","Don’t be concerned; instead, fill out the application and contact us at one window. Our experienced international education specialists will be more than happy to guide you through the entire process."],bullets:["The number of failed subjects is equivalent to the number of backlogs.","The number of attempts to pass failed subjects is equivalent to the number of backlogs.","Canada:","Australia:-","New Zealand:-","The UK :-","The US:-","Ireland:-"]}]},{slug:"top-mba-college-27",title:"TOP MBA COLLEGE",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/top-mba-college/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/mba.png",intro:"TOP MBA COLLEGE’S IN ABROAD. WHAT IS MBA? MBA stands for Master of Business Administration. MBA degree is a postgraduate level program which will bring you global opportunities in your career. A MBA curriculum will help you get a strong foundation in business fundamentals viz finance, marketing, strategy, business ethics, accounting , managerial skills, and human resources.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["WHY PURSUE AN MBA FROM ABROAD?","having an MBA degree from abroad can get you into multinational and globally owned companies. More than just the degree you get to gain industry knowledge , you get to experience the business lifestyle. You’ll grow your professional network, and boost your career and salary prospects.","well who doesn’t love a good income ? this is one of the most important reasons to pursue MBA from recognized B-school of abroad. The package is higher than that of an MBA graduate of an Indian B-school.","studying abroad will help you explore many different things and industries in the market. You will also be exposed to various opportunities to network with students from different backgrounds, teaching professionals, potential individuals and other people who have years of experience in their respective field.","MBA isn’t just a basic college-lectures-home degree. More than the studying part it gives you real life experiences and it boost your skills and knowledge in a way that you start seeing things in a different way. It clears your vision and also gives a headstart to your personality."],bullets:["Career opportunities :-","Attractive job package:-","Strong network opportunities:-","Skills and knowledge enhancement:-"]},{heading:"EXAMS YOU REQUIRE TO STUDY MBA :-",paragraphs:["You will be required to clear either GRE or GMAT with a good score to study MBA in any international university. While GRE and GMAT can be attempted to a max of 5 times in one year . GRE and GMAT tests a student’s ability in verbal,quantitative,integrated reasoning and analytical writing."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Check this :- (our gre gmat blog) for better understaning",paragraphs:["An IELTS score of 6.0 to 6.5, is required to prove your English proficiency in the English language, and it is mandatory in all study destinations abroad.","Also read :- (our ielts and toefl blog )"],bullets:[]},{heading:"TOP MBA COLLEGES ABROAD",paragraphs:["USA leads the world in many top B-school. An American MBA degree dominates most of the rankings in the world. But , there are many different MBA b-school in other countries with top rankings and with different formats and costs.","Keep in mind that MBA rankings fluctuate every year. Here listed are some top MBA program that consistently get top featured by prominent MBA rankings.","UNITED STATES:-","It is a birth place of MBA degree . A US MBA degree have a duration of 2 years. They tend to accept young candidates as compared to other countries. To pursue MBA from US you would be required to have minimum of 3-5 years of experience in the field.","Cost of MBA degree in US:- $10,000-$35,000 (approx.)","TOP COLLEGES:-","EUROPE:-","In Europe the MBA degree is 1 year. This one year MBA degree program is more intense and suited for experience professionals with slight higher work experience average of atleast 6-8 years of experience. The advantage of 1 year MBA program is that it would include low cost which includes- tuition fees,living expenses etc. Europe is a good choice for MBA if you want it budget friendly and less time consuming compared to other countries.","Cost of MBA Degree in Europe :- EUR 35,800 (approx)","TOP COLLEGES:-","UNITED KINGDOM","The united kingdom has some of the oldest universities. UK is bit expensive but no doubt when it comes to quality of education or packages. The multiculture society and London being the economic and financial centres of the world, make UK attractive and quite the option for MBA. In most universities it is necessary to have three years of full-time managerial work experience.","Cost of MBA degree in UK:- £5,000- £30,000 (approx.)","TOP COLLEGES:-","CANADA","It became the most popular destination for international students. Mainly, because of the amazing opportunities to transition from studying to legal working status. Canada has multiculture expose and good living standards. It affordable education for students with world class resources.","Cost of MBA degree in Canada:- CAD 30,000 – CAD 42,000 (approx.)","TOP COLLEGES:-","AUSTRALIA","Australia started gaining ground as most desirable MBA destinations. They are pretty arranging for scholarships for international students. An MBA degree from Australia can provide exposure to rich,multi-culture experience. Along with opportunity to train in competitive world class environment. The cost of MBA Programs In Australia is low compared to other leading programs in different countries but the living costs are quite high.","Cost of MBA degree in Australia :- AUD 39,000- AUD 90,000 (approx.)","TOP MBA COLLEGES"],bullets:["Stanford GSB","Wharton","Harvard HBS","Columbia business school","Booth (Chicago)","Kellogg(Northwestern)","Sloan (MIT)","Berkeley Haas (California)","Yale","Tuck(Dartmouth)","Stern (NYU)","Ross(Michigan)","Anderson(UCLA)","McDonough(Georgetown)","Hough(Florida)","Questrom(Boston)","Scheller(Georgia tech)","Foster(Washington)","Kenan-Flagler(North Carolina)","Kelly(Indiana)","INSEAD – France","IE Business school – Spain","IESE Business school – Spain","ESADE – Spain","HEC Paris – France","IMD – Switzerland","SDA Bocconi – Italy","Rotterdam (Erasmus) – Netherlands","Mannheim – Germany","ESMT(Berlin) – Germany","EDHEC – Germany","Copenhagen Business school – Denmark","ESSEC – France","Vlerick – Belgium","Judge (Cambridge)","London Business School","Alliance Manchester","Said (Oxford)","Cass (City University)","Lancaster","Warwick","Imperial college","Cranfield","The university of Edinburg(Edinburg)","University of Toronto- Rotman school of management","Western university – Ivey Business school","Queen’s university Stephen J.R Smith school of business","Schulich school of business , York university","HEC Montreal","McGill University – Desautels Faculty of Management","University of British Columbia – Sauder school of business","University of Ottawa – Ottawa","Macquarie","Queensland business school","Melbourne business school","AGSM (UNSW)","UNSW Sydney (Kensington)","The University of Western Australia ( Perth)"]}]},{slug:"top-13-most-instagrammable-places-to-visit-in-london-28",title:"TOP 13 MOST INSTAGRAMMABLE PLACES TO VISIT IN LONDON",tag:"Life abroad",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/top-13-most-instagrammable-places-to-visit-in-london/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/London.png",intro:"London is the capital city of England. It is one of the most desired locations for tourists and travellers worldwide. Every street and alley in London is designed with care to reflect the city’s centuries-long history. In London, there are so many magnificent attractions for people of all ages to visit. It has it’s own royalty and living standards. It is one among the world’s most beautiful spots to explore. If you enjoy taking pictures and have a knack for finding the perfect shot, You should travel to London. However, London is enormous, and you will tyre easily walking from one end of the city to the other while you search for the ideal spot. As such, we’ve made a list of 15 unique and hidden locations just for you. Address:",sections:[{heading:"TOP 13 MOST INSTAGRAMMABLE PLACES TO VISIT IN LONDON",paragraphs:[],bullets:["The Sky Garden"]},{heading:"20 Fenchurch St, London EC3M 8AF",paragraphs:["The Sky Garden – It’s no great secret that the “Walkie Talkie” building in Fenchurch Street has a beautiful garden on its roof.","You can have breakfast in the Sky Garden any morning without making a reservation, but word of this secret spot hasn’t gotten out to many people. Once you get to the top, all you have to do is stop for a coffee and a croissant. Better yet, there won’t be any unwanted bystanders in your pictures.","Address:"],bullets:["Upstairs at Rules"]},{heading:"Covent garden, 34-35 Maiden Ln, London WC2E 7LB",paragraphs:["Upstairs at Rules- The upstairs bar of London’s oldest restaurant recently underwent renovations that resulted in the opening of the so-called Winter Garden, a vast conservatory filled with chaise-lounges, tropical plants, and stunning flowers, where you can sip the city’s finest cocktails.","Address:"],bullets:["The Barbican Conservatory"]},{heading:"Silk St, London EC2Y 8DS",paragraphs:["The Barbican’s awe-inspiring conservatory is the ideal location for taking stunning shots. Even better, entry is free.","However, you will need to get there quickly if you wish to take photographs in peace.","Address:"],bullets:["Peggy Porschen"]},{heading:"116 Ebury St, Belgravia, London SW1W 9QQ",paragraphs:["When it comes to the term “beautiful in pink,” Peggy Porschen gives a whole new meaning. This Belgravia bakery is a favourite among royals and celebrities, and it features a pastel pink front that is bordered by rose bushes. Very endearing, to put it mildly!","Address:"],bullets:["God’s own Junkyard"]},{heading:"Unit 12, Ravenswood Industrial Estate, Shernhall St, Walthamstow, London E17 9HQ",paragraphs:["This treasure trove of neon signage is comparable to stepping into a fast-paced 1970s throwback flick.","This is London at its most Instagrammable, with its vibrant hues, flashing lights, and tongue-in-cheek slogans.","Address:"],bullets:["Big Ben and the Palace of Westminster from the South Bank"]},{heading:"Belvedere Rd, Bishop’s, London SE1 7GA",paragraphs:["On the opposite side of Westminster Bridge between Big Ben and the Palace of Westminster is a little passage leading beneath the bridge.","The entrance to the corridor frames Big Ben and the Palace for a great photograph of two of London’s most renowned structures.","Address:"],bullets:["The Colourful Houses of Notting Hill"]},{heading:"34C Lancaster Rd, Notting Hill, London W11 1QR",paragraphs:["People visit Notting Hill for the rainbow-hued houses on Lancaster Road and Portobello Road.","The neighbourhood is Instagrammable to the core, with its abundance of quaint cafes and lovely boutiques, but the rainbow-hued houses on Lancaster Road and Portobello Road are the main attraction.","Address:"],bullets:["Neal’s Yard"]},{heading:"Neal’s Yard, London WC2H 9DP",paragraphs:["Neal’s Yard is not only one of the favourite spots in London, but also one of the city’s most photogenic.","Hidden in two alleys near Covent Garden, this area was saved from demolition in the 1990s and now features a plethora of colourful buildings and plants.","Address:"],bullets:["London Eye"]},{heading:"The Queen’s Walk, Bishop’s, London SE1 7PB",paragraphs:["Since it opened at the turn of the millennium, the Coca-Cola London Eye has topped everyone’s list of things to do in the city.","It’s also a great spot to snap some shots of the nearby Palace of Westminster and the River Thames.","Before you descend from the peak of your adventure, hop in your capsule and snap that picture of the capsule in front of you, the River Thames, and the Palace of Westminster.","Address:"],bullets:["Tower Bridge"]},{heading:"Tower Bridge Rd, London SE1 2UP",paragraphs:["Whether you’re a professional or an amateur photographer, you’ll be blown away by the Tower Bridge photo opportunities.","You can get some fantastic shots from a traffic island in the middle of the road. Another classic London backdrop is the area around the Dolphin statue on St. Katharine’s Walk.","Address:"],bullets:["Richmond Park"]},{heading:"Richmond, Kingston upon Thames TW10 5HS",paragraphs:["Due to its history as a royal hunting ground, Richmond Park still features free-roaming deer.","It’s not only unusual for London, but also one of the quietest places to take pictures.","Caution: when trying to take a shot at a deer, don’t get too close to the animal.","Address:"],bullets:["Little Venice"]},{heading:"Maida Ave, Little Venice, London W2 1ST",paragraphs:["Despite its North London location, Little Venice’s canals bring to mind those of Amsterdam and the Venetian lagoon (we guess the clue was really in the name)","Taking a boat ride along the river is a great opportunity to capture one of London’s most unique neighbourhoods – not to mention providing a beautiful backdrop for your secret London Instagram photos.","Address:"],bullets:["Natural History Museum"]},{heading:"Cromwell Rd, South Kensington, London SW7 5BD",paragraphs:["We’ve come to value the Natural History Museum’s imposing architecture as one of London’s most spectacular shooting settings nearly as highly as its fascinating collections. Almost.","Tip: Arrive early to snap photos of the enormous blue whale skeleton on display in Hintz Hall.","DO YOU KNOW?","London is a city rich in diversity and a desirable place to live. With one of the highest numbers of international students, London is also a major hub in the world of higher education."],bullets:[]},{heading:"That being the case, why not take advantage of the chance to attend college in London?",paragraphs:["Contact us to schedule a free counselling appointment."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"tips-and-tricks-to-crack-exams-29",title:"TIPS AND TRICKS TO CRACK EXAMS",tag:"Exams",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/tips-and-tricks-to-crack-exams-1/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Ielts-and-toefl.png",intro:"Well, everyone gets anxious and shiver starts running through the spine before writing any exam be it school/college level or international competitive exams. Exams could make your stress level go odd, which can stop you from performing your best. Every year the difficulty of competitive exams increases and some of the students make it to the top and some don’t . Thus, it’s important to make a strategy before diving into any exam. WHAT IS IELTS?",sections:[{heading:"TIPS AND TRICKS TO CRACK IELTS,TOEFL(2023)",paragraphs:["(IELTS) full form is International English Language Testing System. It’s a foreign based English proficiency test for applicants who want to study or work in abroad countries. To get admission in international universities you would need to crack IELTS exam , where English is a primary communication language.","The IELTS exam is divided into 4 categories :- Reading, writing, speaking, listening.","To check student’s abilities and command of language , one needs to crack all the 4 aspects of IELTS exam. IELTS band score is graded on the scale of 1-9."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Types of IELTS exam?",paragraphs:["Two types of IELTS exams :- i) IELTS Academic","ii) IELTS General Training.","Speaking and listening sections are same for both of these tests, however, the content matter for the writing and reading sections are different depending on which test one takes. The writing , listening, and reading sections of the IELTS tests are completed on the same day of the test with no breaks between them. However, the Speaking section can be completed either a week before or after the other tests date.","IELTS Exam fees :-","Rs. 15,500 for registration.","Writing format:-"],bullets:[]},{heading:"i) There are 2 tasks in writing and both must be completed",paragraphs:["ii) For this section will check your use of english language appropriately.","Time for writing format :","– 60 mins","Reading format :-","i) It consists of 40 questions , made to test your reading skills.","ii) There will be 3 reading passages given to you.","Time for reading format :-","60 mins"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Listening format :-",paragraphs:["i) There are 4 parts with 10 questions each .","ii) You will listen to 4 audio recordings of native english speakers.","Time for listening section :-","approx 30 mins","Speaking format:-","i) The speaking test is taken oral between the examiner and the test taker’s.","ii) There are three parts to the test and each part fulfils a specific function","Time for speaking section :-","11-14 mins"],bullets:[]},{heading:"IELTS exam date(2022):-",paragraphs:["The IELTS test is available multiple times throughout the whole year. Book an IELTS exam date 2022 it is more convenient to them, keeping in mind about your university admission timeline according to that book your slots. Exam takers who are likely to appear for the IELTS are suggested to choose an exam date 3-4 months before their application deadline, to avoid any last-minute rush or error. The IELTS exam is taken across various major cities in India.","WHAT IS TOEFL?","The full form of (TOEFL) is Test of English as a Foreign Language. It Is a foreign based english proficiency. This test is taken to check english language ability of non-native speakers .","It assesses your ability to both speak and understand English by measuring your English ability through these 4 sections :- reading, speaking, listening and writing.","Types of TOEFL exam?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"There are 3 types of TOEFL exam :-",paragraphs:["This type of test is most preferred mode of the exam and it represents more than 98% of the TOEFL tests given worldwide.","The type of test has been re-launched keeping the pandemic and student safety In mind.","3."],bullets:["TOEFL iBT (Internet-based Test) :-","TOEFL Test on Paper :-"]},{heading:"TOEFL Home Edition :-",paragraphs:["This type of test is the TOEFL iBT test that is conducted from the safety of the home with a proctor to supervise during the entire time of the TOEFL test.","TOEFL exam fees in india:-","US $190","TOEFL EXAM PATTERN","Writing format:-"],bullets:[]},{heading:"ii) Task 1- you will need to read a short passage, listen to short lecture and write accordingly(20mins)",paragraphs:["iii) Task 2- you will need to write an essay based on personal experience or opinions on given topic.(30mins)","Reading format:-","i) Consists of questions based on 3-4 passages.","ii) You have 54-70 mins to answer all the questions in the section.","iii) Passages will be mostly based on academic topics.","iv) material that might be found in an UG university textbooks.","Listening format:-"],bullets:[]},{heading:"i) Based on to measure to understand conversations and lectures in english",paragraphs:["ii) You’ll get to hear 3-4 class room based conversations and lectures in english.","iii) You can take notes of the audio to help you answer the questions easily.","iv) You will have 41-57 minutes to complete this section","Speaking format:-","i) Based on to measure your ability to speak fluently and effectively .","ii) There four questions based on real-life situations you might witness in and outside classroom.","iii) Question 1- independent task- requires you to speak completely based on your ideas,opinions and experiences.","iv) Question 2-4 – integrated tasks – require you to collab your English-language skills like listening and speaking, or listening, reading and speaking all together.","v) You will have 17 mins to complete this section"],bullets:[]},{heading:"TOEFL Exam date (2022)",paragraphs:["The TOEFL exam is held multiple times in a year across different ETS TOEFL test centres. If you are taking the TOEFL exam you should be ready at least a month before your first application deadline. It will give you lot of time to work for your applications, and just in case you need to re-write the exam you can do it after three days of the first attempt. So, if the deadline for your application is october, you should take the TOEFL exam by september. If you feel like retaking the TOEFL, you can take it in the latter half of September again.","20 EASY TIPS AND TRICKS TO CRACK (IELTS AND TOEFL)","5 tips for writing section :-","vocabulary is a basic of every language. Writing test requires a strong vocabulary . it is not necessary to use heavy or bombastic vocabulary but also, it shouldn’t be more commonly used.","Making grammatical errors could be your major loss. It shows your low knowledge in english language. Focus on using right phrases and tenses while writing. Minor errors are okay but major errors can make your band score drop.","Practice writing on your own everyday. Pick a topic- set a timer of 60mins- write- ask your friend or teacher with good english knowledge to analyze it. The more the practice the better the results.","Read as many books as you can, know how to frame sentences. Reading books can be very beneficial to help you with using accurate vocabulary.","if you are one of them who is a slow writer or doesn’t have a proper time management and end up loosing high score questions."],bullets:["Build up a strong vocabulary:-","Avoid making errors :-","Practice:-","Read books:-","Time management :-"]},{heading:"5 tips for reading section:-",paragraphs:["1)Read books,newspapers, articles, magazines:-","because most of the reading passages will be given through these sources. It’s important to know the depth of your pronunciations of words . read aloud while you are reading.","2)Watch english news, series and movies :-","watching and listening could help you with your pronunciation and framing of words. Doing this on a regular basis will make you familiar with the accent and pronunciations, giving you a better idea of the native usage of words.","3)Practice pronouncing difficult words and passages:-","don’t limit yourself to read basic words or passages. Take note of words which are to pronouncing , listen on google it’s pronounce and make a note of it and practice it.","4)Focus on relevant information:-","it’s not necessary to understand every word or sentence in the passage, Focus on the relevant information and important details to answer the questions.","5)Skimming & other Reading Techniques :-","learn various reading techniques like skimming, speed reading, scanning etc can help you in building effective reading techniques."],bullets:[]},{heading:"5 tips for listening section",paragraphs:["1)Listen to english podcasts , news, documentary :-","build your listening skills by watching and listening to english podcasts,news,etc. This could help you polishing your hearing abilities.","2)Listen to mock audio clips:-","start listening to mock audio clips both in American and british accent. It could help you with understanding the accents and words clearly during the exam.","3)Meditation:-","practice meditation to boost your concentration level. It’s important to stay focused while listening to audio clips.","4)Focus on minor details:-","be careful while listening to audio clips , especially during the introduction, because introduction contains all the small and minor but the most important details and information about the situation and characters.","5)Pay special attention to keywords:-","as the audio clip will be played only once , pay attention to keywords, as you read the questions, underline the keywords, and try to predict the context of the recordings – who will be speaking, what the speech will be about."],bullets:[]},{heading:"5 tips for speaking section",paragraphs:["1)Develop a habit to speak in english :-","start speaking in english . it will help you to speak fluently and improve vocabulary as you speak. If you speak regularly in english with your friends and family it will also improve your fluency and confidence in the language.","2)Build your concentration level:-","start building your concentration level by meditating, paying attention to detail, developing listening skills.","3)Involve in the conversation:-","involve yourself in the conversation. And focus on the questions by keeping your ears wide open.","4)Be easy:-","don’t apply too much pressure while speaking, be calm and casual. Speak as if you are a native english speaker.","5)Work on your vocabulary:-","work on your vocabulary and grammar thoroughly . it’s important to know what you speak and how you speak. Use correct words and grammar while speaking."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Official Reference link for IELTS:-",paragraphs:["https://www.ielts.org/for-test-takers/sample-test-questions"],bullets:[]},{heading:"https://www.ets.org/toefl/test-takers/ibt/about/content.html",paragraphs:["While you are here, check out how IELTS band scores are calculated.","https://onewindow.co/ielts-band-scores/"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"scholarships-for-jain-minority-students-30",title:"SCHOLARSHIPS FOR JAIN MINORITY STUDENTS",tag:"Funding",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/scholarships-for-jain-minority-students/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Jain-.png",intro:"SCHOLARSHIPS AND EDUCATION LOAN FOR JAIN MINORITY STUDENTS! The population of Jains in our country is less than 0.4% yet they stand ahead in contributing the most to the Indian economy. Jains were given the status of a “minority religion” by the Government of India, as per Section 2(c) of the National Commission for Minorities (NCM) Act (NCM), 1992. The Jain community is one of India’s most prosperous groups. They have become a prosperous community through years of hard work and an understanding of business. However, not everyone in the community is financially secure, and this sparked the interest of several influential community members. The Jain community decided to assist students who have the academic potential to achieve greater heights but are unable to do so due to financial constraints. In addition, the ministry of minority affairs has established a few scholarships for minority students. The Jain community and the ministry of minority affairs offer the following scholarships for Jain students:",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:[],bullets:["JITO(JAIN INTERNATIONAL TRADE ORGANIZATION) EDUCATION LOAN AND SCHOLARSHIP FOR STUDYING ABROAD:"]},{heading:"JITO Education Loan Overview:-",paragraphs:["The JITO education loan program is designed to provide interest subsidies on loans taken out by students from banks or NBFCs. To be eligible for the loan subsidy scheme, students must register for the program within three months of the loan’s approval date or by March 31st. JATF assists by reimbursing the interest that students paid during the previous fiscal year, which ended on March 31. Therefore, the interest subsidy pertains to the previous fiscal year and not the current one.","If you intend to apply for the interest subsidy under the JELP scheme, you must do so between 1 April and 31 July. Another notable feature of the interest subsidy program is that it is only available during the student’s academic career. After completion of your course, no interest subsidy will be provided on the international education loan, regardless of the loan period.","If you are pursuing a two-year master’s degree, you will receive an interest subsidy for those two years, regardless of the repayment period. 2% of the total loan amount is subsidized by the JELP for overseas education loans. Since the amount of loans is excessive, there is a substantial difference in terms of interest payments.","After submitting a JELP interest subsidy application, the committee will decide in this regard. They will evaluate all relevant parameters in accordance with their rules’ specifications. And the committee’s decision will be binding."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Eligibility Criteria for JITO",paragraphs:["The eligibility criteria are pretty strict in the JELP as it is solely restricted to students from the Jain community. So, it’s important to see the full eligibility criteria that can help you in moving ahead with the scheme:","– The student applying for the interest subsidy must belong to the Jain community and must be an ardent follower of the faith and principles.","– The student must not be more than 30 years old.","– The student applying under the JELP scheme must have secured more than 50% marks at all the levels of academics from the 10th standard onwards.","– The quantum of loan taken by the applicant must be more than INR 50,000 for the entire course."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Application Procedure under JELP Scheme :-",paragraphs:["This is a complete step-by-step guide to the JELP scheme application process. To get help from the scheme, you must read it carefully and fill out the application form as soon as possible:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"– Visit the official website of JATF and download the registration form. (JATF)",paragraphs:["– The registration form should be in such format as prescribed by the JATF. Before the deadline, the student must turn in a properly filled out and signed application.","– As a sign that the registration form has been received, the student is given a registration number. If there are any changes or explanations that need to be made, they are sent by email.","– Students must keep a note that they have to complete the registration process within 3 months of the sanction of the bank loan, or before 31st March to claim the benefits under the interest subsidy scheme.","– There is no cost for registration, it’s completely free of cost."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Procedure to Claim Interest Subsidy with JITO",paragraphs:["The application procedure for interest subsidy starts when the registration is done. You can begin the JELP scheme application process once the relevant authorities have approved the registration process. Here is the step-by-step procedure for claiming an interest subsidy:","– The application procedure under the JELP scheme for claiming interest subsidy starts on the 1st of April every academic year.","– Students have two options to get the subsidy claim form. The first option is to download the application form from the website. The second choice is to email jelp@jatf.in with your request.","– The duly filled application along with all the relevant documents must reach the JATF head office in Mumbai on or before 31st July of the academic year.","– The application form must also have the signature of the applicant, and the bank branch manager from which you have taken the education loan.","Repayment","It might be the best part of the whole plan. Students who get help from the JITO Education Loan Program in the form of a discount on the interest on their loans for studying abroad don’t have to pay a single cent after the program is over.","This is a free activity whose only goal is to help talented students from the Jain community reach higher levels. The end goal is to help students get the best education they deserve even if money is an issue. The JELP interest subsidy is free money that can be used for anything."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Other Important Details about JITO Education Loan Program",paragraphs:["Before submitting an application for the interest subsidy program, students should be aware of the following vital information:","– If you are receiving a JELP interest subsidy, you are required to provide the JATF Head Office with a course progress report, a copy of your most recent grade report, and any other academic information they may require.","– Beneficiary students are required to promptly notify the JATF Head Office of any changes to their personal information, including but not limited to, their mailing address, telephone number, email address, college, and major.","– Once you’ve completed your education, you’ll need to update the JATF headquarters on your professional status.","– Applicants can only receive JATF funding from one of the two available schemes (JELP or SEED) (Students Empowering Education and Development).","Documents Required"],bullets:["A letter of loan approval from the bank.","If you have a copy of your fee receipts, please provide it.","The acceptance letter from the institution or college.","The course fee and any other financial information, such as scholarships, fee waivers, or funding options.","If the student is pursuing further education abroad, they must submit a copy of their GRE, TOEFL, GMAT, IELTS, SAT, or any other pre-admission exam scores.","The grade sheets for all classes beginning with 10th grade.","Recent Tax Returns / Proof of Income for both Parents (self, if any).","One passport-sized photo, a PAN card, an Aadhar card, and the most recent electric bill.","Mandatory recommendation from a JATF Patron Member or JITO Chapters/JITO CP/FCP/Patron Member.","Students travelling overseas are required to provide a copy of their passport.","Certificate of Being a Jain Follower / Certificate of Religion","Any document which certifies the birth date.","Bank Interest Payment Certificate for April through March.","Copy of Pass Bank / Bank Statement demonstrating Interest Payment.","If the loan is entirely repaid, the bank issues a No Due Certificate."]},{heading:"Difference between JELP and SEED",paragraphs:["Additionally, the SEED project is part of the JITO. There are some changes, but the overall nature of the plans is comparable. The two proposals are intended to expand the scope and include more students from the Jain community.","Key characteristics of the SEED project that distinguish it from the JELP scheme:","– In contrast to the JELP program, the SEED project is a loan. JELP is an interest subvention program.","– The loan granted under the SEED project is an interest-free loan that must be repaid by the student.","– The whole loan amount must be repaid within one year after course completion, and if the applicant fails to do so, he or she will be required to pay 12% annual interest on the remaining balance.","– The reimbursement may be made in a single or multiple instalments within one year of course completion.","– The applicants must have earned more than 60 percent throughout all academic levels from 10th grade onward.","– You must have covered fifty percent of the total cost of your schooling.","– The maximum interest subsidy for undergraduate and vocational programs is 1 lakh Indian rupees. The ceiling for postgraduate education is 3 lakh INR.","– Students are required to attend an interview, and if they have already arrived at their study location, parents or legal guardians may go in their stead.","For further information regarding JITO education loan visit JATF .","Under the Overseas Study Scheme for Minority Students, the Chief Minister of Telangana will provide eligible students with a TS Overseas Scholarship or Financial Aid to cover the costs of attending an overseas educational institution.","The CM Overseas Scholarship Scheme is an initiative by the government to help economically disadvantaged minorities obtain a college education in a foreign country. Telangana Overseas Scholarships are available to minority students who have been accepted for studies at a postgraduate or doctoral level at an overseas institution between January and December.","Higher education students in the fields of engineering, management, agriculture, science, medicine, nursing, the social sciences, and the humanities would get a total of Rs.20 lakh over the course of four years from the government."],bullets:["Overseas Scholarship Telangana for Minorities:"]},{heading:"Under the CM Overseas Scholarship Scheme, the Telangana State Minority Welfare Department is looking for",paragraphs:["Muslim, Christian, Sikh, Jain, Buddhist, and Pharisee","students who want to study abroad.","Those who want to go to graduate school must have a bachelor’s degree in engineering with at least 60% marks. Only those with 60% marks in their PG are eligible for the PhD CM Overseas Scholarship program.","Eligibility and Mandatory requirements:"],bullets:["Income Criteria: The annual family income must be less than Rs.5 lakhs from all sources.","Students must be residents of Telangana.","They should have a valid passport.","P.G. will only be permitted for P.G. admission in the case of Humanities.","The eligible nations are the United States, the United Kingdom, Australia, Canada, Singapore, Germany, New Zealand, Japan, France, and South Korea. Premier Universities in the Ten Countries Mentioned.","The candidate is not allowed to alter the University, Course, or Research Topic for which the Scholarship has already been Approved. This is one of the mandatory requirements.","10% of overseas scholarships are reserved for students majoring in the Humanities, Economics, Accounting, Arts, and Law.","The candidate must have valid TOEFL/IELTS and GRE/GMAT scores meeting the minimum requirements. 1. TOEFL score: 60; 2. IELTS score: 6.0; 3. GRE score: 260; 4. GMAT score: 500.","He/she must not be availing any other scholarship or financial assistance from any other source.","In the online application, applicants are required to list their educational qualification marks alongside their total marks.","Applicants will provide accurate information on the online application form; those who provide false information will have their applications rejected and legal action taken against them.","PhD Programs are also eligible for the program.","Allocation of seats to BC students shall be based on both Reservation and Merit; I 5% of the Target for BCs shall be reserved for EBCs; and"]},{heading:"(i) 5% of the Target for BCs shall be reserved for MBCs",paragraphs:["(ii) For income criteria, the income of the student’s parents+ is not to be considered family income if the student is employed.","Documents required:","These are the mandatory documents which should be uploaded during the registration.","FAQ’S:","Full-time graduate/postgraduate students can receive up to INR 15 Lakhs per year in funding from the scholarship, and Ph.D. students can receive up to INR 20 Lakhs per year.","Telangana Social Welfare Residential Educational Institutions Society accepts online applications for the Telangana Overseas Scholarship on their website.","The Higher Education Department of Telangana and the Telangana Social Welfare Residential Educational Institutions Society work together to form a committee that reviews the scholarship applications. The committee looks at things like GPA, financial need, and how applicable the major is to the student’s long-term plans.","*FOR ANY FURTHER INFORMATION REGARDING TS OVERSEAS EDUCATION SCHOLARSHIP VISIT IT’S OFFICAL WEBSITE*","https://telanganaepass.cgg.gov.in","*REGISTRATION FORM*:","https://telanganaepass.cgg.gov.in/OverseasReg.do?stu_details(studentAction)=unspecified&","For more information contact –","9154 89 89 44"],bullets:["Application form.","Admit card of last qualifying examination.","Mark sheet of last qualifying examination.","Passport size photograph.","Passport copy.","Proof of admission to a foreign university and","Income certificate.","What is the amount of scholarship provided?","How to apply for the Telangana Overseas Scholarship?","How are scholarship applications evaluated?"]}]},{slug:"manage-your-finances-abroad-31",title:"MANAGE YOUR FINANCES ABROAD",tag:"Life abroad",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/manage-your-finances-abroad/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/manage-finance.webp",intro:"HOW TO MANAGE YOUR FINANCES ABROAD! The world today is more liberal and open vis-a-vis other nations. One most common way of erasing boundaries is student exchange programmes or allowing more individuals to study within your country by making the process simpler. Travelling to your dream city/could start adrenaline rush in your body. However, studying abroad could be expensive, more so for Indians as rupees stand no chance in front of dollars,pounds etc. Nonetheless, all is not lost in paradise and with sound financial planning, one can fulfil their dream of studying abroad.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["You have to manage your money very effectively in abroad . Spending unnecessary money on unnecessary things could make you broke . We understand that travelling abroad could be very overwhelming and exciting for you. You might have waited all your life to live the most happening yet unbounded lifestyle there. It’s normal to get influenced from your surroundings but smartness isn’t in showing off how good and partyholic rich lifestyle you are living but real smartness is in knowing when and how to use your money effectively.","6 Helpful tips to manage your finances abroad","1.The Big B:-","Budgeting plays a crucial role when it comes to studying abroad. Many people often start budgeting in the later stages but this is a very tricky gamble. We always recommend people to first start with a budget. This will come in handy in shortlisting colleges and universities as well.","It is very important to be realistic when formulating the budget. “We will see” attitude can drown you in a pool of sorrows and we definitely wouldn’t like that for you. Finding the right balance between your income and expenses is the real deal of budgeting. Knowing the difference between what you need and what you want will determine your spending habits and the level of comfort you enjoy financially.","2.The more the merrier:-","Having multiple income sources is very important in covering the cost of living while studying abroad. Some of the important sources of income include monetary assistance from family. Here it is important to have the necessary documents relating to the source of income, tax papers etc. Honest and open discussions with the family can make the process smoother.","3.Get a part-time job:-","find a job for yourself to cover your daily expenses. As you are in a new financial system using a new currency it can be difficult to gauge how much money you will need for miscellaneous expenses, such as, transport, healthcare, books, etc. Having a part-time job can ease your load. Working on your own could make you independent and responsible enough to not spend blindly. In most of the countries, you can work up to 18-20 hours per week and full-time during your term breaks.","4.Scholarships & Grants:-","Scholarships and grants are also offered to individuals wanting to study abroad. There are scholarships based on subjects, colleges, regions, etc which may partially or completely cover your expenses. More than 3060 scholarships are available for people wanting to study abroad. Scholarships cover most of your expenses like tuition fees, accommodation, health insurance, travel, etc. and some scholarships are fully funded.","5.Use your student status :-","Get your student card as soon as possible. This card can help you avail many discounts on restaurants, shops, movie tickets, places, transportation and various cultural events. In fact, there are many such websites which offer great student discounts on festive events and shopping, check them out. Students id’s are always the best option to save money.","6.Manage your social activities:-","It’s obvious to get tempted and influenced by surroundings to party and meet new people around in the new country. This can be a big expense for you, if you are not careful with your spendings. You should have it planned all your social activities and have a fixed amount set aside for your entertainment purpose.","Sometimes, instead of going out to meet people, you could invite them to your house, which will work out cheap and best . Just for the sake of showing off your happening life on social media or to attract a bunch of people. Remember, that you spend according to your budget. Spending blindly can end up making you broke in completely unknown country."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The hole in the pocket, or not?",paragraphs:["The first and foremost step in this direction is to account for your expenses. The primary and unavoidable expenses include application fees, tuition fees, living expenses including rent, electricity bills, etc, transportation expenses, mandatory health insurance, grocery and essential expenses, recreational expenses, expenses on clothing and personal items such as phones, laptops, etc. All these expenses are unavoidable and we at One Window can help you find the appropriate figure to fill in the blanks for you. We make sure to leave no gaps behind so that you’re never in a tough spot.","Slack the cost","Once you have known the costs, cost cutting becomes the next obvious step in this direction. Many universities have accelerated programmes that allow students to take more courses for the same price, or to study in a less expensive community college for a short period reducing the cost drastically. Cultural living can help in reducing your living expense largely. Taking inputs from industry experts is a smart way to go about it. At One Window we have the best experts who have the latest information on changing policies making the process stress-free for you.","There is no doubt about the fact that the benefits of studying abroad outweigh the cost of it. Realistic planning with the help of a professional can truly make the process seamless and transform your dream into a breathing living reality."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"is-study-gap-acceptable-in-canada-32",title:"IS STUDY GAP ACCEPTABLE IN CANADA",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/is-study-gap-acceptable-in-canada/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/study-gap.png",intro:"Canada is one of the most preferred study abroad destinations in the world because of its prestigious schools, low tuition rates, and warm atmosphere. Canada has a very adaptable educational system, and many universities are willing to accommodate students taking time off between degrees. Students applying to associate’s, bachelor’s, and master’s programmes may take a one-year break between courses, while those applying to doctoral programmes may take up to five years.",sections:[{heading:"What is a study gap?",paragraphs:["A student’s “study gap” occurs whenever they stop actively pursuing further education. During this time, they are free to pursue a wide range of other pursuits, such as taking a gap year to get work experience or preparing for an entrance or competitive exam."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Is the study gap accepted by Canadian universities and colleges?",paragraphs:["Gaps in education are generally recognised at Canadian universities and colleges, though the exact length of time allowed may vary. Canadian universities have varying policies when it comes to the number of years students can take off between degrees. Taking a year or more off before enrolling at a university overseas is common among students from other countries. In addition, candidates should provide documentation and reasonable justifications to back up their gap year claim."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How many years of a study gap are accepted in Canada?",paragraphs:["Probable students always have this doubt in their minds: how much of a study gap is acceptable in Canada? Usually, a 2-year gap is accepted by most of the universities in Canada for undergraduate courses, and a 5-year gap is acceptable for postgraduate courses. However, we recommend that you contact the university where you intend to apply and find out how much of a study gap they will accept for enrolment.Some Canadian institutions also require students to submit valid reasons and legitimate documents to support their claim for a study gap.","Reasons accepted in Canada for a study gap:","Students planning to take a break from their studies must provide an explanation for their decision. Here is a short list of study break justifications that are typically recognised by Canadian universities.","Students planning to take a break from their studies must provide an explanation for their decision.","A student who has experienced significant health issues throughout their studies is permitted to temporarily suspend their studies and return to them at a later date.","A student’s education might be put on hold if he or she is called to active military duty and picked back up thereafter.","Taking the right examinations for longer than the allotted time may be necessary to acquire the grade point average you need to get into that prestigious foreign university. Students need study abroad time to prepare for these tests.","There are times when a break from school is necessary for personal reasons. In order to pay for their time abroad, students may need to make financial preparations. Canadian higher education institutions generally accept and take into account the aforementioned factors.","Students should be aware that institutions are able to research what students did during their gap years and will require proof before admitting them. During the visa application process, you may be asked to provide evidence such as medical records, course completion certificates, or proof of employment in the form of pay stubs.","6.Skills development:","Not every student pursues a career path; instead, some continue their education. Students often seek out non-academic practical experience after completing their formal education. in the same way that students study foreign languages and public speaking (international). Long-term, students may benefit from acquiring these skills.","It is common practice for students to take time off from school to study in preparation for entrance exams in order to pursue a higher education in another country.","Beyond these, students often take a year off before continuing their education in order to save up the necessary funds. Canada’s academic institutions typically take these explanations into account and accept them. Any work or education done during a person’s gap years must be verified with official documentation.","During the visa application process, you will need to provide evidence such as transcripts, medical records, pay stubs, skill certificates, and so on to prove that the time off between semesters was necessary.","Study gaps can be useful.","Taking a break from your studies to focus on developing your personal skills and gaining professional experience can be quite beneficial. During your gap years, you will grow as a person and have more time to study abroad, prepare for tests, and establish your financial footing.There is no need to be concerned about a break in your education because, if you have the appropriate paperwork and legitimate grounds, you will be able to gain admission to a university in Canada.","The way in which you choose to spend your gap year can have a significant impact on the rest of your life and provide you with the impetus you need to make positive strides forward. Talk to our specialists in foreign education if you have questions or concerns about any gaps in your education, and they will point you in the correct direction."],bullets:["Professional Experience/Internships:","Health issues:","Military Service:","Entrance exams:","Personal reasons:","Second try:"]}]},{slug:"gre-and-gmat-33",title:"GRE AND GMAT",tag:"Exams",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/gre-and-gmat/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/gre-gmat.webp",intro:"HOW TO CRACK GRE AND GMAT? WHAT IS GRE? The full form of (GRE) is Graduate record examinations. It is a standard test that is taken for admissions requirement for many graduate schools in the United States and Canada and other few countries. GRE measures the overall academic readiness of a candidate who wants to attend the graduate school.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["GRE exam is scheduled multiple times in a year. GRE Subject Tests are only extended to physics , chemistry, Psychology and mathematics.","The exam pattern for GRE :- verbal reasoning, quantitative reasoning and analytical writing skills.","This test is about 3 hours and 45 minutes.","Types of GRE exam ?","There are 2 types of GRE tests :-","1.","General test(GRE)","2.","Subject test (GRE)","GRE exam fees :-","US $213 = 17,394.11 Indian Rupee.","GRE slot booking:-","GRE Exam Slot Booking: The GRE slot booking is the process of GRE exam registration. The GRE slot booking consists of three fine steps:","1.GRE Registration","2.GRE Exam Dates","3.GRE Test Centres","Sections of GRE exam :-","i) It asses Reading Comprehension, Text Completion and Sentence Equivalence."],bullets:["Verbal Reasoning:-"]},{heading:"ii) Each section consists of 20 questions",paragraphs:["iii) 20 questions – 6 text completion, 4 sentence equivalence, and 10 critical reading questions.","i) It asses basic high-school level mathematical knowledge and reasoning skills.","ii) Consists of 20 questions :- 8 quantitative comparisons, 9 problem solving items, and 3 data interpretation questions.","i) It consists of 2 different essays :- issue task, argument task.","ii) In issue task you would need to construct your own argument.","iii) In argument task you would need to evaluate someone else’s argument.","iv) You will use a basic word processor developed by ETS to type your essay responses. (only insert text, delete text, cut-and-paste and undo the previous action)"],bullets:["Quantitative Reasoning:-","Analytical Writing :-"]},{heading:"WHAT IS GMAT EXAM ?",paragraphs:["The full form of (GMAT) is graduate management admission test. It assess our analytical, verbal and writing skills and problem solving abilities. It is a multiple choice computer based standardized test. It is the top most exam taken to get into your preferred business school.","This test is for people who are applying for management/business programs (MBA etc.)"],bullets:[]},{heading:"GMAT exam pattern :-",paragraphs:["i) Quantitative , ii) verbal iii) integrated reasoning and iv) analytical writing assessment.","GMAT Exam fees :-","US $275 (INR 22,456.91)","Sections of GMAT:-"],bullets:["Quantitative Reasoning:-"]},{heading:"i) In this section you would have 31 questions",paragraphs:["ii) It consists of 2 problem types:- data sufficiency, problem solving."],bullets:[]},{heading:"iv) 17-18(problem solving questions)",paragraphs:["v) This section will test your mathematic knowledge and reasoning skills.","i) In this section you would have 36 questions.","ii) It consists of 3 problem types:- critical reasoning, sentence correction, reading comprehension."],bullets:["Verbal Reasoning :-"]},{heading:"iii) It will test your command of standard written english",paragraphs:["iv) To check your ability to read critically.","i) In this section you would have 12 multi-part problems total.","ii) It consists of 4 problem types:- multi-source reasoning, table analysis, graphics interpretation, two- part analysis."],bullets:["Integrated Reasoning:-"]},{heading:"iii) It covers same quantitative concepts",paragraphs:["iv) It tests your quant and verbal skills together.","v) Also , tests your ability to interpret and analyze graphs and tables."],bullets:["Analytical writing :-"]},{heading:"i) In this section you only have one problem (essay)",paragraphs:["ii) You will be presented with brief argument in which you have to critique the author’s argument .","iii) It tests your logical and creative thinking.","5 EASY TIPS AND TRICKS TO CRACK (GRE) and (GMAT)","1.Collect all your materials:-","Gather all your study materials you need for your exam. Books, online videos, mock papers etc. Buy some GRE and GMAT books from the market and download official GRE and GMAT apps. You can also refer to online study materials which are easily available.","2.Build a strong vocabulary:-","Again , the stronger the vocabulary the higher the score. Strengthening your vocabulary could help you score better in all the 3 sections of GRE. Read english books, novels, watch series,movies, listen to podcasts .","3.Take mock tests:-","Practice mock tests as much as possible. It would be easy for you to understand the test format and pattern during the exam.","4.Give extra time for quantitative reasoning:-","In both the tests quantitative reasoning is the most important section. give special attention and time to quantitative section . In this section it requires problem solving skills, basic mathematic and arithmetic skills. Focus more on practicing and storming your brain for this section.","5.Manage your time:-","Managing your time during the test is bigger task itself. Lack of time management can make you miss out important questions and your score may drop. Practice as much as possible."],bullets:[]},{heading:"GMAT Exam Dates 2022:-",paragraphs:["There is no proper fixed date for official GMAT exam 2022, you can choose any date according to your availability and convenience. Just in case if you need to retake the GMAT test you can do it after 16 days. You can take or retake the GMAT exam after every 16 days. Preferably , candidates are suggested to register two to three months before the actual exam date.","If you are registering online or by phone, you can get registered as late as 24 hours before the exam date. It is safer to go for early registration as you will have a proper set schedule to prepare for the exam accordingly. Even the coaching institutes will recommend you to register at the earliest available date so that you get a plenty of time to prepare for the exam."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Official website for test preparation for (GMAT):-",paragraphs:["https://www.mba.com/exams/gmat-exam/prep-for-the-exam?nav=header"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"accomodation-34",title:"ACCOMODATION",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/accomodation/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/accomodation.png",intro:"HOW TO FIND A RIGHT ACCOMODATION. Finding a right accommodation in abroad is not just piece of cake. You have to check every single detail before getting your perfect accommodation. Abroad could be very taxing and risky at the same time. But, if seen isn’t risk everywhere? Getting a perfect accommodation could be overwhelming but with right knowledge and research you won’t have a lot on your plate to deal with. Many people tend to delay the accommodation part while dealing with visa or admission process and because of this you end up facing difficulties to find a right accommodation.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["For Indian students the task difficulty increases as they need apartments, hostels with Indian students who are already staying. The mindset of staying with people of same country could look easy and fun but it could also stop you from exploring yourself and getting to know new people in the new country /city . You need to make yourself flexible enough to adjust with anyone and anywhere.","Besides, finding a right accommodation is the big question here. Don’t worry you are on the right article , we got you!","(photo)","One of the most important factor of abroad is the living. There are so many variety of options available for a student to accommodate",". Like studio apartments, hostels, pg’s , university housing, dorms or lofts.","Uni housing can be in campus or close to campus. If you want to save time and money university housing is a good option but, again university housing is based on first come first serve basis.","It’s also important to check basic factors and needs before finalizing the apartment. Afterall, this is a major decision which shouldn’t be made in hurry because you will be living,eating,studying,sleeping etc there. You have to make sure of lot of things before selecting your RIGHT accommodation.","Top 5 things to check before selecting your accommodation.","Most obvious and important factor to check among all. Make sure you check and research every apartment thoroughly with it’s cost of living and everything and choose the most reasonable one.","Rent also varies from city and area you decide to live and also depends on what kind of accommodation you are looking for. Like, studios and loft can cost you ton and basic apartments or hostels could be a cheap shot . You also need to decide how much you had like to spend on rent based on your monthly expense.","Check out :- https://4stay.com/ They provide you with the cheapest rooms as per your requirements and the location you want.","sharing your apartment can cut down your rent to half. Isn’t that great? But, the catch is are you willing to share one? Like if you are okay sharing your apartment with 3-4 people you could live together and divide the monthly expenses among yourself. Many international students do that.","Before renting an apartment make sure you check the neighbourhood safety. Just to save money, don’t risk your safety . lookout for places which are environment friendly. It’s better to choose an apartment which is nearby to the university."],bullets:["Rent :-","Sharing apartments:-","Location :-"]},{heading:"Check out :- https://www.universityliving.com/?gclid=EAIaIQobChMIpfmEve2R-wIVjgkrCh0fFwWhEAAYASAAEgJAVfD_BwE",paragraphs:["They got the best customer feedback and is great in providing rooms which are affordable yet environment friendly.","Make sure you understand lease/contract before signing it . There are many fake / fraud sites and firms which may fool you into loosing your money. Check whether the contract includes wifi,cable, utilities etc. Before signing , visit the location once it will help you gain a view. Such requests are approved and they will give you viewing slip for your assigned unit.","to save your time and money, prefer furnished units to unfurnished one’s . As, you are already committed to course work and are in the process of integrating to a new environment, so try and avoid the extra burden of having to go shopping for furniture and utensils. Buying and assembling furniture can be expensive and time-consuming affair.","Just In case you have been allotted an unfurnished unit, contact your classmates or seniors to find out if anyone is selling used furniture for a reduced price. Most universities also have social media groups like “for free and for sale” where students put up everything from table lamps and books to beds and tables for sale. This is a good place to find a bargain for yourself.","Check out:-","https://amberstudent.com/?utm_source=google_adwords&utm_campaign=branded_all&utm_content=AmberStudent-All&keyword=amberstudent&gclid=EAIaIQobChMI9I21p–R-wIV1H8rCh0HUgnOEAAYASAAEgLKoPD_BwE","Amberstudent are professional in their service, they are excellent in follow-ups’s and the prices are cheap and affordable.","These are the 5 things that should be checked before confirming the accommodation. Beware of the frauds and scammers to con you.","Basic checklist?","Finding accommodation could be challenging but with the right website and the right guidance you got it all covered.","Make sure you choose your apartment wisely after looking at all the points above mentioned. You have to keep in mind that you won’t always meet your expectations but make sure your basic needs are met.","Get in touch with (one window link) to make sure your abroad journey goes seamlessly."],bullets:["Understanding lease/contract:-","Furnished or unfurnished apartment:-","Check whether the house has everything you need for a while?","See if you are provided with internet / wifi connection?","Check if you are charged extra for any certain utilities etc.","Check if there is laundry service available.","Check if you have proper study desk to work and study.","Check if you are provided with kitchen utensils or not."]}]},{slug:"6-things-to-do-after-your-visa-is-approved-35",title:"6 THINGS TO DO AFTER YOUR VISA IS APPROVED",tag:"Visa",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/6-things-to-do-after-your-visa-is-approved/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/6-things-1.png",intro:"Getting your visa approved is a long process. And the real struggles starts after your visa gets approved. You would have so much on your plate to deal with and you will be confused with so many questions rushing in your head. Like, where to start? How to start? What to do? Etc. There will be complete chaos in your mind. The only solution is to “Divide and Plan”. Divide and categorize your work while keeping in mind that which should be addressed first and prioritize accordingly and then plan your strategy. Here are some things mentioned to do after your visa is approved. Top 6 things to do after your visa is approved:-",sections:[{heading:"Book your flight tickets:-",paragraphs:["Get done with booking your flight tickets as soon as possible. As , the prices are always fluctuating. Delaying your tickets to the last is a bad option. So, pre-book your tickets early so that it can help you save a ton.","Finding a right accommodation abroad isn’t a piece of cake. If you need your comfort-satisfactory-home type accommodation you have to go an extra mile before you even reach there.","As easy going it seems it isn’t , you have to research,check,talk it out and fix. There are several type of accommodation you can check for like","(studio apartments,hostels,PG’s,university housing,lofts)","Also read (our accommodation blog)","*Note*-"],bullets:["Accommodation:-"]},{heading:"‘watch out for the fraud site’s which can con you into loosing your personal details and ton of amount’",paragraphs:[],bullets:["Pay your tuition fee:- As your admission is confirmed , you have to pay some initial deposit or 1st installment to the university you chose. Delaying the fee payment could even mean your admission getting cancelled, a situation you would want to avoid at any cost."]},{heading:"How to pay your tuition fee online?",paragraphs:["One of the biggest concerns for students aspiring to study in colleges and universities in the US, the UK, and Canada is how to pay their education fees and that too, on time. Universities in different countries accept various forms of payment. The process of payment varies from college to college.","Top 5 Methods to pay your fees abroad.","i. Bank transfer:- Paying your fees through bank is one of the best and safest option. By clearing your tuition fees through bank you would have proper invoices and fee receipts with you for any future references.","Bank transfers can be done via net banking or by visiting the nearest branch of your bank. You will be required to bring the beneficiary bank details of your college or university and the","SWIFT"],bullets:[]},{heading:"code of that bank (",paragraphs:["SWIFT is a global messaging platform that facilitates international money transfers electronically)."],bullets:[]},{heading:"ii.Payment platforms:-",paragraphs:["Sometimes there are specific payment platforms for Universities worldwide which a students can use to clear their fees. Some of these platforms, have tie-ups with a few Indian banks.","If a student happen to use any of these platforms and transfer their tuition fees using the banks they would have a tie-up with the remittance tends to get processed faster."],bullets:[]},{heading:"iii. Demand draft:-",paragraphs:["A cashier’s cheque, or demand draft, can also be a way to pay for your tuition fees abroad. You can visit your bank and ask them to issue a demand draft with the required amount in the name of your university. You can then mail it to your university to clear your fees.","iv.Credit Cards:-","Most of the universities accept payments through credit cards. Remember that this method may attract charges from your card issuing bank.","v.Forex cards:-","Another secure and safe way to pay your tuition fees is through a forex card. It’s a prepaid card that you can load money in a foreign currency.","They are globally accepted and it enables you to withdraw cash at foreign ATMs. They are way more convenient because you can use them to clear all your pending fees at your university or for any other purpose you require.","Opening a bank account for your abroad studies can help you pay for your day-to-day expenses and they make other transactions like payment of fees, rent, etc. It’s better to make one from india itself to cut the load off from you.","It’s important to get a sim card of the country you are going to. If you want to avoid any international charges on your calling, get a sim card. Eg:- if you are going to Uk , research for the UK based companies which provide mobile service and sim cards.","Last but not the least, the most important step is to apply for education loans . if it seems difficult to cover your expenses go for this option. You can also cover up this loan by doing part-time jobs there which are usually of high pay compared to india."],bullets:["Open a bank account :-","Get your abroad sim card :-","Education loan:-"]}]},{slug:"the-new-shorter-gre-general-test-discover-exciting-new-changes-36",title:"The New Shorter GRE General Test: Discover Exciting New  Changes!",tag:"Exams",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/the-new-shorter-gre-general-test-discover-exciting-new-changes/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/GRE-general-test.png",intro:"two hours, making it quicker, smarter, and more challenging than ever before! Let’s dive into the details of this groundbreaking test update and how it can supercharge your path to graduate success.",sections:[{heading:"Exam (GRE®) in 2023. Prepare for the all-new Shorter GRE, which will be completed in less than",paragraphs:["This Blog covers the following questions:-","What is the shorter GRE?","What are the new changes in GRE?","Why there is a shorter GRE?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What doesn’t change on the shorter GRE?",paragraphs:["What is the difference between the current GRE and the new shorter GRE?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What is the shorter GRE?",paragraphs:["Say good-bye to lengthy test sessions and hello to a quicker, more effective GRE experience!"],bullets:[]},{heading:"half! Now, you can demonstrate your skills and potential in less than two hours, allowing you",paragraphs:["more time to focus on what truly matters"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What are the new changes in GRE?",paragraphs:["Here is a full list of all the most important changes that will be made to the new, shorter GRE:","Change 1: Reduced Exam Duration:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"The duration of the GRE General Test will be cut in half, from approximately 4 hours to",paragraphs:["approximately 2 hours. This is a significant change intended to reduce test-taker fatigue and","improve their concentration."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Change 2: Elimination of the “Analyze an Argument” Task from the Analytical Writing Section",paragraphs:["The Analytical Writing section will only consist of the “Analyse an Issue” task moving forward."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The “Analyze an Argument” task will be eliminated. This means that for the Analytical Writing",paragraphs:["section, you will only be required to write one essay instead of two, sparing you time and","effort."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Change 3: Reduced Number of Questions",paragraphs:["There will be fewer questions in the Quantitative Reasoning and Verbal Reasoning sections."],bullets:[]},{heading:"From 40 to 27, the precise number of questions per section will be reduced. Despite a reduction",paragraphs:["in the number of questions, the average time available to respond to each question remains","unchanged."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Currently, the GRE contains a section that is not assessed and is used for ETS research. This",paragraphs:["section, which does not contribute to the test-taker’s total, will be eliminated from the GRE’s","shortened version."],bullets:[]},{heading:"test accommodation due to a disability or health-related need. No unscheduled pauses are",paragraphs:["permitted for those who are testing at home."],bullets:[]},{heading:"current 10–15 calendar days. This will expedite the application process for applicants. This will",paragraphs:["help applicants complete their applications quicker."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Amit Sevak, chief executive officer of ETS, emphasises that the GRE modifications are aimed",paragraphs:["towards enhancing the test-taker experience without compromising discipline and validity.","We’re dedicated to ensuring a simpler, shorter, and more enjoyable test-taking journey.","What is the new GRE format 2023?","Section","No. of Sections","No. of Questions","Time Limit","Analytical Writing","1 section","1 essay task","30 minutes","Quantitative Reasoning","2 sections","27 questions","47 minutes","Verbal Reasoning","2 sections","27 questions","41 mints","Total Time","1 hour 48 minutes"],bullets:[]},{heading:"What doesn’t change on the shorter GRE?",paragraphs:["Quantitative and Verbal sections on the new GRE will continue to be section-adaptive.","Graduate and professional programmes will continue to utilize the GRE as they have in the past.","The GRE examination fees will remain at 22,550."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The score scale on the new GRE test will remain the same",paragraphs:["What is the difference between the current GRE and the new shorter GRE?","Section","Current GRE","New Shorter GRE"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Analytical Writing tasks",paragraphs:["The essay part of the test consists of two parts:","1.”Analyse an Argument”","2.”Analyse an Issue”","(60 minutes total).","One essay task (30 minutes) – “Analyze an Issue” task only","Quantitative Reasoning questions","40 questions (70 minutes)","27 questions (47 minutes)","Verbal Reasoning questions","40 questions (70 minutes)","27 questions (47 minutes)","Scheduled Break","10-minute break after the 2nd hour","No scheduled break","Total Test Time","3 hours, 45 minutes","1 hour, 58 minutes","To sum up,","Many test takers, particularly those who are in the middle of their test preparation, are confused by the new GRE exam changes. But, no matter where you are in your GRE journey, we at one window are always ripe for any changes. Book a free consultation with our counselor and find out the right GRE exam for you."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"how-to-overcome-homesickness-37",title:"How To Overcome Homesickness?",tag:"Life abroad",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/how-to-overcome-homesickness/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Homesick.png",intro:"Travelling abroad for your higher studies , work or travel could be a major change in your life. Coming from completely different culture ,environment ,society and going to completely brand new country or city could be a huge step. Homesickness is normal. It’s normal to miss your home , family, friends, environment, food etc. Homesickness is basically getting emotionally distressed about missing out. Trying too hard to overcome homesickness could also develop distress and anxiety in your body, instead be easy on yourself, let yourself feel things only then you could handle the feeling of homesick. Most often distress comes from not accepting our feelings. Instead of stressing too much on feeling homesick remember that it is ‘okay’ to feel this way and at some point these feelings won’t be an issue. Moreover, you could also develop a feeling of ‘FOMO’. Constantly checking your social media ,constantly worrying about what’s happening at your home place , thinking and imagining yourself there won’t help you instead it will create more chaos in your mind and spoil your mental health.",sections:[{heading:"i. Feeling sad , anxious , nervous, angry",paragraphs:["ii. Developing a feeling of insecurity , getting panic","iii. Headaches","iv. Having a disturbed sleep pattern"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Top 6 ways to help you overcome Homesickness",paragraphs:["“Patience is bitter, but it’s fruit is sweet.”- Aristotle quoted this very small yet deep quote about having patience is one of the most positive virtues in achieving anything in life. Instead of stressing over things which are not practically possible . Try meditation and mindfulness to keep yourself calm and patient . because every process takes time, the process maybe slow but its worth the wait.","Talking about your feelings could be difficult but it isn’t impossible. Talking to your loved one’s could make the distance feel shorter. Videocall , text, audio call them it would help you feel less anxious but talking all the time or everyday could take you back again to the starting point and would keep you off from making new friends. You need your old loved one’s but you also need to start adjusting with your new surroundings. Remember the mantra 3E’s ‘evolve’ , ‘experience’ , ‘explore’ . practice these 3E’s in your life for better living.","social media detox is one of the best tip to overcome homesickness, constantly checking your social media, checking about what people or your home friends are doing could make your homesickness even worse. The reels, the posts, the stories you never noticed normally you would start overthinking it more when you are away, you would want your old comfort life back, you would miss your home food, home place, old friends etc. you could build a feeling of giving up and going back to normal but instead of nagging yourself continuously on how things would have been different if you never came to abroad you should take a social media detox which has only pro’s . 1) it could help you with improving your mental health. 2) it could divert your mind from reel life to real life.","How to take a social media detox?"],bullets:["Homesickness won’t go overnight :-","Talk to your loved ones(not everyday):-","Social media detox:-"]},{heading:"i) Let people know :-",paragraphs:["The first step to taking a social media detox is to tell people.","Let people you interact the most know that you won’t be online for a while. This will do a few things.","First, it will keep you accountable. If you’re back within a few days posting stories and posts the people you informed might call you out on it. This will help you stick with the detox.","Secondly, it will let people know you haven’t disappeared if you wind up sticking with it."],bullets:[]},{heading:"ii) Delete your social media apps:-",paragraphs:["This step is required. I can guarantee you that you won’t succeed, if you keep those apps on your phone during your detox you’ll only check them once a week.","For this to work, you’ll need to disconnect completely. If that seems too hard, try a shorter detox."],bullets:[]},{heading:"iii) Plan what you will do during your detox :-",paragraphs:["after deleting your social media apps you would wonder how much extra time you have with yourself. Plan what you will do during your detox and actually applying it or doing it in your spare time.","Some suggestions that I found useful:","*Reading"],bullets:[]},{heading:"*Learning something new (language, hobby, skill)",paragraphs:["*Working on a side project or business","*Exercising, gym, yoga"],bullets:[]},{heading:"“Go for a mediation retreat. Enjoy your time with yourself and explore your mind.”",paragraphs:["“Comfort is your biggest trap and coming out of comfort zone is your biggest challenge” – Manoj Arora . A well said quote by Manoj Arora . I understand that getting out of your comfort is a difficult task but it’s one of the tip to overcome homesickness because sticking to same old routine, comfort could never help you achieve your big goals in life. Try making yourself flexible in every situation you already won half the battle if you can adjust in any situation. If you become too attached to where you are and who you are ,you can’t grow , you can’t evolve, or expand the best version of yourself Give up on excuses.","keeping yourself physically fit is okay but keeping yourself mentally fit is important. There are gonna be times where you would loose yourself, wave of sadness,distress,anxiety will hit you. But, keeping yourself away from all of these is the trick. Firstly, you gotta train your mind to be stronger than your emotions or else you’ll lose yourself every time. Secondly, distance yourself from negative thoughts and feelings . avoid overthinking or stressing about things it will get you no where and lastly, make a habit of meditating every day. Learn more about your cognitive thinking.","Here are some apps to help yourself :-"],bullets:["Leave your comfort zone behind :-","Keep your mental health above everything :-"]},{heading:"i) Calm (meditation and sleep app)",paragraphs:["ii) Headspace (meditation and sleep app)","iii) Gratitude (self care journal )"],bullets:[]},{heading:"iv) Fit mind (mental fitness)",paragraphs:["Everytime you feel like giving up, remind yourself everyday that why you chose to move abroad , what made you take this decision at first because anyone can give up it’s so easy to do. But you gotta hold your strength and move forward. Self motivate yourself . start believing in yourself, you are braver than you think. More talented than you know.","If you are anxious about getting homesick or doubting yourself again and again to move abroad , talk to our certified yet experienced consultants for FREE NOW!","One window overseas education offers one-stop solutions for all overseas educational needs. One Window has already helped around 6,500students to get admission. We provide you complete guide and clear all your doubts about moving abroad."],bullets:["Remind yourself why you chose to move abroad:-"]}]},{slug:"ielts-band-scores-how-are-they-calculated-38",title:"IELTS Band Scores : How are they Calculated",tag:"Exams",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/ielts-band-scores/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/IELTS.png",intro:"IELTS band scores ranges from 0 to 9. Your IELTS band score might also be in.5 increments (for example 6.5,7.5). The International English Language Testing System (IELTS) is the most common English language proficiency test in the world for higher education and international migration. IELTS Band Scores: How they are computed",sections:[{heading:"IELTS Band Scores: How they are calculated",paragraphs:["The","IELTS","Band Scores Chart below explains the scoring to assist you in calculating and comprehending your score."],bullets:[]},{heading:"How the IELTS Band Scores are calculated",paragraphs:["IELTS band scores range from 0 to 9. Your IELTS band score might also be in.5 increments (for example 6.5,7.5). The International English Language Testing System (IELTS) is a test of all four of the core language skills: listening, reading, writing, and speaking. We get a band score for each skill (Reading band score, Listening band score, Speaking band score, and Writing band score) and an overall band score. Consider the example in the table below.",".IELTS Listening and Reading Band Scores:","Your IELTS Listening band score and Reading band score are determined by how many of the 40 questions you answer correctly. You don’t lose points if you get an answer wrong.IELTS LISTENING SCORES:IELTS ACADEMIC READING SCORES:IELTS GENERAL TRAINING READING SCORES:IELTS Writing Assessment Criteria:Both writing tasks 1 and 2 are graded using four criteria. Please see the details below for more information on each criterion for scoring.IELTS Speaking Assessment Criteria:The IELTS speaking criteria are listed below.","Listening","Reading","Writing","Speaking","Overall","8","7.5","7","7.5","7.5","According to","IELTS website",", the overall IELTS band score is calculated to the nearest whole band score or 0.5 band score which indicates that the actual band score, as displayed in the table above, is not an exact 7.5. Your score is pushed to the nearest whole or half point, depending on which one is closest. Here are some examples of how this functions:","If you have a total score of 7.1, it will drop to 7. This is the closest score to a whole band. So, 7.1 is closer to 7 than 7.5 is.","If your computed overall score is 6.75, your ultimate result will be band 7. Therefore, 6.75 is closer to band 7 than to band 6.5.","If your cumulative score is 6.25, it will be raised to 6.5. The score of 6.25 falls perfectly between bands 6 and 6.5, so IELTS will round it up to the nearest band score, which is 6.5.","As stated above, your score is rounded up or down to the nearest half point or whole point. This information may be verified on the official website.","Descriptions of IELTS Band Scores:","In the following table, you’ll find an explanation of each band score, along with details regarding the minimum required level of English proficiency for band scores 5 to 9.","Brand Score","English Level","Description","Keypoints","9","Expert User","Has perfect grasp of the English language, is correct, appropriate, very flexible, fluent, and understands everything.","2.full understanding","8","Very Good User","only occasionally makes mistakes (which are not systematic) or uses inappropriate language. able to handle in-depth discussion but rarely makes mistakes while dealing with complex problems.","2.uses complex language well","7","Good User","possesses a strong mastery of the English language but occasionally uses words improperly or inaccurately. Can speak in complex sentences and comprehends complex arguments well.","1.only occasional errors","6","Competent User","has a good command of English but occasionally makes mistakes, uses improper terms, or causes misunderstandings. can speak in complex sentences well, but works best in familiar settings.","5","Modest User","has rudimentary English proficiency and is able to deal with overall meaning Make lots of mistakes. has superior English in everyday circumstances. does not do well with difficult language."],bullets:["accurate","rare errors","uses complex language quite well in most situations","some errors","use some complex language which is best in familiar situations","frequent errors","has difficulties with complex language"]},{heading:"[content_block id=14313 slug=zoho-form]",paragraphs:["IELTS Listening and Reading Band Scores:","Your IELTS Listening band score and Reading band score are determined by how many of the 40 questions you answer correctly. You don’t lose points if you get an answer wrong.","IELTS LISTENING SCORES:","Correct Answers","Band Scores","39-40","9","37-38","8.5","35-36","8","32-34","7.5","30-31","7","26-29","6.5","23-25","6","18-22","5.5","16-17","5","13-15","4.5","11-12","4","IELTS ACADEMIC READING SCORES:","Correct AnswersBand ScoresIELTS GENERAL TRAINING READING SCORES:","40-39","9","38-37","8.5","36-35","8","34-33","7.5","32-30","7","29-27","6.5","26-23","6","22-19","5.5","18-15","5","14-13","4.5","12-10","4","9-8","3.5","7-6","3","5-4","2.5","Correct Answers","Band Scores","40","9","39","8.5","37-38","8","36","7.5","34-35","7","32-33","6.5","30-31","6","27-29","5.5","23-26","5","19-22","4.5","15-18","4","12-14","3.5","9-11","3","6-8","2.5","IELTS Writing Assessment Criteria:","Both writing tasks 1 and 2 are graded using four criteria. Please see the details below for more information on each criterion for scoring.","Task Achievement (Task1)","Presenting accurate information","Providing an overview","Highlighting key features / stages","Supporting detail with data"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Giving relevant main points which are supported & developed",paragraphs:["Giving a clear position (stating an opinion when asked)","Providing a conclusion"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Organising information / ideas into paragraphs",paragraphs:["Having one central idea in each paragraph(T2)","Using a range of linking devices","Lexical Resource","Using a range of words & paraphrasing","Using collocations","Spelling","Avoid errors","IELTS Speaking Assessment Criteria:","The IELTS speaking criteria are listed below."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Grammar Range & Accurancy",paragraphs:["Using a range of sentence structures","Using a range of grammar tenses","Punctuation","Avoid errors","Fluency","Talking at length","Talking without pauses or hesitations","Talking without self-correction","Able to be understood","Using linking devices","Lexical Resource","Using a range o words & Paraphrasing","Using collocations","Using less common vocabulary","Avoiding errors"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Grammar Range & Accurancy",paragraphs:["Using a range of sentence structures","Using a range of grammar tenses","Avoiding errors","Pronunciation","Able to be understood throughout the test"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Accent does not affect understanding",paragraphs:["Accurate word and sound pronunciation"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"cgpa-to-gpa-conversion-table-39",title:"CGPA to GPA – Conversion Table",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/cgpa-to-gpa-conversion-table/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/GPA-USA.png",intro:"What is GPA? Grade Point Average (GPA) is a word that you may encounter in both high school and college. A GPA is a figure that shows your average score. A grade point may be expressed as a number or as a letter. For example, if you receive an A, it generally stands for a number, usually a multiple of four, so if you get an A, your GPA is four, and if you get a B, the grade point drops to three, and the scale continues to move according to the grading system. Depending on the nation you are in and which institution you are enrolled in, there are several methods for calculating your grade point. Let’s look at how to determine your GPA using an example: Assume you take three courses each worth three credits.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["Subject 1- A (4 on the GPA scale)","Subject 2- C (2 on the GPA scale)"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Subject 3- B (3 on the GPA scale)",paragraphs:["Now multiply the grade by the credits to get 12 for Subject 1, 6 for Subject 2, and 9 for Subject 3. If we put these numbers together, your total grade point is 27, and if we divide it by the total credits, which is 9 (3+3+3), we obtain the number 3, indicating that your GPA is 3. As a result, credits play an essential part in calculating your grade point.","GPA to CGPA","While most institutions require candidates to submit their grades on a 10-point scale, others require candidates to submit their grades on a four-point scale. Candidates are not obliged to do the conversions directly; they are handled by third-party agencies; nevertheless, candidates may perform a preliminary calculation for reference purposes only."],bullets:[]},{heading:"GPA to CGPA Conversion Table",paragraphs:["GPA and CGPA are words that are frequently encountered in the educational sector. These words relate to various methods of grading or assigning grades to students based on their academic achievement in various disciplines. While pupils in younger courses are given marks, the grading system is popular in higher education. The misunderstanding between GPA and CGPA comes from the fact that certain schools and institutions distinguish between the two grading systems and prioritise one over the other when deciding on student admittance. Let’s examine the two grading systems in more detail.","Candidates may use the method given below to convert 10 point CGPA to 4 point GPA: if your university’s statement of marks indicates you have a CGPA of 7.0 and your batch topper’s CGPA is 8.0, you can calculate your score as:","CGPA (10 Point)","GPA (4 Point)","Grade (4 Point Scale)",">8.5",">3.7","A","8.0 – 8.4","3.7","A","7.5 – 7.9","3.3","B+","7.0 – 7.4","3","B","6.5 – 6.9","2.7","C+","6.0 – 6.4","2.3","C","5.5 – 5.9","2","D+","5.0 – 5.4","1.7","D","<5.0","0 – 1.3","F"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How to Convert GPA to CGPA?",paragraphs:["To compute CGPA, students need sum their total grades from all semesters in that subject.","Students should divide their final grade by the number of courses they attended, i.e. credit hours.","Students must add the figures that will finish and indicate the final CGPA achieved by a student in a course or in a class after subtracting the CGPA of each topic.","The Central Board of Secondary Education stated in 2017 that the 10th Board results will be in the form of CGPA rather than percentage.","The CGPA can only be in even numbers. For instance: 8, 8.2, 8.4, 8.6, 8.8, 9….","What is CGPA?","The Cumulative Grade Point Average (CGPA) is an abbreviation for the Cumulative Grade Point Average. The CGPA is the average of a student’s grade point average across all courses, except the sixth extra subject. The Central Board of Secondary Education released the CBSE Class 10 and 12 results in the form of CGPA. The CGPS is also noted on the students’ report cards.","If a student wishes to know how much of a % they received on the board test, they must convert their CGPA to Percentage. We have discussed in detail how to compute the CGPA and % from the CGPA Grades. Knowing the percentage will also assist pupils get admission to college and advance in their careers. Class 10 and 12 percentages are essential in the interview and shortlisting process."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What is the Importance of GPA and CGPA?",paragraphs:["Both GPA and CGPA are important, and they are linked to one another.","GPA is used to calculate CGPA for a topic. Students must get high GPA scores in order to achieve a higher CGPA, since CGPA is determined by adding all semester results, while GPA is obtained from a single semester.","A high GPA and CGPA is required for admission to the world’s most prestigious institutions. Some important things to remember are:","GPA should be maintained in order to get a high CGPA.","During the admissions process, colleges want a student’s CGPA and GPA.","CGPA and GPA are required for different scholarship programmes.","A good CGPA and GPA are required to apply for placements.","CGPA and GPA are also required for higher education programmes."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"cost-of-studying-abroad-in-australia-for-indian-students-40",title:"Cost of Studying abroad in Australia for Indian Students",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/cost-of-studying-abroad-in-australia-for-indian-students/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Study-in-AUS.png",intro:"Australian universities have seen a rapid increase in applications from India in recent years, owing to its world-class education, multicultural environment, and post-study career options offered for overseas students. As a country, it attracts students from all over the world for a variety of reasons. Australia, like all other countries, has some of the world’s greatest universities. These colleges’ advancement is aided by consistent innovation, strong professors, and high-quality education. While academics contribute to the call value, it may be the only country that has a pull due to its good living conditions and promise of great student life. When we decide to study abroad, one of the first questions we ask is, “How much does it cost to study in Australia?” You don’t have to be concerned! We understand that it is a significant expenditure, but with careful planning, you can realise your dream of studying abroad while staying within your budget. Don’t worry, we’ll cover most of your concerns about the Cost Of Studying Abroad In Australia For Indian Students in this blog.",sections:[{heading:"Study Abroad Australia Tuition Fees",paragraphs:["Tuition fees vary depending on the course of study and the institute. Australia has 40 public universities, two international universities, and one private university. For Indian students, education can be highly costly, costing up to $50000. Bachelor’s degree programmes cost between $20000 and $45000, while Master’s and PhD programmes cost between $22000 and $50000. Applicants should be aware that the cost of studying in Australia is closely proportional to the university chosen and the degree chosen. Applicants should be aware that these are estimates and are subject to change from time to time.","If you wish to reduce your educational costs, you could apply for one of the many scholarships offered to Indian students. Try researching about the Australia Awards, APEC Women in Research Fellowship Program and Endeavor PG Scholarship Awards. Apart from the aforementioned scholarships, various universities in Australia provide a variety of postgraduate and undergraduate scholarships to Indian students. Don’t worry if you get stuck somewhere; the education experts at One Window will be pleased to assist you."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Study Abroad Australia Accommodation Costs",paragraphs:["Most Australian institutions provide on-campus housing for international students. You can also choose off-campus housing, where you can share an apartment with other Indian students or stay with an Australian family. Rent is normally paid in advance every two weeks. Keep in mind that rental prices differ from city to city."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Hostels and Guesthouses- AUD 4320 – 7200 a year",paragraphs:["Shared Rental – AUD 4560 – 10320 a year","On-campus – AUD 5280- 13440 a year","Homestay- AUD 11280- 15600a year"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Boarding schools – AUD 11000 – 22000 a year",paragraphs:["Please keep in mind that the cost of living in Australia is highly dependent on the preferences and lifestyles of students. A luxury lifestyle would result in a high cost of living, whereas a moderate to modest lifestyle would result in lower costs of living.","[content_block id=14313 slug=zoho-form]"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Miscellaneous Expenses",paragraphs:["While tuition costs in Australia account for the majority of international students’ expenses, we must also include other expenses that are directly tied to tuition costs. This is a new section that we’ve created to offer students a realistic idea of how much studying in the country will cost. These are frequently the various expenses that a student must consider while enrolling in a university. These charges are crucial and go hand in hand with tuition prices because they are mandatory.","General living expenses in Australia are $21,041 per year, according to the Australian government. An Indian student’s annual budget ranges from $20,000 to $27,00 (given your lifestyle choices). A student’s average annual travel expense is approximately AUD 2000. A student’s average annual travel expense is approximately AUD 2000."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"9-cheapest-countries-for-indian-students-to-study-abroad-2022-list-41",title:"9+ Cheapest Countries for Indian Students to Study Abroad-2022 list",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/9-cheapest-countries-for-indian-students-to-study-abroad-2022-list/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/9-countries.png",intro:"It is feasible to study abroad on a budget, believe it or not! Some of the best places to study abroad also have inexpensive, if not non-existent, tuition fees. Here’s everything you need to know about the top ten cheapest countries for Indian students to study abroad. Czech Republic In the Czech Republic, you can benefit from inexpensive tuition and living costs.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["You can study for free in public universities if you have a strong command of the Czech language, as degrees offered in Czech do not require a tuition charge. The average tuition for English-taught degrees at public institutions ranges from 0 to 20,110 EUR per year, depending on the university and specialisation.","The tuition fee is the same whether you are an EU/EEA student or a non-EU/EEA student. Private institutions normally charge higher tuition since they can charge anything they want.","When compared to most Western European countries, living expenditures in the Czech Republic are significantly lower. Students spend an average of 315 to 675 EUR per month on all expenses, including housing, food, transportation, and social activities.","Norway","Norway is one of the most affordable countries for Indian students to study in. Its public schools are free to all students. Yes! Yes, you read that correctly. In Norway, there are no tuition fees. That does not, however, imply that you can remain there for free. Living in Norway can be a bit excessive in actuality. To join the student union, you must pay between Rs.3000 and Rs.5000. The student union fee includes things like health, cultural events, and counselling.","An individual’s monthly expenses in Norway range from Rs.60,000 to Rs.100,000.","Germany","Germany, like Norway, provides free education to all citizens (including bachelor’s and master’s degrees). Keep in mind, however, that this only applies to public universities. They demand an “administrative fee,” which can range between Rs.10,000 and Rs.30,000. This charge, on the other hand, covers student services such as the cafeteria, transportation, and health.","Furthermore, Germany’s cost of living is comparable to that of Norway, ranging from Rs.60,000 to Rs.100,000 per month. As previously said, obtaining a degree from a European country at such a low cost is a fantasy. As a result, the country attracts a large number of visitors each year due to its high standard of living and attractive landscapes of alpines, woods, churches, and castles.","Denmark","Despite having a tiny population, Denmark is regarded as one of the world’s most promising education locations for overseas students. This is largely due to the country’s concentration on research and development. Learning from industry pros will provide you with a wealth of practical knowledge and exposure.","Tuition at Danish universities ranges from Rs. 4.5-12 lakh per year, and the cost of living in the nation ranges from Rs. 7.5-10 lakh per year.","Belgium","Belgium has long been regarded as the heart of Western Europe, thanks to its status as the official headquarters of the European Union (EU). As the EU and NATO’s headquarters, you’ll have plenty of possibilities to learn about a wide range of cultures and study in languages other than English, such as French, Dutch, and German. Graduating from a Belgian institution will improve your chances of landing an international career.","This country offers the most beautiful natural scenery and diverse culture. Being a student in Belgium allows you to quickly travel throughout Europe, with Paris, London, and Amsterdam all being only two hours away. Belgian chocolates, waffles, and beer are reason enough to visit.","Italy","With its breathtaking countryside beauty, cultural legacy, rich tradition, and delectable food, Italy is a fantastic place for those wishing to study abroad. Aside from being one of the most popular vacation locations and the epicentre of global fashion, Italy also provides international students with world-class but affordable education.","The country is home to the world’s oldest still-operating university, the University of Bologna, which was founded in 1088. It also boasts several universities ranked in the QS World University Rankings.","Spain","Thousands of international students flock to Spain each year because of the high-quality education it provides at a moderate cost. Spain is one of the most affordable places to study abroad, with some of the lowest tuition fees in Europe.","Taiwan","Taiwan, in Asia, is another of the cheapest countries to study in. Tuition rates for undergraduates at National Taiwan University, for example, start at TW$100,920 (US$3,300) per year for liberal arts programmes and rise to TW$124,200 (US$4,050) in the nation’s leading university, which is ranked 72nd in the QS World University Rankings® 2019.","This makes it one of the most affordable countries for Indian students to pursue a master’s degree. Taiwan has more than 120 English-language courses offered at more than 40 universities, and it is also a popular place to learn Mandarin. Taiwan offers a good quality of life due to its low housing costs; housing costs as little as TW $ 88,000 (US $ 2,900) per year.","Finland","Residents of the EU or EEA are entitled to free research in several European nations, including Finland, which is the finest country for Indian students to study abroad. Another alternative is to apply without paying any tuition fees, which would mean that exchange students would not have to pay foreign students’ tuition expenses. In addition, the cost of living is reasonable; the overall cost of attending university in Finland is at least $9,300 per year.","Austria","Austria’s tuition prices are reasonable for international students. Austrian universities offer dependable educational opportunities. In Austria, lodging and public transportation are both very affordable. The entire cost of studying in Austria is a minimum of $10,500 per year. The University of Vienna, Vienna University of Technology, Universität Innsbruck, and others are well-known universities. Austria is one of the top countries for Indian students to study in.","If you are truly interested in studying abroad, get in touch with us. With the right guidance from One Window’s study counsellors, you can achieve your dream of studying abroad."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"is-studying-abroad-in-uk-worth-it-for-indian-students-42",title:"Is studying abroad in UK worth it for Indian students?",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/is-studying-abroad-in-uk-worth-it-for-indian-students/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/UK.png",intro:"As Indian students, we’re often met with two questions that everyone seems to be dying to know the answer to – 1) why should I study abroad? 2) which country should I choose to study in? While these are both valid and important questions, it’s worth remembering that no matter where you choose to study abroad, there will always be advantages and disadvantages. So before you embark on your international student journey, keep these things in mind as you decide whether or not study abroad in UK is worth it for Indian students. Study Abroad India offers quality degree programs and a wide range of options to choose from. There are also plenty of opportunities to work after graduation. But is studying abroad in UK really worth it, or should you consider staying back in India instead? Here’s what you need to know",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["Study Abroad in U.K.","The Study abroad in UK campaign was a joint initiative by British Council, Department of Education and Science and Higher Education Funding Council for England. The aim of these three bodies was to urge potential overseas students to see what U.K has to offer when it comes to top class higher education. Britain offers world class universities that provide challenging courses and excellent learning environments. It is no wonder why so many people look at Britain as their first choice when it comes to study abroad destinations. Students who have studied in U.K have time and again praised its educational system; they have also had nothing but good things to say about campus life too! Studying in Britain is certainly one of those ‘must do’ kind of things if you’re looking to pursue an international degree program today!"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Cost of Studying Abroad in UK",paragraphs:["Cost is a major consideration when studying abroad. Expenses such as tuition fees, living costs, visa application fees and travel expenses must be planned and budgeted before you leave. The cost of studying varies according to where you want to study, so it’s essential to do your research first. Compare tuition fees at institutions of your choice and find out what kind of financial aid is available. The average annual fee at top universities is around £9000-£12000 (£2000-3000 per year). However, some may ask for higher tuition fees. Check whether they offer any","scholarships","that are open to international students or have an exchange program with any foreign institution. These programs can save up to 50% on your overall costs!","Living Expenses","Most of us don’t realize just how much a life overseas can cost. While tuition may be comparable, or even lower than studying at home, and overall quality may be better, there are many other costs you’ll want to plan for – especially if you want to stay living overseas after graduation. There will be extra costs added to your living expenses that come with studying abroad. Just because they weren’t included in your education doesn’t mean they won’t be part of it. Before you take any more loans out, sit down and crunch some numbers with"],bullets:[]},{heading:"One Window Study Abroad Expert",paragraphs:["—it could end up saving you serious cash down the road.","[content_block id=14313 slug=zoho-form]"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How to apply for a study visa?",paragraphs:["Applying for a study visa doesn’t have to be difficult. Just follow these steps: 1. Gather your documents 2. Choose a school 3. Apply! We know it sounds simple, but we also know that students are sometimes overwhelmed by what needs to be done and where to start. To help make studying abroad easier for you, we created an online application tool with step-by-step instructions on how to apply for a study visa. opt for our free counselling session and we will direct you through everything you need to get started on your new adventure of studying abroad in UK. Our team is available on calls or email if you have any questions along the way."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What is the duration of a study visa?",paragraphs:["If you’re planning to study abroad in UK, you’ll need a student visa. The length of time your student visa lasts depends on whether you’re studying a full-time course or part-time. Full-time courses will require a Tier 4 (General) Student Visa while part-time courses may require an extended Tier 4 (Child) Student Visa. In order to apply for these visas, you must be enrolled at a university that has been authorized by Her Majesty’s Home Office. These schools are known as Designated Schools. Currently there are over 600 Designated Schools throughout England, Scotland and Wales. It’s important to note that when studying at a non-designated school, you might still qualify for a visa but it’s important to check with your school first before applying. This is because if they aren’t listed as Designated then they aren’t allowed to offer any type of degree/qualification that can be used towards immigration purposes."],bullets:[]},{heading:"What are the documents required to apply for UK Student Visa from India?",paragraphs:["To apply for UK student visa, you will need a letter of acceptance from your school or college confirming that you have been accepted and that there is space available to study. You will also need an ID card, like a passport or driver’s license, with at least six months left before expiry. If you are under 18, parents must provide parental consent. All documents should be translated into English. Without these documents, applications may be delayed by many weeks or months."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Can you work with UK Student Visa?",paragraphs:["Yes, you can work with a student visa provided that your Tier 4 visa is not yet expired. The Tier 4 visa allows you to study full-time at a registered and certified educational institution and work part-time during vacation periods. You can check if your employer is licensed by going through their website. You will also need to inform your Tier 4 sponsor about any job offers you receive so they can report back to UKVI (UK Visas & Immigration). Your sponsor must send them official written confirmation of your employment within 14 days of being informed of it. If they fail to do so, they could lose their sponsor license which means future international students sponsored by them will have no recourse but to find another education institution as their main Tier 4 Sponsor."],bullets:[]},{heading:"International Opportunities",paragraphs:["There are lots of ways you can take advantage of studying at university level. You can immerse yourself in a new culture, broaden your horizons, and build a social network; on top of gaining valuable knowledge and experience. However, one question you should ask yourself is: is studying abroad in UK worth it? And how does that compare to staying at home and going to college back home?","Conclusion","While there are a ton of benefits to study abroad in UK. Deciding whether or not to move out of your home country can be difficult. Ultimately, you want to ask yourself if studying abroad is worth it for your career and personal goals. In many cases, moving overseas may prove to be an exceptional experience and aid you in achieving a successful future—but only if you plan ahead carefully. Think about your end goal first: do you intend on working back at home or staying overseas long-term?"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"silver-scholarship-programs-for-indian-students-to-study-abroad-2022-43",title:"Silver Scholarship Programs for Indian Students to Study Abroad – 2022",tag:"Funding",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/study-abroad-silver-scholarships-2022/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/Yale.png",intro:"Are you a student still undergoing graduation? Are you also someone who aspires to study abroad? Do you wish to have an admission opportunity to study in an Ivy League College before you get your Undergrad degree in your hands? Well, apparently some wild wishes do come true! You might not believe it, but there is a way of getting admission to Yale University for an MBA before you finish your bachelor’s. Let us tell you how.",sections:[{heading:"Yale Silver Scholars Program",paragraphs:["Yale Silver Scholars Program is a magical opportunity that paves way for a college senior to study for an MBA degree and begin a career in the booming industries of his choice."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Initiation and applicability",paragraphs:["Silver Scholars is a 3-year early admission program open to college seniors all across the globe. It aims to create a holistic environment enabling candidates hailing from different educational backgrounds to share their skills, talent, and knowledge.","The best thing about the program is that it is a contrast to the traditional style of Master’s Programs that rely on previous work experience for admitting students into the university as well as for providing job placements.","Although, the program admits only 5% of students in comparison to the rates of a traditional MBA course admission at Yale University.","Additionally, Yale Silver Scholars Program is the only one that provides an offer for an internship while you are still studying for a master’s degree."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Skills and other requirements",paragraphs:["The program does not require a yet-to-be undergraduate student to have any work experience but looks for other crucial skills such as:","Extraordinariness in college senior","Enthusiasm in learning"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Maturity in handling obstacles",paragraphs:["Active participation in co-curricular activities","Short term internships","Decent GMAT/GRE Score"],bullets:[]},{heading:"A strong story conveyed through resume and interview (if selected)",paragraphs:["[content_block id=14313 slug=zoho-form]"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Eligibility Criteria",paragraphs:["A student is supposed to apply through the full-time MBA Program admission route itself, but for availing of the silver scholar’s program he/she has to take care of the following points:","You are only eligible to apply if you are in the third year of your undergraduate course."],bullets:[]},{heading:"You must provide two letters of recommendation: one from an academic instructor/ faculty member for your performance  and",paragraphs:["Another from an employer (in case of part-time job/ internship) or an organization where you actively volunteered in/ performed any extra-curricular activity.","Application form","Things to include in your application:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Official transcripts of your undergraduate course-two certified copies",paragraphs:["Copies of previously secured degrees, if any.","Curriculum Vitae","GMAT/GRE Score","TOEFL/IELTS Results","Two letters of recommendations"],bullets:[]},{heading:"One creatively drafted main essay",paragraphs:["For guidance on the application filing process and help in drafting the essay, reach out to one of our experts at (hyperlink)","Selection Process","Best applications are picked after a thorough analysis of the candidate’s profile. The selected candidates are called for an interview which can be in-person or through video-calling means as chosen by the applicant.","The interview majorly focuses on the ambitions, interests, and skills of the candidate.","Selected candidates receive an offer letter from the Yale University Office."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Yale Silver Scholars Program- Structure",paragraphs:["The program admits you to a 3 year MBA course. We have compiled the details separately for each year, mentioned below:","Year One","The candidates in the first year of the program share the classroom with fellow undergraduates coming from different educational as well as cultural backgrounds.","They are introduced to a very unconventional study syllabus and are encouraged to develop efficient management skills.","They are offered to learn a palette of core courses from prominent faculties.","These courses are:","Introduction to Negotiation","Power and Politics","Managing Groups and Teams","Global Virtual Teams","Basics of Accounting"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Modeling Managerial Decisions",paragraphs:["Probability Modelling and Statistics","Basics of Economics","Year Two","With the theoretical knowledge gained in the first year of the program, candidates are offered a full-time internship where they apply the same to practice real-life scenarios. This helps them shape their skills to secure a successful career in the future. These internships are provided at various reputed organizations, some of which are Pfizer, Boston Consultancy Group, Coca Cola,","Motorola and CARE.","Year Three","Post the second-year internship, the third-year MBA program commences wherein the students choose their electives at Yale as well as other global universities for Advanced Management in areas that complement the career path they have chosen so far.","All students must meet at least one of the following Global Studies Requirements which are:","Taking a course in International Experience, by spending ten days in a location situated overseas to understand the global economy"],bullets:[]},{heading:"a mini-course for Advanced Management hosted by one of the member schools in the Global Network",paragraphs:["Completing an online GNC (Global Network Course) a virtual course to look at a cross-national business problem in which students work with a global team","Selecting other approved electives that involve international travel and study"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Access to career advisors and their guidance in the building profession",paragraphs:["Opportunity to network with talented people","Our Word","There are many other benefits to this program and ways you can easily apply for the same. We would love to help you out with your doubts and queries about the same. Book an appointment with one of our","expert consultants here","to know more!"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"study-abroad-scholarships-and-grants-to-apply-for-in-2022-44",title:"Study Abroad Scholarships and Grants to Apply for in 2022",tag:"Funding",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/study-abroad-scholarships-and-grants-to-apply-for-in-2022/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/Scholarship.png",intro:"With the COVID-19 restrictions finally loosening, a large number of countries have opened their doors again for people to learn, seek career opportunities and grow in a plethora of fields. Students aspiring to build a career abroad find it very difficult to build one with the high costs of living, hefty tuition fees of world-known universities, and travel expenditures. But you need not worry! We are here with a list of Scholarships and Grants by various reputed universities and colleges for the year 2022-2023 that you can apply for and avail benefits that make it easier for you to fill the canvas of your dreams with flying colors of success. Listed below are some of the scholarships that are most beneficial and convenient to apply for Indian students.",sections:[{heading:"The Inlaks Shivdashani Scholarships",paragraphs:["The top universities in the US, Europe, and the UK grant the Inlaks Shivdashani Scholarships.","These scholarships are especially granted to the young minds of India aspiring to study for a Master’s degree or a full-time doctorate program.","Required qualifications:","Mathematics, Environment, and similar subjects:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"minimum 70 percentile/ 7.2 CGPA or GPA 2.8 from a recognized Indian university",paragraphs:["Humanities, fine arts, social science, law, architecture:","minimum 65 percentile/ 6.8 CGPA/GPA 2.6 from a recognized Indian university","Age limit:","maximum 30 years","Minimum duration of residence in India: 6 months","Aids provided:","100% tuition fee, health allowance, one-way travel allowance.","Living expenditure","Maximum funding : USD 1.00,000.","To know more:","https://www.inlaksfoundation.org/scholarships/how-to-apply/"],bullets:[]},{heading:"GREAT Scholarships India",paragraphs:["To encourage the world to visit, study and do business with the UK, the government has introduced the GREAT Britain Campaign to stimulate jobs and growth.","The campaign also funds 20 scholarships for Indian students of the same name along with the British Council and participating UK Universities.","Required qualifications:","Students should be a citizen of India."],bullets:[]},{heading:"(More are listed on the official document linked below)",paragraphs:["[content_block id=14313 slug=zoho-form]","Aids provided:","100% tuition fee, health allowance, one-way travel allowance.","Living expenditure","Maximum funding : USD 1.00,00.","To know more:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"https://www.britishcouncil.in/study-uk/scholarships/great-scholarships",paragraphs:["To get our help in applying for GREAT Scholarship to study in the UK, get in touch with one of our experts at","One Window","."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Commonwealth Scholarships",paragraphs:["Commonwealth Ph.D. Scholarships, Commonwealth Split-site Scholarships, Commonwealth Shared Scholarships, Commonwealth Master’s Scholarships, Commonwealth Distance Learning Scholarships, and Commonwealth Medical and Professional Fellowships are some of the Commonwealth Scholarships offered to students of Commonwealth Countries to study in the UK.","Required qualifications:","The student should be a citizen/ permanent resident of Commonwealth Country.","Undergraduate degree of upper second class.","Appropriate Master’s Degree for students applying for PhD","Aids provided:","100% tuition fee, two-way travel allowance.","Living expenditure"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Fulbright Scholarship",paragraphs:["Since 1946, foreign Students from around 155 Countries are being provided with Fulbright Scholarships to study in United States Universities and Academic Institutions.","Scholarships are offered for Masters and PhDs in courses from all fields including interdisciplinary courses for varying periods depending on the selected program.","Required qualifications:","US Bachelor’s Degree or equivalent (minimum 55% marks).","The student should demonstrate leadership skills.","Highly motivated.","Aids provided:","100% tuition fee.","Two-way travel allowance for economy class air travel.","Living expenditure.","Accident and sickness coverage.","J-1 Visa Support."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Application deadline",paragraphs:["16th May, 2022 (Fulbright-Nehru Master’s Fellowships)","To know more:","https://www.usief.org.in/uploadip/ip2023-24/Fellowships_for_Indian_Citizens_2023-2024_brochure.pdf"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Yale Silver Scholars Program",paragraphs:["This program is a unique opportunity for Indian graduates to pursue an MBA Degree from the very highly reputed Yale University. The program along with the degree courses also paves way for securing internships at various organizations like BCG, Pfizer, Time Inc., and more.","Required qualifications:","Active participation in Extra-Curricular activities","Internship experience"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Decent GMAT/GRE Score",paragraphs:["Combination of intelligence and common sense, maturity and curiosity, passion and compassion","To know more:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"https://som.yale.edu/programs/mba/admissions/silver-scholars",paragraphs:["To get our help in applying for Yale Silver Scholars Program, get in touch with one of our experts at One Window."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Erasmus Mundus Scholarships",paragraphs:["7 world-class and internationally renowned universities, namely the University of Glasgow, University of Barcelona, Uppsala University, Erasmus University Rotterdam, University of Gottingen, Universidad de Los Andes, and Kyoto University offer to teach an International Master’s Degree to students from all over the world.","The Course is Global Markets, Local Creativities (ERASMUS MUNDUS INTERNATIONAL MASTER) IntM.","Required qualifications:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Quality of (academic) references (10% weightage)",paragraphs:["Other (eg. Relevant research/ Professional experience/ Interests) (20% weightage)","Aids provided:","Tuition fees","Full Insurance Coverage"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Other mandatory participation costs",paragraphs:["Student travel and installation costs","Monthly Subsistence Allowance","To know more:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"University of New South Wales (UNSW Sydney) Future of Change India Scholarship",paragraphs:["These Scholarships are offered to Indian students willing to take up full-time undergraduate or postgraduate courses at UNSW.","Required qualifications",":","Academic Merit","Citizenship of India","Residents of India"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Must have applied and received an offer of admission* into a UNSW Undergraduate or Postgraduate coursework degree program",paragraphs:["[content_block id=14313 slug=zoho-form]","Aids provided:","$10,000 per annum paid as a stipend for a minimum duration of the program","To know more:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"https://www.news.futurestudents.unsw.edu.au/india-scholarships?gclsrc=aw.ds",paragraphs:["Although this article sums up quite a lot of information, we do understand that the Scholarship Application process can be a complicated one. Book a one-on-one talk session with one of our experts at","One Window","and let us help you have a hassle-free application experience!"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"can-you-study-dentistry-abroad-without-clearing-neet-45",title:"Can you study dentistry abroad without clearing NEET?",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/can-you-study-dentistry-abroad-without-clearing-neet/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Dentist.png",intro:"It was not long ago that the world was hit by a global pandemic and the need for more doctors, dentists and paramedics increased manifolds. The importance of and inclination towards studying dentistry as a field of Medical Science is as much as that of studying MBBS, if not more. Being a dentist not only brings a lot of repute but also is a crucial branch of Medical Science. To become a dentist in India, a student has to appear for NEET after passing the 12th standard.",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["This article covers everything you need to know about the exam, and also has answers to if you can study dentistry abroad without clearing NEET.","What is NEET?","NEET is the abbreviation for The National Eligibility Entrance Test which is the qualifying test for MBBS and BDS Programmes in Indian Medical and Dental Colleges. Conducted and looked over by the National Testing Agency, this test was formerly called the All India Pre-Medical Test (AIPMT). To conclude NEET is the gateway to entering the field of Medical Sciences and a good NEET Score is one to getting admission to a good medical school/college.","Modifications in requirements and its Impact.","Earlier, students willing to study BDS abroad were not obligated to sit in the NEET Examinations as some medical schools/colleges/universities granted admissions that were not based on NEET Scores. A student who aspired to study BDS from an Indian college was supposed to take a NEET Examination compulsorily.","But, as per the notice of the Dental Council of India, rolled out on September 5th, 2018, the eligibility criteria to pursue undergraduate dental courses abroad were made stringent by making NEET a compulsory test that had to be taken if a student were to become eligible to obtain “Eligibility Certificate” from the Dental Council of India (DCI) and practice dentistry.","Attached above is the official notice. If you are an aspirant/qualified student/ professional from abroad having trouble interpreting the notice, feel free to reach out to us at","040-42006447","and we shall be happy to help!"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Impact of update on Aspirants",paragraphs:["Now that we know about the new mandates and requirements for getting admission to medical colleges/universities abroad, it seems to be harder than before to be able to enroll in a field that was never easy before and now has become even more difficult than ever!","But, here are our two cents on the whole issue, and our advice on how to tackle it.","First of all, the option of not appearing for NEET was desirable as it saved the students from facing disappointment in the form of a low score due to poor performance.","Secondly, most students prefer studying in India itself since they are not aware of the benefits of studying dentistry abroad."],bullets:[]},{heading:"The course of Action for students appearing for NEET",paragraphs:["If the new condition worries you, One Window is here to help you with a brilliant strategy to successfully enroll for admission to a good medical college/university abroad and secure your career and future in a few simple steps!","Prepare for NEET with complete dedication and without a doubt. Honestly, if you score well, you have the world’s worth to achieve and if you don’t you’ve got nothing to lose. Step in to prepare for your exams with this mindset and take it easy.","Appear for exams and go through the examination process calmly.","Look into the benefits of studying dentistry abroad before you start preparing or while you wait for your results."],bullets:[]},{heading:"medical schools/colleges/universities abroad",paragraphs:["that admit Indian students and accept NEET Scores but have a low cut-off rate.","Trust us, there are plenty of reputed colleges and universities abroad that have a very low NEET Score cut-off rate waiting for brilliant minds to take admission with them and make them into exceptional professionals!","Benefits of Studying Dentistry Abroad!","Since most students do not know of the benefits, we are here to list down all the advantages of getting admission to a medical college abroad:","Quality Medical Education facility is provided to Indian students.","Admission to these colleges does not require a deposit of any capitation fee.","The study environment is quite safe and engaging.","The highly advanced and modern infrastructure of International Universities.","Affordable accommodation and food costs.","Bright career opportunities for medical graduates.","Option to go for Post graduation and MD courses with ease.","Not only WHO & MCI but also many global bodies have recognized medical universities located in different nations."],bullets:[]},{heading:"[content_block id=14313 slug=zoho-form]",paragraphs:["Which Countries (and colleges) are best for students aspiring to study Dentistry abroad?","Sweden","Sweden has universities that specialize in dentistry whose education system has a global presence in the world and the medical industry in the country is quite advanced.","Karolinska Institute","The University of Gothenburg","Malmo University","China","China offers a world-class education in dental training because it has clinical learning & teaching facilities that are commendable and the technology most modern in the world.","Some of the best colleges are :","Hong Kong University","Peking University","Sichuan University","Hong Kong University","Belgium","Belgium offers a very high salary rate to dentists and an efficient education system. With some of the world’s top-ranked universities situated in it, Belgium provides education for very cheap tuition fees.","Some Universities are:","KU Leuven","Ghent University","University of Liege"],bullets:[]},{heading:"University of Liege",paragraphs:["With these steps, any student can land admission and pursue a successful career with a decent learning experience in a reputed medical college/university abroad.","The options are not limited to just these countries and listed universities, there are many different options per your performance and preferences— to know more about them reach out to us at"],bullets:[]},{heading:"https://ankitjain2.zohobookings.in/#/customer/counselling",paragraphs:["and chalk out a bright future education plan for you with us!"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"list-of-best-law-courses-in-canada-after-12th-for-indian-students-colleges-eligibility-ranking-46",title:"List of Best Law Courses in Canada after 12th for Indian Students: Colleges, Eligibility & Ranking",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/list-of-best-law-courses-in-canada-after-12th-for-indian-students-colleges-eligibility-ranking/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Law-CAD.png",intro:"Students who want to study abroad in Canada can go through our list of best law courses in Canada after 12th for Indian students. One Window provides the information about overseas education in Canada, India student visa process and ranking of various colleges from across Canada which offer excellent courses like Bachelor of Laws (LLB), Bachelor of Commerce (BCom), Bachelors in Business Administration (BBA), etc. For more details, Read.",sections:[{heading:"Explore Top Colleges",paragraphs:["To study abroad on a student visa to Canada or to find higher education courses at overseas universities and colleges that accept international students who want to pursue law studies , consider doing a bit of research about various Canadian educational institutions offering law courses. To help you with your search, we’ve curated a list of some good colleges and universities offering law courses both at bachelors and post-graduate levels. For more details about these top law schools, read on.","Course Details","A foreign student requires a study permit if they want to study at a university, college or other type of school in Canada. However, they are not required to hold a valid study permit while they’re studying at a designated learning institution (DLI). International students planning on doing any kind of coursework or training that takes more than six months will require an open work permit. You cannot apply for a work permit through your Study Permit; you have to apply separately by creating an account on Global Affairs Canada’s website."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Top-Ranked University In Toronto (Canada)",paragraphs:["The University of Toronto is Canada’s top-ranked university, placed ninth overall on a list of 75 Best Global Universities by U.S. News and World Report. According to Forbes Magazine (2018), it is also North America’s third best value for international students."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Eligibility for admission in university of Toronto",paragraphs:["Undergraduate applicants should have a minimum C+ (73% if using a 4-point scale) average or equivalent as determined by their high school and faculty. For example: A 73% average is required to be eligible for most Arts and Science courses. A 79% average is required to be eligible for most Engineering courses. You are also encouraged to see How do I calculate my overall average? here on our website.","[content_block id=14313 slug=zoho-form]"],bullets:[]},{heading:"The University Of Calgary (Canada)",paragraphs:["The University of Calgary is a public research university located in Calgary, Alberta. Founded by royal charter on September 1, 1966, and originally operated out of two buildings on land leased from the City of Calgary before moving to its current location."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Eligibility for admission in university of Calgary (Canada)",paragraphs:["The prerequisite for admission to graduate studies at University of Calgary is a bachelor’s degree from an accredited university or college. A grade point average (GPA) of at least 3.0 on a 4.0 scale is also required; however, admission will be granted without regard to GPA if an applicant has completed an honours program with distinction and/or has demonstrated exceptional academic achievement in their field."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Ryerson University (Canada)",paragraphs:["Ryerson offers a four-year undergraduate program that can be completed entirely on-campus. However, most students choose to study abroad through Ryerson’s global exchange program; its partners include some prestigious universities such as University College London (UCL), Hanyang University (South Korea) and Imperial College London (UK). The duration of each exchange program varies—students can typically spend a semester or two at their overseas partner university."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Eligibility for admission in Ryerson university",paragraphs:["The eligibility criteria differ from program to program. Some programs require a minimum admission average (GPA) while others rely on marks obtained at individual subjects. In addition to these entry requirements, you will also need to meet Ryerson’s English language requirements (minimum TOEFL score 600 or IELTS 5.5 band)."],bullets:[]},{heading:"York University (Canada)",paragraphs:["York University is a public research university in Toronto, Ontario, Canada. It is Canada’s third-largest university. York’s main campus is located in North York, an urban centre and one of five municipalities within the City of Toronto. The university has two satellite campuses: Keele Campus near Newcastle upon Tyne, England; and Glendon College at Glendon College (in Toronto). The campuses are approximately 150 kilometres (93 mi) apart."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Eligibility for admission in York university (Canada)",paragraphs:["The student should have attained minimum 60% aggregate marks in 10+2/Intermediate. In case he/she has not completed 10+2/Intermediate then he/she must have passed class XII from a recognized Board with minimum 45% aggregate marks and an eligible candidate needs to be well conversant with English language. The applicants who are appearing at final year examination can also apply but they will not be considered till their results are declared."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Université de Montréal (Canada)",paragraphs:["The Université de Montréal (UdeM) is a public research university in Montreal, Québec. It was established on 1 July 1965 following the merger of Laval University and Université de Montréal. Its campus is located on 132 hectares in Montréal’s Quartier Latin (Latin Quarter), adjacent to Montreal’s historic cultural neighbourhood."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Dalhousie University (Canada)",paragraphs:["Dalhousie University (Dal) is a public research university located in Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada. Dalhousie offers more than 4,000 courses and 180 degree programs in twelve undergraduate, graduate and professional faculties. In April 2016, 834 bachelor’s degrees were awarded. The annual tuition for international students at Dalhousie is CA$23,836; however please note that cost of living will vary depending on your lifestyle choices."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Eligibility for admission in Dalhousie university (Canada)",paragraphs:["An applicant must complete their 10+2 or equivalent from a recognized board with marks at minimum 50% aggregate (45% in case of candidates belonging to reserved category). No particular subjects are mandatory for applying for any particular program. But application form will be rejected if any subject combination is entered which exceeds 20 credits.","Conclusion","For Indian students seeking education abroad, obtaining a student visa for Canada has always been quite easy. Each year a large number of students from India apply and get admissions to study at one of top law courses in Canada. No doubt that Canadian universities are among some of most reputed and oldest universities in North America with campuses spread across 8 cities from Vancouver to Halifax. The University admission process is generally straightforward but like any other foreign country they have their own rules and regulations that you need to follow while applying."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"what-are-the-common-myths-of-studying-abroad-myths-busted-47",title:"What are the common myths of studying abroad? Myths Busted !",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/what-are-the-common-myths-of-studying-abroad-myths-busted/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Myths.png",intro:"Many of us wish to study in other countries. The prospect of walking into a foreign university campus and experiencing a new country is both terrifying and thrilling. Many people have the opportunity to realise their dreams and achieve a lot, but other people never try. It’s difficult to make such a momentous decision, and these typical study abroad myths don’t help. We’ve refuted the most common ones to clear the air. Scroll down and debunk these common myths of studying abroad with us!",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["Studying abroad is just too expensive!","Whether you’re studying locally or across the globe, you can expect to spend a lot of money. Well, that is what most people think when they first come across the term “","Studying Abroad.","” The good news is that international education is growing more popular, and more financial aid options are becoming available, as well as countries that provide low-cost education. One of the most common misconceptions about studying abroad is that you must have a solid financial background.","Even though it may appear to be one of the most difficult aspects of studying abroad, many institutions now provide scholarships specifically for international students. It’s only a matter of scouting them out and signing up for them. Inquire about any additional scholarships that may be available from the foreign department of the university you desire to attend. Contact One Window now to know more about such financial aids and give your dreams the boost that they deserve."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Studying in a new country requires you to learn a new language",paragraphs:["When it comes to foreign programmes, this fallacious myth is often cited as one of the most difficult aspects of studying abroad, but our study abroad statistics prove otherwise! You’re probably thinking of taking an English-taught course when you travel overseas. So, in most circumstances, learning the native language of the country (if it isn’t English) isn’t necessary.","Because most foreign colleges place a strong emphasis on attracting international students, English-taught programmes are becoming increasingly widespread. Having said that, even knowing a few basic phrases in the native language of that country can provide you with a far more real study abroad experience, allowing you to immerse yourself deeper in the culture and communicate with people more confidently.","You can’t study abroad if you are an introvert.","It will motivate you to open up if you are an introvert. Communication skills will improve as a result of meeting strangers and developing friends.","It has nothing to do with being an introvert when it comes to studying abroad. It’s not even close to being handicapped. When it comes to interacting, introverts are quite capable. Many people, especially when they travel overseas, become extroverts in no time.","Studying abroad is a free pass to party hard in a foreign country!","It all comes down to studying, learning, and honing vital abilities. Without a doubt, some students regard studying abroad as a holiday, to party, drink, and hook up with other students. Some programmes are more akin to glorified holidays, transporting busloads of international students from one attraction to the next. But times have changed, and studying abroad has become so much more. It broadens one’s horizons while also providing a plethora of personal and professional changes. While it should be enjoyable, it is also a serious undertaking that will test pupils on a personal level and affect their academic careers. Studying abroad entails enrolling in classes, preferably given by local academics on topics that are relevant to the area — and, if feasible, in the local language.","Students will study various educational systems and instructional methods. Once they’ve had an opportunity to see things from a different perspective, they’ll find themselves questioning their opinions and notions. Students will learn to respect diverse cultures and solve challenges in an environment that is different from their own."],bullets:[]},{heading:"[content_block id=14313 slug=zoho-form]",paragraphs:["Studying abroad is not safe anymore.","Well, have you ever been the street and out of nowhere seen people tripping over, walking in your drawing room where you have been forever and still getting your feet to get hit by the leg of the sofa, well I have and trust me when I say this, no place is safe if that is something you are afraid of? It’s a big bad world out there and you have to step out and face it.","Don’t allow that to keep you from discovering a whole new world and gaining access to a wealth of information.","Employers don’t pay much attention to whether you had studied abroad or not.","Studying abroad can help you develop some of the most employable skills.","Employers are increasingly looking for personnel that are culturally aware and can communicate in another language. Of course, there are many ways to acquire these skills, but studying abroad will allow you to not only improve in these areas, but also to gain marketable international qualifications, demonstrate your ability to work with a diverse range of people, and demonstrate that you dare to move to a new location and challenge yourself.","However, you cannot simply state on your CV that you “study overseas.” You must demonstrate and market yourself to potential employers, outlining what you learned, how the experience made you a better person, and how this would affect the ideas you have for a new career."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Let us know if you have any questions about studying abroad, you can avail a",paragraphs:["FREE !! 30 Minute session with Our STUDY ABROAD education Counsellors","."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"top-5-universities-of-canadato-complete-your-masters-48",title:"Top 5 Universities of Canadato Complete Your Masters",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/top-5-universities-of-canada-to-complete-your-masters-from/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/masters-CAD.png",intro:"Canadian universities have traditionally had a strong reputation for both teaching and research. Have you seen what they’re doing in terms of telecom and cyber research? Foreign schools and institutes highly regard the top universities in Canada. These top universities in Canada continue to attract the best and brightest students to their distinguished degree programs. All of these top-ranked colleges, universities, law schools, medical schools, and engineering institutions have a worldwide reputation and continue to be the most popular universities among international students. If you want to study at some of the greatest universities in the world for your Master’s degree, Canada is the place to go. It has the highest rankings and the best reputation. We at OneWindow",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["have curated the ” Top 5 universities in Canada ” that you must look for to seek admission in."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Cost to study in top universities in Canada",paragraphs:["International students must assess their financial budgets before beginning the process because of the expenditures associated with studying abroad. The cost of pursuing an MS in Canada comprises one-time pre-arrival fees, tuition fees, and living expenses.","The average annual tuition charge for graduate"],bullets:[]},{heading:"education in Canada",paragraphs:["is between 20,000 and 30,000 CAD. The cost of a master’s degree in a Canadian university ranges from 11.11 lakhs to 16.6 lakhs INR for Indian students.","The cost of living in Canada is around 2% lower than in the United States. A few states, on the other hand, may have substantially lower costs. The average monthly accommodation expense is between 850 and 1,000 Canadian dollars per year.","Top Universities for Masters in Canada","1","."],bullets:[]},{heading:"University of Toronto",paragraphs:["Since its founding in 1827, the University of Toronto has grown to become Canada’s leading educational institution. It is a historic structure with over 100 years of discovery and knowledge creation. With a strong desire to invent and innovate, it is renowned to be one of the top research-intensive universities in the world. Also known as UToronto, this public research university is a great place for intensive learning. It is a global leader in research and education, with a substantial presence in both Canada and the rest of the world. It is the only Canadian university to be ranked among the top 20 universities in the World University Rankings 2022."],bullets:[]},{heading:"[content_block id=14313 slug=zoho-form]",paragraphs:["The University of British Columbia (UBC) is a world-class teaching, learning, and research institution. It has continuously been placed among the top 20 public institutions in the world. As one of the world’s best universities, they have a critical role to play in making the world a better place. It implies that UBC offers a diverse range of job options: it’s not your usual workday. With such diversity, the people must also be distinct. It isn’t a cookie-cutter atmosphere where anything goes. People with a vision, a spark, tenacity, and enthusiasm are the ones that survive.","McGill University is one of Canada’s most well-known colleges and universities, as well as one of the world’s most prestigious universities. Their student body is the most internationally varied of any research-intensive university in Canada, with students hailing from over 150 countries.","This University is dedicated to recognizing and nurturing bright minds since its founding in 1887. With ground-breaking approaches to teaching and learning, as well as world-class interdisciplinary research. McMaster University, which is now ranked #4 in Canada, is one of the Canadian universities to routinely rank among the top 100 universities in the world.","5","."],bullets:["The University of British Columbia, Vancouver","McGill University, Montréal","McMaster University, Hamilton"]},{heading:"University of Alberta, Edmonton",paragraphs:["The University of Alberta in Edmonton is a leading teaching and research university in Canada. With a worldwide reputation for excellence in the humanities, sciences, creative arts, business, engineering, and health sciences. Its graduates have the highest employment rate in Canada and the globe. Thanks to a variety of internship and work experience programs, as well as job shadowing and career mentorship options.","These Canadian universities have been ranked based on their overall rankings in the"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Best Global Universities list",paragraphs:[". Rating was done by members of the academic community from around the world & within North America, based on their research performance. These are Canada’s top international universities.","To know more about the admission process, scholarships and unlock the world of global education and a better future,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Get in touch with One Window",paragraphs:[". Our counsellors will help you in each step and pave the way to get into your dream university."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"meaning-of-gpa-its-importance-while-seeking-admissions-in-the-usa-49",title:"Meaning of GPA & Its Importance While Seeking Admissions in the USA",tag:"Admissions",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/meaning-of-gpa-its-importance-while-seeking-admissions-in-the-usa/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/GPA-USA.png",intro:"What is GPA? W ithout knowing meaning of GPA, Many times! students get stuck with GPA. They can’t help but wonder what is a grade point 3 or a grade point 4 and how will this help them in securing admission in/their dream university in the USA. With this guide about “",sections:[{heading:"The guide",paragraphs:["Meaning of  GPA”","One Window has all the info you need to go through to understand the meaning and importance of GPA, scroll down now!","The meaning of","“","grade point average","“","is abbreviated as GPA. In the United States, it is a common method of assessing academic achievement. It goes like this: Depending on the content of the course, each one is assigned a set amount of “units” or “credits.” Most courses in high school have the same amount of units, however, this is not the case in college. Most college courses have a three-unit load (about three hours of lecture per week and six hours of homework per semester), however, the number can range from fractions of one to more than five. GPA is calculated using an A, B, C, D, and F grading scale. Several grade points are allocated to each grade.","An A grade is worth four points, whereas a B is worth three, a C is worth two, a D is worth one, and an F is worth none. Whether you’re working on secondary or college grades, the method is the same.","Check :","CGPA to GPA conversion table","to convert your score now !"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How does your GPA work?",paragraphs:["Your Grade Point Average is a score used to evaluate your accomplishment throughout your degree program. In the same manner that your professors and instructors give you a grade to evaluate your progress or success in their course.","Average GPA","is a statistic that represents how well you did in your classes throughout the semester, term, and year. Your GPA will fluctuate throughout your time at university and will be affected by how well you improve your total grades (or, in some cases, how much you fell behind)."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Comparison of GPA to Indian Grading System",paragraphs:["In India, we simply divide the scores into distinctions and divisions, with distinctions indicating great performance, the first division indicating satisfactory performance, and so on. The standards for your grades in America are also defined. A GPA of 2.0 or higher is usually required to pass a course. Less than that is deemed a failing grade, and you will have to retake the course. This is not standard, and it varies amongst universities, institutions, and schools.","A minimum GPA of 2.75 may be required by some colleges or professional courses. In general, anything above 3.75 is considered good, 2.75–3.75 is considered normal, and less than 2.5 is considered poor.","Check out","How much GPA is Required for Admission in UMBC","?"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Why is GPA so Significant?",paragraphs:["When a university admissions committee examines your GPA, the first question that springs to mind is. –","“","What kind of a student are you","?","” A higher GPA says a lot about you, whereas a lower GPA paints a negative picture in their minds.","However, this does not imply that all students with lower GPAs are not efficient or good enough to study overseas. Or that they would never have the opportunity to do so. Several students"],bullets:[]},{heading:"approach our counsellors",paragraphs:["with a desire to pursue higher education overseas. Who, for whatever reason, we’re unable to achieve higher grades in high school. Because of their hard work and perseverance, students can gain admission not just to prestigious institutions abroad, but also to good universities in the United States.","The grade point average (GPA) is a statistic used by most organizations and institutions to assess your performance. When you apply for a study visa in a specific nation, the visa officer may query your low GPA. And be skeptical of your sincere intentions to study abroad. In such instances, you don’t need to force him or her to believe you; instead, you can handle the matter more patiently and effectively.","When applying for education, such as a postgraduate degree in another country, your undergraduate grades are not your only option. GPA in 10th or 12th grade is taken into account, so make sure the grades are good."],bullets:[]},{heading:"GPA is not The Ultimate Game Changer",paragraphs:["It’s not always possible for GPA to rule the roost! A low GPA does not imply that the student lacks creativity or intelligence. There are a variety of explanations for a person’s low GPA; it could be family problems or emotional distress, resulting in a poor GPA in his final-year exams.","Certain factors, such as your résumé, SOP, LOR, and test scores, will count more than your GPA, once you  move on to further study and careers. As a result, there are other ways to demonstrate your ability, and a high GPA isn’t the only option.","What is your grade point average (GPA) and"],bullets:[]},{heading:"where do you wish to study abroad?",paragraphs:["Please tell us about your qualifications. One Window shall surely help you with your international admissions."],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"hdfc-credila-education-loan-for-study-abroad-50",title:"HDFC Credila Education Loan for Study Abroad",tag:"Funding",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/hdfc-credila-education-loan-for-study-abroad/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/hdfc-loan.png",intro:"HDFC credila education loan allow for individuals, at undergraduate as well as more advanced academic stages, to pursue their educational goals without having to stress about finances. A trusted education loan provider such as HDFC Credilacan help you realise your dreams of studying overseas. With student loans from HDFC Credila, thousands of Indian students arepursuing 1800 courses in3400 academic institutes located across 50 countries.",sections:[{heading:"Advantages on Offer",paragraphs:["Read on to understand the advantages an education loan from","HDFC Credila","provides:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Sanction Letter before Admission",paragraphs:["– You can get a sanction letter for your education loan prior to being admitted to the academic institution of your choice. This makes for a great incentive, as your university may need to see proof of your ability to finance your education.","Tax Benefits","– Under Section 80E of the Income Tax Act, 1961, there is no upper cap on the interest paid on an education loan*. A loan from HDFC Credila is advantageous, as it is the sole non-banking financial company in India to offer this attractive tax benefit. Know more about the applicable tax savings, by taking advantage of the tax calculator available on the HDFC Credila website."],bullets:[]},{heading:"All Expenses Covered",paragraphs:["– Beyond your tuition fees, you also need to account for your living expenses along with ancillary costs, such as travel expenses, purchasing a laptop etc. A student loan from HDFC Credila includes these expenses, thus taking away any possible stress around finances.","No Margin Money","– What serves as perhaps the most pertinent aspect of academic loans brought forth by HDFC Credila is the fact that the loan does not require you to chip in any margin money, something a lot of other lenders mandate."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Transparency in Processing",paragraphs:["– As a parent, you might be concerned about who your child seeks to avail an education loan from. HDFC Credilais one of the most trusted entities operating within the realm of financial services. You need not worry about getting a loan from HDFC Credila, as here, the entire process is transparent and made clear from the get-go."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Ease of Application",paragraphs:["– Applying for an education loan has never been easier as you can submit an online application*. Furthermore, you can arrange to have a loan specialist visit your home to discuss the finer details of your loan."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Sense of Financial Responsibility",paragraphs:["– By taking on this loan, students develop a responsible attitude towards their finances, which leaves parents to focus on directing their hard-earned savings towards their own retirement along with planning for any contingencies.","Conclusion","A student loan can enable you to attend the","university of your dreams!","and to start your career after you graduate. Have faith and do not be afraid to give yourself the opportunity that education loans provide you with. Offering a range of attractive features and benefits, a loan from","HDFC Credila","is an investment in your future!","*T&C apply"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"luxury-brand-management-51",title:"Luxury Brand Management",tag:"Destinations",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/luxury-brand-management/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/LBM.png",intro:"With the immensely rising popularity of creative courses around the globe, there is a huge demand for Luxury Brand Management as a course. This course details the strategies and gimmicks used in the luxury industry to market and advertise the brand and take the product and the brand name to scaling heights of success. The growing competition among brands has led to an urgent need to market one’s brand through the right channels and on the right platforms. Luxury Brand Management is all about how to use every possible method of building a brand’s image and creating popularity and allure for a brand. Luxury products and services demand a niche style when it comes to their means and methods of advertising; which creates the need to understand these brands better and choose the ideal ways of advertising. These brands cater to an elite clientele with a certain pocket size; which implies that the psychology of this subset of people needs to be carefully considered, analyzed, and studied while planning any branding activity for these luxury brands.",sections:[{heading:"Luxury Brand Management",paragraphs:["This is what luxury brand management deals with: the mindset of the client; the perfect portrayal of a high-end brand; communication skills development; the various techniques, means, mediums, and channels used for marketing and lastly, the detailed analysis of the results of the marketing activities to foresee, plan and organize branding better."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Career Prospects for Luxury Brand Management",paragraphs:["Given the popularity and increase in awareness about luxury brands, there is a huge demand for marketing managers possessing experience and creative intellect. This course is well designed and targeted towards building strategic brand managers for this segment of products and services. This course opens up a multitude of possibilities as career options for an aspirant in the fashion industry or the creative field of advertising.","There is a wide range of profiles a student can explore after this course:","Luxury Brand Manager"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Fashion Retail buyer",paragraphs:["Supply chain manager for a luxury brand","Luxury marketing director","Communication manager","Brand Strategist","These are some exciting profiles that can be pursued after this course. Each career has rising demand and an attractive pay package with various additional perks and benefits.","These positions will require one to have knowledge of:","Market Research"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Planning budget for research and branding activities",paragraphs:["Networking and building contacts with external agencies"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Planning outdoor and indoor marketing campaigns",paragraphs:["Planning and executing social media campaigns","Salary Prospects:","Luxury brand management makes one competent for several careers. Depending on the profile one wishes to pursue, the salary packages in this field are quite promising.","As a brand manager for retail stores of luxury brands such as Louis Vuitton, Prada, Chanel, you can expect to earn about"],bullets:[]},{heading:"50,000 to 3 Lakhs INR per month",paragraphs:[". As a digital marketing professional for luxury brands, you can earn about","40,000 to 1 Lakh INR per month",".","All in all, one can expect a salary package ranging between 10 Lakhs INR per annum to 50 Lakhs INR per annum. The growth and demand in this field are relatively high and make this an immensely rewarding career option for aspirants.","Studying Luxury Brand Management Overseas:","As the world is coming closer to technology and social media, we are forging new connections and understanding cultures around the globe. In an age of globalization, it is necessary that we keep our minds open to new ideas and learn, evolve, and grow with the new changing branding trends in the world. Therefore, as a student, it is imperative that you soak in the latest developments in the luxury industry and this is possible when you gain complete exposure in the form of an internationally recognized education. There are various reasons why the course of Luxury Brand Management should be studied abroad. Some of the key reasons include:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Understanding and comprehension of global trends",paragraphs:["Tutelage from world experts in the field"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Experiential learning through projects in real-time",paragraphs:["Living in a virtually connected world that functions as ONE entity, it becomes important to absorb all the knowledge and skills that are applicable internationally. Thus, studying this course from an internationally recognized university is a preferred choice and shall open better career opportunities for any marketing or branding aspirant.","Top 10 Business Schools for Luxury Brand Management 2021:","ESSEC Business School","London Business School (LBS)","Columbia Business School"],bullets:[]},{heading:"INSEAD – Europe Campus",paragraphs:["International University of Monaco (IUM)","SKEMA Business School"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Bologna Business School",paragraphs:["NYU Stern School of Business, New York University","HEC Paris","SDA Bocconi School of Business","Future of Luxury Brand Management:","The earning potential of people is rising over the years. Alongside, social media has hugely helped in creating awareness of luxury brands with celebrities endorsing and marketing for such brands and adding to their popularity and demand of luxury brand management.","Globalization has led to the creation of an audience and clientele that sees, understands, and recognizes the nuances and niceties of luxury. Therefore, luxury branding is a popularly rising field of study with an increase in demand for professionals in this industry. This has thus, become one of the top career options across the world with an exciting and rewarding path ahead.","Additional readings","Cracking the Visa Interview","6 Steps to Choose a University","Education Loan for Study abroad"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"visa-interview-question-52",title:"Visa Interview Question",tag:"Visa",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/visa-interview-question/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Visa-Interview.png",intro:"One of the most important steps to acquiring an overseas education is getting a visa. Visa is imperative to study in any foreign country. Most students dread the visa interview, as there are chances of the visa getting rejected. This would lead to a halt in your education plans. Therefore, this article provides certain tips and dos and don’ts for a visa interview. This would make your visa process much simpler and keep you calm through it.",sections:[{heading:"Cracking the Visa Interview",paragraphs:["Most importantly, you should have all your documents handy and be well dressed for your interview.","You must be on time and exude confidence and composure.","You should answer confidently and provide honest answers to the visa officer. Remember, they have all your document copies and also have knowledge about your financial background. Therefore, don’t try to fool them or give false information.","Be certain about your education plans and why you are choosing a particular country, course, and college. The Visa Officer wants to know why you should be allowed to go to a particular country and if you have the financial means to afford the education you have planned for.","Make sure to have answers for the following questions:"],bullets:[]},{heading:"How do you plan to sponsor your education?",paragraphs:["The answers to these questions should be handled tactfully and honestly. You must always tell the visa officer that you plan to return after the course. Or if your college offers certain work opportunities after the course, then make sure to mention the same to the visa officer.","Be certain about your career and plans in case the visa officer asks about it. You should be clear as to why you have opted for a particular course and how it is aligned with your career goals.","Inform the visa officer about your family in the country, which is your reason to return to the country. Make sure you know well about your finances and bank statements and be prepared to answer any questions regarding the same.","Make sure to know everything about your course and college- the course duration, the course content, the infrastructure of the university, the research being conducted in the university related to your course, commencement date, etc.","Also, have your accommodation plans ready. You should know beforehand where you would stay. The visa officer needs to know that you are prepared to go and have made all the arrangements.","Do not get into arguments with the visa officer. Be polite and keep your answers precise and to the point.","Never give the impression that you don’t plan to return to your country or that you have plans to immigrate and seek employment in a foreign country. You must make sure to convey that you will return once your course terminates.","Lastly, don’t forget to thank the interviewer for his time and patience.","Additional readings","Cracking the Visa Interview","6 Steps to Choose a University","Education Loan for Study abroad"],bullets:[]}]},{slug:"education-loan-for-study-abroad-53",title:"Education Loan for Study Abroad",tag:"Funding",published:"One Window archive",sourceUrl:"https://onewindow.co/education-loan-for-study-abroad/",imageUrl:"https://onewindow.co/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Loan-for-study.png",intro:"While students and their guardians are always on the lookout for the best study abroad options, they are always considering costs. Many students, during their research phase, rule out good options only because of the tuition fee. This is because most of them are not aware of the number of options – such as loans and scholarships – that are out there to ease their financial burden. Free information helps create trust and adds to their credibility. Take for instance GyanDhan",sections:[{heading:"Education Loan For Abroad Studies",paragraphs:[", which is actually an"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Education Financing Platform",paragraphs:[". Even before prospective customers contact GyanDhan, they can avail of a lot of information from the website.","GyanDhan is one startup that has created a positive vibe in the education market space. Unlike many other overseas education consultancies out there, GyanDhan does not charge students any money for providing them with basic information. In fact, they believe in providing the student with every bit of information possible, and only connecting the dots where needed.","It is their tie-up with some of the world’s best universities, some of India’s top banks,"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Non-Banking Financial Companies (NBFCs)",paragraphs:[", and international players that really help students. On their own, students find it really hard to reach out to these institutions to get their work done. But going through an organized channel like GyanDhan streamlines the process and assures these institutions as well, because of GyanDhan’s market credibility and trust factor.","On several occasions, GyanDhan has gone out of the way to convince banks and NBFCs to grant loans to students in unusual cases. There are many problems with the","collaterals","of student’s guardians. The guardian might not have a good CIBIL score at times. And then there are cases where the student or guardian might not have any collateral at all. Regardless of the complexity, GyanDhan has helped every student acquire the highest concession on their educational costs."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Availing Education Loan",paragraphs:["GyanDhan helps the student at each step of the process of availing of a loan. If students reach out to lenders themselves, the process might take 15 or more days. Applying through GyanDhan, the turnaround time reduces to half or less. The student gains an extra advantage by having a checklist of all the documents needed to take the application further.","The company assigns a Personalized Loan Counsellor to each customer, to avoid any lag in their loan application process. Students can also track the status of their education loans with the help of the Loan-Tracking tool available on GyanDhan’s website.","The moment students start researching colleges to study abroad, they shortlist and eliminate colleges based on the tuition fee. Taking into account other expenses, such as accommodation costs, insurance costs, living expenses, application fee, and airfare, students and their parents start getting headaches looking at the cumulative sum."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Education Loan Options",paragraphs:["GyanDhan has tie-ups with some of the leading banks, including State Bank of India (SBI), Bank of Baroda, and Axis Bank. While all banks have different interest rates and policies, the best thing is the number of flexible options GyanDhan provides students with. Students with little to no collateral still have a chance to avail an unsecured loan, given the range of options provided by GyanDhan.","All banks have their own advantages. With SBI, there is the option of loan insurance –"],bullets:[]},{heading:"Rinn Raksha Insurance",paragraphs:[", which provides the student with an extra discount on the interest rate. For example, a student who has to pay an interest rate of 10.75 percent, has to pay an interest rate of only 10.25 percent after buying the Rinn Raksha Insurance. It is a","one-time cost","that protects the student’s guardians from making any further payments, in case something unfortunate happens to the student.","GyanDhan has tied up with top","NBFCs","such as","Credila, InCred, Avanse",", and","Auxilo",". If students don’t have an earning co-applicant, another good alternative is international players. By connecting the student to some credible international players like","MPower Financing",", GyanDhan does not leave any stone unturned in providing students with enough loan options to suit their requirements and situation.","Through effective communication and regular follow-ups, GyanDhan has been able to provide students with loans in cases where it seemed far from possible. In one case, a student without collateral was granted a loan by an NBFC, including the collateral owned by the guardians of another co-applicant who had applied for the same university.","Students whose Co-applicant have had bad CIBIL scores, collaterals of insufficient value, or collaterals not complying with the lender’s policies, have all been granted loans – thanks to GyanDhan’s credibility and convincing abilities."],bullets:[]},{heading:"Collaboration of GyanDhan and One Window",paragraphs:["GyanDhan and One Window Overseas Education have been working together for around 3 years to help study abroad aspirants throughout the university application and education financing process.","GyanDhan","refers to its students, who want assistance regarding admissions in different universities abroad, to One Window. Similarly, students who connect","One Window","for university application and are looking for education loan assistance, are referred to GyanDhan for first-rate assistance. In this way, these two entities together become a one-stop solution for students to get the best help in terms of university application, scholarships, and free education loan assistance.","Final Word","At a time when startups are wrapping up their businesses, GyanDhan looks to set a benchmark and establish a foothold in the market for a longer run. This is all because of their openness. Openness in transparency levels, and the willingness to learn and keep evolving."],bullets:[]}]}],d=Object.fromEntries(c.map(a=>[a.slug,a]));a.s(["articleBySlug",0,d,"articles",0,c],93247),a.s([],69310)}];

